window.BATTLES_EN = {"alesia":{"locked[1].name":"Crossbow","locked[2].name":"Knight","locked[3].name":"Cannon","enemies[1].name":"Balearic Slingers","enemies[4].name":"Legionaries","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"Hannibal","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"Legion","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"Hannibal","content.quiz[21].q":"In what year, according to legend, was Rome founded?","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Balearic Slingers","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Legionaries","title":"Alesia","year":"52 BC","side":"Vercingetorix's Gauls","foe":"Julius Caesar's Romans","intro[0]":"52 BC. Vercingetorix, the young leader of the Arverni tribe, united almost all of Gaul against Julius Caesar. The Roman legions are marching toward Alesia, a fortified town where the Gallic army has taken shelter.","intro[1]":"Your mission is to defend the Gallic wall made of logs and stone and keep the Romans out of the town. Survive 30 waves.","victoryText":"In history, Alesia fell. The relief army failed to break through Caesar's siege lines, and Vercingetorix surrendered to save his warriors. Gaul came under Roman rule. But you held the walls, and the French still honor Vercingetorix as one of the first heroes of their history.","defeatText":"The legions have stormed Alesia, but the Gauls can still gather their strength and try again.","theme.spawnSign":"From Caesar's camp","fortress.name":"ALESIA","fortress.banner":"VER","features[0].text":"Alesia — town of the Mandubii","defenders[0].name":"Gallic Spearmen","defenders[0].note":"Cheap units that strike quickly with spears","defenders[0].fact":"Most Gallic warriors were ordinary tribesmen armed with spears and large shields.","defenders[1].name":"Arverni Warriors","defenders[1].note":"Main fighting force: long swords and spears","defenders[1].fact":"The Arverni were a powerful tribe from the mountains of central Gaul. Vercingetorix himself came from this tribe.","defenders[2].name":"Noble Warriors","defenders[2].note":"Heavy fighters in chain mail who can pierce armor","defenders[2].fact":"Gallic nobles wore chain mail and helmets. Some say the Celts even invented chain mail.","defenders[3].name":"Gallic Archers","defenders[3].note":"Shoot far and often","defenders[3].fact":"After the siege of Avaricum, Vercingetorix ordered archers to be gathered from all across Gaul and sent to him. Thousands answered the call.","defenders[4].name":"Fire and Pitch from the Walls","defenders[4].note":"Burning pitch and fat deal area damage","defenders[4].fact":"The defenders of Avaricum threw burning pitch, fat, and torches onto the Roman siege ramp to set the siege towers on fire.","defenders[5].name":"Bituriges Miners","defenders[5].note":"Do not attack, but slow enemies with tunnels","defenders[5].fact":"The Bituriges had iron mines and were skilled at digging tunnels beneath the Roman siege ramp at Avaricum.","locked[0].name":"Catapult","locked[0].line":"The Gauls did not build siege engines like these, but the Romans did","locked[1].line":"Military crossbows would not appear in Europe until the Middle Ages","locked[2].line":"Knights would not appear for almost another thousand years","locked[3].line":"Gunpowder would reach Europe more than 1,300 years later","abilities[0].name":"Scorched Earth","abilities[0].fact":"Vercingetorix convinced the Gauls to burn their own villages and supplies so the Romans could find no grain or horse feed.","abilities[1].name":"The Relief Army","abilities[1].fact":"An army gathered from all across Gaul to rescue Alesia. According to Caesar, it had 8,000 cavalrymen and about 250,000 infantrymen.","enemies[0].name":"Recruits","enemies[0].fact":"In early 52 BC, Caesar recruited new soldiers in Cisalpine Gaul, in northern Italy.","enemies[1].fact":"Caesar wrote that slingers from the Balearic Islands served in his army in Gaul.","enemies[2].name":"Germanic Runners","enemies[2].fact":"Germanic horsemen were accompanied by foot soldiers who held onto the horses' manes and ran as fast as the horses.","enemies[3].name":"Germanic Cavalry","enemies[3].fact":"Caesar hired horsemen from beyond the Rhine River. The Germanic cavalry often decided the outcome of battles against the Gauls.","enemies[4].fact":"A legionary wore chain mail, a bronze helmet, and a large shield called a scutum. He carried a throwing spear called a pilum and a short sword.","enemies[5].name":"Veterans of the Tenth Legion","enemies[5].fact":"The Tenth Legion was Caesar's favorite legion. Caesar himself stayed with it during the Battle of Gergovia.","enemies[6].name":"Centurions","enemies[6].fact":"A centurion commanded about a hundred soldiers and led them into battle. The Romans lost 46 centurions at Gergovia.","enemies[7].name":"Scorpions","enemies[7].fact":"A scorpion was a small Roman siege weapon that looked like a giant bow. It fired heavy bolts with great accuracy.","enemies[8].name":"Siege Towers","enemies[8].fact":"At Avaricum, the Romans rolled wooden siege towers up a ramp toward the wall so their soldiers could attack the defenders from above.","bosses[0].name":"Titus Labienus","bosses[0].fact":"Titus Labienus was Caesar's best lieutenant. In 52 BC, he defeated a Gallic army near Lutetia, modern-day Paris.","bosses[1].name":"Gaius Trebonius","bosses[1].fact":"Gaius Trebonius was one of Caesar's commanders. He led legions and was skilled at building siege fortifications.","bosses[2].name":"Mark Antony","bosses[2].fact":"Young Mark Antony served under Caesar in Gaul. During the night battle at Alesia, he moved soldiers to the places where the enemy was attacking hardest.","bosses[3].name":"Gaius Julius Caesar","bosses[3].fact":"Caesar personally led the war in Gaul and described it in his book Commentaries on the Gallic War.","chapters[0].title":"The Gallic Revolt","chapters[0].year":"52 BC","chapters[0].fact":"In early 52 BC, almost all of Gaul rose against Rome. Vercingetorix of the Arverni was chosen to lead the revolt.","chapters[1].title":"The Siege of Avaricum","chapters[1].year":"52 BC","chapters[1].fact":"The Gauls burned their villages to leave the Romans without food, but they spared Avaricum. Caesar captured it after a 25-day siege.","chapters[2].title":"Gergovia","chapters[2].year":"52 BC","chapters[2].fact":"At Gergovia, Vercingetorix fought off Caesar's assault. Meanwhile, Labienus defeated the Gauls near Lutetia.","chapters[3].title":"The Cavalry Battle","chapters[3].year":"52 BC","chapters[3].fact":"Caesar's Germanic cavalry defeated the Gallic horsemen, and Vercingetorix led his army into Alesia.","chapters[4].title":"Caesar's Siege Lines","chapters[4].year":"52 BC","chapters[4].fact":"Caesar surrounded Alesia with two lines of earthworks and ditches. One faced the town, while the other faced outward to stop the relief army.","chapters[5].title":"The Relief Army and Surrender","chapters[5].year":"52 BC","chapters[5].fact":"In history, the relief army failed to break through the Roman lines, and Vercingetorix surrendered to Caesar. But his name became a symbol of the Gauls' fight for freedom.","waves[0].title":"Recruits","waves[1].title":"Marching Column","waves[2].title":"Slingers","waves[3].title":"Across the Snowy Cevennes","waves[4].title":"Germanic Horsemen at Noviodunum","waves[5].title":"Legions at Avaricum","waves[6].title":"Beneath the Walls","waves[7].title":"Siege Towers","waves[8].title":"Scorpions Strike","waves[9].title":"Assault in the Rain","waves[10].title":"Mount Gergovia","waves[11].title":"The Tenth Legion","waves[12].title":"The Trumpet Goes Unheard","waves[13].title":"Battle at the Wall","waves[14].title":"Labienus","waves[15].title":"Horsemen from Beyond the Rhine","waves[16].title":"Centurions","waves[17].title":"Flank Attack","waves[18].title":"The Chase","waves[19].title":"Trebonius","waves[20].title":"The Siege Ring Closes","waves[21].title":"The Outer Line","waves[22].title":"Lilies and Spurs","waves[23].title":"Hunger in the Town","waves[24].title":"Mark Antony","waves[25].title":"The Relief Army Arrives","waves[26].title":"Battle on the Plain","waves[27].title":"Night Assault","waves[28].title":"The Northern Camp","waves[29].title":"Caesar's Red Cloak","content.waveFacts[0]":"In early 52 BC, the Carnutes tribe killed Roman merchants in the town of Cenabum, sparking a revolt across Gaul.","content.waveFacts[1]":"The Gauls passed news of Cenabum by shouting from field to field. Before sunset, the news had reached the Arverni lands, 160 miles away.","content.waveFacts[2]":"The Arverni chose young Vercingetorix, son of Celtillus, as their leader. Many other tribes joined him.","content.waveFacts[3]":"In winter, Caesar led his army across the Cevennes Mountains. His soldiers cleared a path through snow six feet deep, almost as tall as a person.","content.waveFacts[4]":"Near Noviodunum, about 400 of Caesar's Germanic horsemen sent the Gallic cavalry fleeing.","content.waveFacts[5]":"Vercingetorix convinced the Bituriges to burn more than twenty of their own towns in a single day so the Romans could find no grain.","content.waveFacts[6]":"The Bituriges begged to spare Avaricum, their most beautiful town. Caesar immediately laid siege to it.","content.waveFacts[7]":"Over 25 days, the Romans built an enormous ramp toward the walls of Avaricum and rolled siege towers up it.","content.waveFacts[8]":"Caesar wrote that a Roman scorpion killed one Gaul after another as they threw burning fat and pitch. Each time a warrior fell, another took his place.","content.waveFacts[9]":"During heavy rain, while the guards took shelter, the legions captured Avaricum. According to Caesar, only about 800 of its 40,000 people escaped.","content.waveFacts[10]":"Caesar approached Gergovia, the main town of the Arverni, high on a mountain.","content.waveFacts[11]":"Caesar launched an assault on Gergovia with his favorite Tenth Legion.","content.waveFacts[12]":"Many soldiers could not hear the trumpet signal to retreat because of the valley. The legionaries climbed toward the walls right into the Gallic attack.","content.waveFacts[13]":"At Gergovia, the Romans lost nearly 700 soldiers and 46 centurions. Caesar himself wrote about this defeat.","content.waveFacts[14]":"Meanwhile, Titus Labienus defeated the army of the elderly Gallic leader Camulogenus near Lutetia, modern-day Paris.","content.waveFacts[15]":"After Gergovia, even the Aedui, Caesar's former allies, rose against Rome.","content.waveFacts[16]":"Caesar called for Germanic horsemen from beyond the Rhine. Because their horses were poor, he gave them the better horses of his own officers.","content.waveFacts[17]":"The Gallic cavalry attacked the Romans on the march, but the Germanic horsemen captured a hill and struck their flank.","content.waveFacts[18]":"The Gallic horsemen fled, and Vercingetorix led his army into Alesia, a town of the Mandubii on a high hill.","content.waveFacts[19]":"When Caesar reached Alesia, he decided not to storm the town. Instead, he would surround it and starve its defenders.","content.waveFacts[20]":"Caesar's inner line of fortifications stretched for 11 Roman miles and included 23 fortified posts.","content.waveFacts[21]":"To defend against the Gallic relief army, Caesar built a second, outer line stretching 14 miles.","content.waveFacts[22]":"The Romans hid traps in front of their earthworks: sharpened branches, pits with stakes called 'lilies,' and stakes fitted with iron hooks.","content.waveFacts[23]":"Alesia had enough food for about 30 days. The Gauls sent the Mandubii townspeople, including women and children, out of the town, but the Romans refused to let them pass.","content.waveFacts[24]":"Mark Antony and Gaius Trebonius commanded sections of the Roman siege lines and defended them against Gallic attacks.","content.waveFacts[25]":"The relief army reached Alesia. According to Caesar, it had 8,000 cavalrymen and about 250,000 infantrymen.","content.waveFacts[26]":"In the first battle on the plain, Caesar's Germanic cavalry once again defeated the Gallic horsemen.","content.waveFacts[27]":"At night, the Gauls attacked the Roman lines from both sides, but many fell into Roman traps in the darkness.","content.waveFacts[28]":"Vercassivellaunus led 60,000 picked warriors around the mountain and attacked the weakest Roman camp in the north. Caesar sent Labienus to help defend it.","content.waveFacts[29]":"Caesar wrote that his soldiers recognized his arrival on the battlefield by the color of his cloak. After this battle, Vercingetorix surrendered.","content.quiz[0].q":"Who led the Gallic revolt in 52 BC?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"Vercingetorix","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"Spartacus","content.quiz[0].explain":"Vercingetorix, leader of the Arverni, was chosen to lead the entire revolt.","content.quiz[1].q":"Where is most of ancient Gaul located today?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"In Germany","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"In Spain","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"In France","content.quiz[1].explain":"Gaul covered the lands of modern-day France, Belgium, and neighboring countries.","content.quiz[2].q":"Which Roman general conquered Gaul?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Pompey","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Julius Caesar","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Scipio","content.quiz[2].explain":"Caesar fought in Gaul from 58 to 51 BC.","content.quiz[3].q":"Which tribe did Vercingetorix belong to?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"The Aedui","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"The Arverni","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"The Bituriges","content.quiz[3].explain":"The Arverni lived in the mountains of central Gaul. Their main town was Gergovia.","content.quiz[4].q":"What was the council of noble Romans that governed the republic alongside the consuls called?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"The Senate","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"The Veche","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"The Areopagus","content.quiz[4].explain":"Senators discussed laws, war, and peace.","content.quiz[5].q":"What did the Gauls do to leave the Romans without food?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"Built a fleet","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"Called for Germanic warriors","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Burned their own villages and supplies","content.quiz[5].explain":"Vercingetorix proposed this plan. Without grain or horse feed, the Romans would have trouble fighting.","content.quiz[6].q":"What was the main military unit of the Roman army called?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"Phalanx","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"Horde","content.quiz[6].explain":"A legion was divided into cohorts and centuries.","content.quiz[7].q":"What was the name of the Bituriges town that Caesar besieged and captured?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Gergovia","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Avaricum","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Lutetia","content.quiz[7].explain":"Avaricum is now the city of Bourges in France.","content.quiz[8].q":"Who were legionaries?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"Soldiers of a Roman legion","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"Greek sailors","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"Gallic priests","content.quiz[8].explain":"Legionaries were Roman citizens who served in the army for many years.","content.quiz[9].q":"Where did Vercingetorix's Gauls fight off Caesar's assault?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"At Gergovia","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"At Avaricum","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"At Alesia","content.quiz[9].explain":"This was one of Caesar's few defeats in Gaul.","content.quiz[10].q":"Which city stands on the site of ancient Gallic Lutetia today?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"London","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"Paris","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"Lyon","content.quiz[10].explain":"Lutetia was a town of the Parisii tribe, which gave Paris its name.","content.quiz[11].q":"Who were the Druids?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Gallic horsemen","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Roman senators","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Gallic priests and wise men","content.quiz[11].explain":"Druids made offerings to the gods, taught young people, and settled disputes.","content.quiz[12].q":"Whom did Caesar hire as cavalry to defeat the Gallic horsemen?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Germanic warriors","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Persians","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Scythians","content.quiz[12].explain":"The Germanic tribes lived beyond the Rhine River and were famous for their brave horsemen.","content.quiz[13].q":"What were the two highest officials of the Roman Republic, elected for one year, called?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"Kings","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"Tribunes","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"Consuls","content.quiz[13].explain":"Two consuls were elected so that neither could take all the power.","content.quiz[14].q":"How did Caesar surround Alesia?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"He built two lines of earthworks and ditches","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"He diverted a river","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"He burned the entire surrounding forest","content.quiz[14].explain":"The inner line faced the town, while the outer line defended against the relief army.","content.quiz[15].q":"Who led the slave revolt in Italy from 74 to 71 BC?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"Caesar","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"Spartacus","content.quiz[15].explain":"Spartacus was a gladiator from Thrace.","content.quiz[16].q":"About how many days could Alesia's food supplies last?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"3 days","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"300 days","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"30 days","content.quiz[16].explain":"That is why Vercingetorix sent his horsemen to call on all of Gaul for help.","content.quiz[17].q":"What is the name of the book Caesar wrote about his war in Gaul?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"The Iliad","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"Commentaries on the Gallic War","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"The Histories by Herodotus","content.quiz[17].explain":"It is our main source about the Battle of Alesia, although Caesar wrote about himself and his own victories.","content.quiz[18].q":"Which relative of Vercingetorix led 60,000 warriors against the northern Roman camp?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"Vercassivellaunus","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"Camulogenus","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"Celtillus","content.quiz[18].explain":"Vercassivellaunus was Vercingetorix's cousin. He was captured.","content.quiz[19].q":"What kind of spear did a legionary throw before close combat?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"Sarissa","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"Pilum","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"Halberd","content.quiz[19].explain":"The pilum could get stuck in an enemy's shield, making it harder to fight.","content.quiz[20].q":"According to Caesar, how did his soldiers recognize him in the final battle?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"By his golden crown","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"By his white horse","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"By the color of his cloak","content.quiz[20].explain":"The general's cloak was brightly colored and could be seen from far away.","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"In 753 BC","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"In 52 BC","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"In 476 AD","content.quiz[21].explain":"According to legend, the city was founded by the brothers Romulus and Remus.","content.quiz[22].q":"What did Roman soldiers at Alesia call 'lilies'?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Flowers on the earthworks","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"Pits with sharpened stakes","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"Gallic shields","content.quiz[22].explain":"The pits were covered with branches, making them hard to see from above.","content.quiz[23].q":"Which river did Caesar cross in 49 BC, starting a civil war?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"Tiber","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"Nile","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"Rubicon","content.quiz[23].explain":"Since then, 'crossing the Rubicon' has meant making a decision that cannot be undone.","content.quiz[24].q":"How did the Battle of Alesia end?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Vercingetorix surrendered to Caesar","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"The Gauls captured Rome","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Caesar left Gaul","content.quiz[24].explain":"He surrendered to save the lives of his warriors.","content.quiz[25].q":"Why was the Gallic wall effective against the Romans?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"It was invisible","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"The stone would not burn, and the long logs made it hard to break with a battering ram","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"It was made of iron","content.quiz[25].explain":"Caesar himself described the Gallic wall this way.","content.quiz[26].q":"In what year was Julius Caesar assassinated?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"In 52 BC","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"In 476 AD","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"In 44 BC","content.quiz[26].explain":"Conspirators killed him in the building where the Senate was meeting, on the Ides of March.","content.quiz[27].q":"Who became the first Roman emperor after Caesar?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"Octavian Augustus","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"Mark Antony","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Nero","content.quiz[27].explain":"Octavian was Caesar's great-nephew and adopted son.","content.quiz[28].q":"What happened to Gaul after Alesia?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"It became an independent kingdom","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"It became part of Carthage","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"It became a Roman province","content.quiz[28].explain":"The Gauls gradually adopted Latin, which later developed into French.","content.quiz[29].q":"Why did Caesar win at Alesia even though the Gauls outnumbered his army?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"Snow slowed down the Gauls","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"His fortifications, well-trained legions, and Germanic cavalry helped him win","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"The relief army never arrived","content.quiz[29].explain":"The Romans were skilled at building fortifications quickly and moving soldiers to wherever they were needed most.","content.events[0].title":"How Should We Fight Caesar?","content.events[0].text":"The legions are strong in open battle. Vercingetorix gathers the tribal chiefs. How can they defeat the Romans?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Burn villages and supplies to leave the Romans without grain","content.events[0].options[0].result":"That is exactly what Vercingetorix proposed. The Gauls burned more than twenty Bituriges towns in one day, while their cavalry cut off the Romans' access to horse feed.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Fight the Romans in one big battle in the open field","content.events[0].options[1].result":"The legions almost always won in open battle. Vercingetorix decided to starve the Romans instead.","content.events[1].title":"The Ramp at Avaricum","content.events[1].text":"The Romans are building an enormous ramp toward the walls of Avaricum and rolling siege towers up it. What should the defenders do?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Dig tunnels and set the ramp on fire","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That is what happened. The Bituriges dug tunnels beneath the ramp and set it on fire, but the Romans put out the flames.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"Wait until the Romans get tired","content.events[1].options[1].result":"The Romans worked day and night. The defenders dug tunnels and threw burning materials to slow them down.","content.events[2].title":"The Assault on Gergovia","content.events[2].text":"The legionaries have pushed too far and are climbing the walls of Gergovia. They cannot hear the signal to retreat. What should the Gauls do?","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Attack the tired Romans from above with all their strength","content.events[2].options[0].result":"That is what happened. The Gauls drove the Romans down the slope, and Caesar lost nearly 700 soldiers.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Abandon the town and escape into the forests","content.events[2].options[1].result":"The Gauls did not leave. They fought off the assault, and Caesar retreated from Gergovia.","content.events[3].title":"Where Should We Retreat?","content.events[3].text":"Caesar's Germanic cavalry has defeated the Gallic horsemen. Where should Vercingetorix shelter his infantry?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"In Alesia, a fortified town on a hill","content.events[3].options[0].result":"That is what he did, hoping Caesar would be unable to storm the town. But Caesar surrounded it instead.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Stay in the open field","content.events[3].options[1].result":"The legions were stronger in open battle. Vercingetorix led his army behind the walls of Alesia.","content.events[4].title":"The Siege Ring Closes","content.events[4].text":"The Roman siege lines are not yet complete. The town has enough food for about 30 days. What should you do?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Send horsemen out at night to call on all of Gaul for help","content.events[4].options[0].result":"That is what happened. The horsemen escaped through gaps in the siege lines, and Gaul gathered an enormous relief army.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Stay quiet and wait for the Romans to leave","content.events[4].options[1].result":"Caesar had no intention of leaving. Vercingetorix sent messengers for help before the siege ring closed.","content.events[5].title":"The Final Decision","content.events[5].text":"The relief army has been defeated and is fleeing. The town is starving. Vercingetorix gathers his council.","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Surrender himself to save his warriors' lives","content.events[5].options[0].result":"That is what he did. He said he had fought not for himself but for Gaul's freedom, and offered to let the chiefs hand him over to the Romans.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Escape alone and abandon the army","content.events[5].options[1].result":"Vercingetorix did not abandon his warriors. He surrendered himself to Caesar.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Gallic Spearmen","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"Most of the Gallic army consisted of ordinary free tribesmen. They went into battle with spears and large wooden shields with iron bosses in the center. They often had no armor, but there were great numbers of them.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Arverni Warriors","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"The Arverni were one of the most powerful tribes in Gaul. Their lands lay in the mountains of central France, and their main town was Gergovia. Gallic warriors fought with long iron swords that were good for slashing. The Arverni were the first to follow Vercingetorix.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Noble Warriors","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"Gallic nobles kept groups of warriors by their side and went into battle wearing chain mail and iron helmets. Some say the Celts invented chain mail and the Romans later adopted it. The nobles were the strongest and proudest part of the Gallic army.","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Gallic Archers","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"After losing Avaricum, Vercingetorix ordered archers to be gathered from all across Gaul. Caesar wrote that great numbers of them answered the call. Archers fought alongside the cavalry and stood on walls to attack enemies from a distance.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Fire and Pitch from the Walls","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"The defenders of Avaricum threw burning pitch, fat, and torches onto the Roman siege ramp. Once, they set the ramp on fire through a tunnel, and the Romans barely managed to put out the flames. Fire was the Gauls' main weapon against wooden siege towers.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Bituriges Miners","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"The Bituriges had iron mines in their lands and knew how to dig underground. At Avaricum, they dug tunnels beneath the Roman siege ramp to make it collapse. They also used loops of rope to pull Roman siege hooks off the walls.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Recruits","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"When the revolt broke out in Gaul, Caesar was in Italy. He immediately began recruiting new soldiers in Cisalpine Gaul, in northern Italy. The recruits had little experience, but they learned quickly while serving alongside veterans.","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"The people of the Balearic Islands near Spain were famous as the best slingers. A sling is a strap used to spin and throw a stone or lead bullet. Caesar mentioned Balearic slingers serving in his army in Gaul.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Germanic Runners","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"Germanic horsemen came to help Caesar with light infantry soldiers. Caesar described how these foot soldiers held onto the horses' manes and ran alongside the riders. In battle, they helped their cavalry and protected wounded warriors.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Germanic Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"The Germanic tribes lived beyond the Rhine River. Caesar hired their horsemen and even gave them the better horses of his own commanders. The Germanic cavalry helped Caesar at Noviodunum, in the battle before Alesia, and in the final battle at its walls.","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"A legionary wore chain mail, a bronze or iron helmet, and a large curved shield called a scutum. Before close combat, he threw a pilum, then fought with a short sword. Every evening, legionaries dug a ditch and built an earthwork around their camp. That is why they were so good at building fortifications quickly.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"The Tenth Legion","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"Caesar valued the Tenth Legion more than any other. Its veterans fought alongside him throughout the Gallic War. At Gergovia, Caesar stayed with this legion and used it to cover the retreat.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Centurions","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"A centurion commanded a century, a unit of about 80–100 soldiers. He led his men into battle, so centurions often died fighting. At Gergovia, 46 centurions were killed, and Caesar mentioned many of them by name.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Scorpions","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"A scorpion was a Roman siege weapon that looked like a large bow on a stand. Twisted cords made from animal sinew powered it, allowing it to fire heavy bolts with great accuracy. According to Caesar, a scorpion at Avaricum killed Gallic defenders on the wall one after another.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Siege Towers","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"A siege tower was a tall wooden structure on wheels. Soldiers pushed it toward a wall and fought the defenders from above. At Avaricum, the Romans rolled their towers up an enormous ramp that took 25 days to build.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Titus Labienus","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Titus Labienus was Caesar's chief lieutenant in Gaul. In 52 BC, he defeated the Gauls led by the elderly Camulogenus near Lutetia with four legions. At Alesia, Caesar sent him with several cohorts to help defend the northern camp. Later, Labienus joined Caesar's enemy Pompey and died in 45 BC.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Gaius Trebonius","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Gaius Trebonius was a legate, one of Caesar's commanders. In spring 52 BC, Caesar left him to finish capturing the town of Vellaunodunum. At Alesia, he and Mark Antony defended their section of the siege lines. Later, Trebonius became one of the conspirators against Caesar.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Mark Antony","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"In 52 BC, Mark Antony was a young commander in Caesar's army. During the night battle at Alesia, he moved soldiers from distant posts to places where the Gauls were attacking hardest. After Caesar's assassination, Antony became one of the most powerful men in Rome, but he lost his struggle against Octavian.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Gaius Julius Caesar","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Caesar commanded the Roman army in Gaul from 58 to 51 BC. During those years, he conquered all of Gaul. In the final battle at Alesia, he personally led his reserves into the attack. Caesar described the war in his book Commentaries on the Gallic War.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Vercingetorix","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Vercingetorix was an Arverni nobleman and the son of Celtillus. In 52 BC, he united many Gallic tribes against Rome. He defeated Caesar's assault at Gergovia, but was surrounded at Alesia and surrendered. He spent several years as a prisoner. In 46 BC, he was paraded through Rome during Caesar's triumph and then executed.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Caesar (the Man)","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Gaius Julius Caesar was a Roman general and politician. After the Gallic War, he crossed the Rubicon and won a civil war. He became dictator for life, but conspirators assassinated him in 44 BC. His name later became a title and gave rise to words such as Kaiser and Tsar.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Vercassivellaunus","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Vercassivellaunus was an Arverni nobleman and Vercingetorix's cousin. He became one of the four leaders of the relief army. On the decisive day, he attacked the northern Roman camp with 60,000 warriors. The attack failed, and he was captured.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Commius","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Commius was the leader of the Atrebates tribe. He had once helped Caesar and even traveled with him to Britain. In 52 BC, he switched sides and joined the Gauls. Commius became one of the leaders of the relief army and managed to escape after the defeat at Alesia.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Marcus Petronius","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"Marcus Petronius was a centurion of the Eighth Legion. At Gergovia, when the Gauls surrounded his soldiers, he charged the enemy alone. Caesar wrote that Petronius died but saved his men. Caesar used this story to show the bravery of Rome's finest soldiers.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"The Gallic Revolt","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"In winter 52 BC, the Carnutes killed Roman merchants in Cenabum, and a revolt spread across Gaul. Vercingetorix was chosen as its leader. Caesar hurried from Italy, crossed the snowy Cevennes Mountains, and captured several Gallic towns. At Noviodunum, Germanic horsemen defeated the Gallic cavalry.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"The Siege of Avaricum","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"Vercingetorix decided to starve the Romans and ordered the towns of the Bituriges to be burned. But Avaricum was spared, and Caesar laid siege to it. The Romans spent 25 days building a ramp, while the Gauls set it on fire and dug tunnels beneath it. On a rainy day, the legions stormed the town, and almost all its inhabitants were killed.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"Gergovia","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"Caesar besieged Gergovia, the main town of the Arverni, high on a mountain. During the assault, many soldiers failed to hear the signal to retreat and kept climbing toward the walls. The Gauls attacked from above, and Caesar lost nearly 700 soldiers. Meanwhile, Labienus defeated the Gauls near Lutetia.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"The Cavalry Battle","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"Caesar was marching south when the Gallic cavalry attacked his column. Caesar's Germanic horsemen captured a hill and struck the Gauls. The Gallic cavalry fled, and Vercingetorix led his army into Alesia. Caesar followed.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"Caesar's Siege Lines","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"At Alesia, Caesar built an inner line of fortifications 11 miles long, followed by an outer line 14 miles long. The Romans dug ditches and hid traps in front of the earthworks. Vercingetorix managed to send horsemen out to call for help. Hunger began to spread through the town.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"The Relief Army and Surrender","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"The relief army attacked the Roman lines three times, while Vercingetorix attacked from inside Alesia. On the final day, Vercassivellaunus struck the northern camp, but Caesar personally brought reinforcements, and the Germanic cavalry attacked the Gauls from behind. The relief army fled. The next day, Vercingetorix surrendered to Caesar.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"The Gallic Wall","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"Caesar described in detail how the Gauls built their walls. They laid long logs across the wall, filled the gaps with earth, and covered the outer face with large stones. Row by row, the wall grew taller. The stone protected it from fire, while the long, connected logs made it difficult to break with a battering ram.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"The Roman Legion","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"A legion had about 5,000 soldiers and was divided into 10 cohorts. Each cohort was divided into centuries. Legionaries served for many years and were well trained. They knew how to fight, but they were also skilled builders. They constructed roads, bridges, camps, and siege earthworks.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Gaul and the Gauls","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"The Gauls were Celtic tribes who lived in what is now France and neighboring countries. Each tribe had its own leaders, and the tribes often fought one another. The Gauls lived in villages and fortified towns called oppida. Their priests and teachers were known as Druids.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"Where Was Alesia?","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"Alesia stood on Mount Auxois in Burgundy, near the modern village of Alise-Sainte-Reine. In the 19th century, Emperor Napoleon III ordered excavations there, and archaeologists found traces of Roman ditches. A large statue of Vercingetorix was placed on the hill. Today, a museum about the battle stands nearby.","content.encyclopedia[34].title":"What Happened Next?","content.encyclopedia[34].text":"After Alesia, some Gallic tribes continued to resist, but by 51 BC, Caesar had conquered all of Gaul. It became a Roman province, and the Romans built roads and towns there. The Gauls gradually began speaking Latin, which later developed into French. Soon afterward, Caesar started a civil war and became the ruler of Rome."},"constantinople":{"locked[1].name":"Cannon","locked[2].name":"Crossbow","locked[3].name":"Knight","title":"Siege of Constantinople","year":"717–718 AD","side":"The Byzantines of Emperor Leo III","foe":"The Arabs of the Umayyad Caliphate","intro[0]":"An Arab army and a huge fleet are advancing toward the Byzantine capital. If Constantinople falls, the road into Europe will be open.","intro[1]":"You are Emperor Leo III the Isaurian. Defend the Theodosian Walls, burn enemy ships with Greek fire, and survive the terrible winter.","victoryText":"In 718 AD, the Arabs lifted the siege, and almost their entire fleet was destroyed in a storm. Byzantium held firm and stopped the invasion of Europe for many years.","defeatText":"The walls of Constantinople could not hold, and the road into Europe opened for the Arabs.","theme.spawnSign":"From Syria","fortress.name":"CONSTANTINOPLE","features[0].text":"Golden Horn","features[1].text":"Theodosian Walls","defenders[0].name":"Stratiot Militia","defenders[0].note":"Cheap and fast: throw javelins","defenders[0].fact":"Stratiots were farmer-soldiers. They received land in return for military service and bought their own weapons.","defenders[1].name":"Skutatoi","defenders[1].note":"Main fighting force: large shields and spears","defenders[1].fact":"The name 'skutatoi' comes from 'scutum,' meaning a large shield. These soldiers held their formation in the center.","defenders[2].name":"Cataphracts","defenders[2].note":"Heavy cavalry: crush enemy horsemen","defenders[2].fact":"Cataphracts were armored horsemen whose horses also wore protection. The Byzantines adopted this type of cavalry from the Persians.","defenders[3].name":"Toxotai Archers","defenders[3].note":"Shoot enemies from the walls at long range","defenders[3].fact":"Byzantine archers, called toxotai, were famous for their accuracy. The bow was considered one of the army's main weapons.","defenders[4].name":"Dromon with Greek Fire","defenders[4].note":"A stream of fire that burns and deals area damage","defenders[4].fact":"A dromon was a fast Byzantine warship. A bronze tube called a siphon was mounted on its bow to shoot Greek fire.","defenders[5].name":"Theodosian Walls (Engineers)","defenders[5].note":"Do not attack, but hold enemies back at the walls","defenders[5].fact":"The Theodosian Walls had a ditch and two rows of walls with towers. The city's engineers constantly repaired them.","locked[0].name":"Varangians","locked[0].line":"Will enter Byzantine service in 270 years","locked[1].line":"Gunpowder will not reach Europe for another 600 years","locked[2].line":"The Byzantines will learn about it from the Crusaders in 400 years","locked[3].line":"Armored Western knights will not appear for several more centuries","abilities[0].name":"Greek Fire","abilities[0].fact":"Greek fire was said to have been invented by Callinicus. It could keep burning on water, and the Byzantines first used it against the Arabs around 674 AD.","abilities[1].name":"Prayer at the Walls","abilities[1].fact":"The people of Constantinople believed the Virgin Mary protected their city. During difficult sieges, they prayed with the patriarch at the walls.","enemies[0].name":"Arab Infantry","enemies[0].fact":"The caliph's army was mainly made up of foot soldiers recruited from across the caliphate.","enemies[1].name":"Syrian Spearmen","enemies[1].fact":"Much of the Umayyad army came from Syria, the lands around Damascus.","enemies[2].name":"Arab Archers","enemies[2].fact":"Arab archers shot quickly and accurately, covering the infantry as it advanced.","enemies[3].name":"Bedouin Cavalry","enemies[3].fact":"The Bedouins were Arab nomads and excellent horsemen. They were often the first to charge the enemy.","enemies[4].name":"Camels","enemies[4].fact":"Camels carried supplies and soldiers. Horses were frightened by their smell, and many camels died from the cold in winter.","enemies[5].name":"Ghulams","enemies[5].fact":"Ghulams were professional heavy cavalry soldiers who wore chain mail.","enemies[6].name":"Sappers and Miners","enemies[6].fact":"Sappers dug tunnels beneath walls and towers to make them collapse.","enemies[7].name":"Siege Tower","enemies[7].fact":"A siege tower was rolled up to a wall so soldiers could climb directly onto it.","enemies[8].name":"Manjaniq","enemies[8].fact":"A manjaniq was an Arab stone-throwing siege engine that launched heavy rocks at walls and towers.","bosses[0].name":"Sulayman ibn Muad","bosses[0].fact":"Sulayman ibn Muad was an Arab commander who led advance units through Asia Minor.","bosses[1].name":"Commander of the Arab Fleet","bosses[1].fact":"The Arab fleet had hundreds of ships, but Greek fire burned them in entire rows.","bosses[2].name":"Umar ibn Hubayra","bosses[2].fact":"According to Arab accounts, Umar ibn Hubayra was one of the commanders of this campaign.","bosses[3].name":"Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik","bosses[3].fact":"Maslama, the brother of caliphs, commanded the land army outside Constantinople for more than a year.","chapters[0].title":"The Arabs March Through Asia Minor","chapters[0].year":"716 AD","chapters[0].fact":"Arab troops marched toward Constantinople by land, while Leo, a Byzantine general, used clever tricks to hold important cities.","chapters[1].title":"Leo III Takes Power","chapters[1].year":"717 AD","chapters[1].fact":"In March 717 AD, General Leo became emperor and immediately began preparing the city for a siege.","chapters[2].title":"The Siege and Greek Fire","chapters[2].year":"717 AD","chapters[2].fact":"The city was under siege from August 717 AD, and the approaching Arab fleet was met by Byzantine fire ships.","chapters[3].title":"The Terrible Winter","chapters[3].year":"717–718 AD","chapters[3].fact":"The winter was extremely harsh, and hunger and disease spread through the Arab camp.","chapters[4].title":"Tervel's Bulgarians","chapters[4].year":"718 AD","chapters[4].fact":"In spring 718 AD, the Bulgarians led by Khan Tervel attacked the Arabs, while Christian sailors from Egypt began switching to the Byzantine side.","chapters[5].title":"Retreat and Storm","chapters[5].year":"718 AD","chapters[5].fact":"In August 718 AD, the Arabs lifted the siege. On the journey home, a storm badly damaged their fleet.","waves[0].title":"Advance Units","waves[1].title":"Scouts","waves[2].title":"Syrians","waves[3].title":"Desert Archers","waves[4].title":"Bedouins","waves[5].title":"Marching Column","waves[6].title":"Sappers","waves[7].title":"Camel Supply Train","waves[8].title":"Ghulams","waves[9].title":"Supply Caravan","waves[10].title":"Raid on the Suburbs","waves[11].title":"The First Stone-Throwers","waves[12].title":"Siege Tower","waves[13].title":"Cavalry Charges","waves[14].title":"Sulayman ibn Muad","waves[15].title":"Bombarding the Walls","waves[16].title":"Winter Supply Train","waves[17].title":"Towers at the Wall","waves[18].title":"The Starving Camp","waves[19].title":"The Arab Fleet","waves[20].title":"Spring Assault","waves[21].title":"A New Army","waves[22].title":"Stone-Throwers on Ships","waves[23].title":"Tunnels","waves[24].title":"Umar ibn Hubayra","waves[25].title":"The Final Hungry Attack","waves[26].title":"Desperate Assault","waves[27].title":"Iron Fist","waves[28].title":"The Army Withdraws","waves[29].title":"Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik","content.waveFacts[0]":"In 716 AD, Arab forces entered Asia Minor and began marching toward the Byzantine capital.","content.waveFacts[1]":"Arab scouts moved ahead of the army, searching for roads and towns.","content.waveFacts[2]":"Syrian spearmen made up a large part of the caliph's army.","content.waveFacts[3]":"Arab archers covered the infantry as it marched through foreign lands.","content.waveFacts[4]":"Bedouins on fast horses raided Byzantine villages. In 716 AD, Leo the Isaurian used a clever trick to keep the Arabs from capturing the city of Amorium.","content.waveFacts[5]":"In early 717 AD, the Arab army marched through Asia Minor while Leo prepared to face it.","content.waveFacts[6]":"In March 717 AD, Leo entered Constantinople and took power. Meanwhile, Arab sappers were already preparing for the siege.","content.waveFacts[7]":"Camels carried supplies for the enormous army marching toward Constantinople.","content.waveFacts[8]":"Ghulams were the caliph's heavy cavalry. They were tough and well armed.","content.waveFacts[9]":"Leo ordered food supplies to be brought into the city and the walls to be repaired before the siege.","content.waveFacts[10]":"In summer 717 AD, the Arabs crossed into Europe and approached Constantinople by land.","content.waveFacts[11]":"The Arabs set up stone-throwing siege engines to destroy the city's towers and walls.","content.waveFacts[12]":"The siege began on August 15, 717 AD, and the enemy rolled a siege tower toward the wall.","content.waveFacts[13]":"Arab cavalry tried to break through to the gates, but the walls held firm.","content.waveFacts[14]":"Sulayman ibn Muad led the Arab cavalry, while Greek fire burned enemy ships in the strait.","content.waveFacts[15]":"Arab stone-throwers fired at the walls, but the Theodosian Walls withstood the bombardment.","content.waveFacts[16]":"During the winter of 717–718 AD, snow covered the ground for a very long time, and Arab camels died from the cold.","content.waveFacts[17]":"The siege towers at the walls were no help. During winter, the camp suffered from hunger.","content.waveFacts[18]":"Food ran short in the Arab camp, and the soldiers ate whatever they could find.","content.waveFacts[19]":"In spring 718 AD, a new fleet arrived to help the Arabs, but Leo met it with fire ships.","content.waveFacts[20]":"In spring 718 AD, the Arabs decided to storm the city once more, hoping for reinforcements.","content.waveFacts[21]":"New forces arrived from Egypt and North Africa, but not all the sailors wanted to fight against Christians.","content.waveFacts[22]":"Arab ships fired stones at the walls, but Leo's fire ships drove them away.","content.waveFacts[23]":"The sappers dug tunnels again, but the defenders stopped them and collapsed the passages.","content.waveFacts[24]":"Umar ibn Hubayra's unit advanced first, while the Bulgarians of Khan Tervel were approaching from behind.","content.waveFacts[25]":"According to the chronicle of Theophanes, the Bulgarians defeated an Arab unit in Thrace.","content.waveFacts[26]":"The caliph's army was starving but still launched one final assault.","content.waveFacts[27]":"The walls remained standing, and the Arab siege towers at the gates never became bridges into the city.","content.waveFacts[28]":"By summer 718 AD, the Arab army was growing weaker, and no reinforcements were arriving.","content.waveFacts[29]":"On August 15, 718 AD, the Arabs lifted the siege, and Maslama led what remained of his army away from the city.","content.quiz[0].q":"Who founded Islam?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"Muhammad","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"Justinian","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"Leo III","content.quiz[0].explain":"Muhammad was the founder of Islam and the first leader of the Muslims.","content.quiz[1].q":"What was the capital of the Byzantine Empire?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"Damascus","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"Constantinople","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"Mecca","content.quiz[1].explain":"Constantinople stood on the Bosporus Strait and was the largest city in Europe.","content.quiz[2].q":"In which city was Muhammad born?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Damascus","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Baghdad","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Mecca","content.quiz[2].explain":"Mecca in Arabia is a holy city for Muslims.","content.quiz[3].q":"Which emperor built Hagia Sophia?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"Leo III","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"Justinian","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"Heraclius","content.quiz[3].explain":"Hagia Sophia was built under Emperor Justinian in the 530s AD.","content.quiz[4].q":"What is the holy book of Islam called?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"The Quran","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"The Bible","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"The Gospel","content.quiz[4].explain":"The Quran is the holy book of Islam.","content.quiz[5].q":"What was 'Greek fire'?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"A celebration bonfire","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"A type of cannon","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"A flammable mixture that water could not put out","content.quiz[5].explain":"Greek fire set enemy ships ablaze and kept burning on water.","content.quiz[6].q":"What was the ruler of the Arab state called after Muhammad?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"Emperor","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"Caliph","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"Consul","content.quiz[6].explain":"A caliph was the successor to the Prophet and the leader of the Muslim state.","content.quiz[7].q":"What protected Constantinople from attacks by land?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"The Theodosian Walls","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"The Great Wall of China","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Hadrian's Wall","content.quiz[7].explain":"The Theodosian Walls and their moat made the city almost impossible to capture.","content.quiz[8].q":"Which dynasty of caliphs ruled from Damascus?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"The Abbasids","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"The Macedonian dynasty","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"The Umayyads","content.quiz[8].explain":"The Umayyads ruled from Damascus from about 661 to 750 AD.","content.quiz[9].q":"Which collection of laws was created under Justinian?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"The Code of Justinian","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"The Twelve Tables","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"Russkaya Pravda","content.quiz[9].explain":"The Code of Justinian collected Roman laws and became a model for legal systems for many years.","content.quiz[10].q":"What is the Muslim pilgrimage to Mecca called?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"A religious procession","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"Hajj","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"The Olympic Games","content.quiz[10].explain":"Hajj is a pilgrimage to Mecca that every Muslim is expected to make if they are able.","content.quiz[11].q":"What did Emperor Leo III ban in churches?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Ringing bells","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Reading the Gospel","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Veneration of icons","content.quiz[11].explain":"Leo III began iconoclasm, a movement against the veneration of religious icons.","content.quiz[12].q":"Which city was the capital of the Umayyad Caliphate?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Damascus","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Baghdad","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Cairo","content.quiz[12].explain":"Damascus in Syria was the capital of the Umayyads.","content.quiz[13].q":"Who were stratiots in the Byzantine Empire?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"Priests","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"Farmer-soldiers who received land","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"Merchants","content.quiz[13].explain":"Stratiots served in the army and received land in return.","content.quiz[14].q":"Which branch of mathematics got its name from the word 'al-jabr'?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"Geography","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"History","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"Algebra","content.quiz[14].explain":"The word 'algebra' comes from a book by the Arab scholar al-Khwarizmi.","content.quiz[15].q":"What was a Byzantine military district ruled by a strategos called?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"Satrapy","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"Nomos","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"Theme","content.quiz[15].explain":"Themes were districts governed by a strategos where stratiot soldiers lived.","content.quiz[16].q":"Which numerals reached Europe through the Arabs?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"Indian numerals, which we call Arabic numerals","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"Roman numerals","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"Egyptian hieroglyphs","content.quiz[16].explain":"Arab scholars brought Indian numerals to Europe.","content.quiz[17].q":"What is the Golden Horn near Constantinople?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"A tower","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"A narrow sea inlet","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"An imperial crown","content.quiz[17].explain":"The Golden Horn is an inlet beside the city. A chain could be stretched across its entrance.","content.quiz[18].q":"In what year did Muhammad migrate from Mecca to Medina, marking the beginning of the Islamic calendar?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"570 AD","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"1453 AD","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"622 AD","content.quiz[18].explain":"This migration is called the Hijra. It took place in 622 AD.","content.quiz[19].q":"What blocked the entrance to the Golden Horn?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"A heavy chain","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"A stone wall","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"A wooden bridge","content.quiz[19].explain":"A thick chain was stretched between towers to keep enemy ships from entering.","content.quiz[20].q":"Where did the Franks stop the Arabs in 732 AD?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"At Thermopylae","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"At Poitiers","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"At Cannae","content.quiz[20].explain":"The Battle of Poitiers stopped the Arabs in Western Europe.","content.quiz[21].q":"Who came to power in Byzantium in 717 AD and saved Constantinople?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"Leo III","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"Justinian I","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"Heraclius I","content.quiz[21].explain":"Emperor Leo III the Isaurian led the defense of the city.","content.quiz[22].q":"Which scholar wrote The Canon of Medicine?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Al-Khwarizmi","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"Omar Khayyam","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"Ibn Sina","content.quiz[22].explain":"Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, wrote The Canon of Medicine.","content.quiz[23].q":"Which Bulgarian khan helped Byzantium in 718 AD?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"Krum","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"Tervel","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"Simeon","content.quiz[23].explain":"Khan Tervel attacked the Arabs, according to the chronicle of Theophanes.","content.quiz[24].q":"What does the word 'Islam' mean?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Submission to God","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"Victory","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Peace among nations","content.quiz[24].explain":"The word 'Islam' means 'submission' or 'surrender to God.'","content.quiz[25].q":"Which cathedral was converted into a mosque in 1453?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"The Church of the Holy Sepulchre","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"Notre-Dame Cathedral","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Hagia Sophia","content.quiz[25].explain":"After the Turks captured Constantinople, Hagia Sophia became a mosque.","content.quiz[26].q":"To which city did Muhammad migrate from Mecca?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Jerusalem","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Medina","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Damascus","content.quiz[26].explain":"He migrated to Medina, where the first Muslim community was formed.","content.quiz[27].q":"Which empire did Justinian want to restore?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"The Roman Empire","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"The Persian Empire","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Alexander's Empire","content.quiz[27].explain":"Justinian reconquered lands that had once belonged to Rome.","content.quiz[28].q":"What happened to the Umayyads in 750 AD?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"They conquered China","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"They were replaced by the Abbasids","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"They converted to Christianity","content.quiz[28].explain":"The Abbasids came to power, and Baghdad became the new capital.","content.quiz[29].q":"Who captured Constantinople in 1453?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"The Arabs","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"The Franks","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"The Ottoman Turks","content.quiz[29].explain":"Constantinople fell to the Ottomans led by Sultan Mehmed II.","content.events[0].title":"Power in the Empire","content.events[0].text":"The Arabs are marching toward the capital, and Emperor Theodosius III is weak. You are Leo, a strategos, and the army is calling you to Constantinople. What will you choose?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Go to the capital and take power","content.events[0].options[0].result":"That is what happened. In March 717 AD, Leo became emperor and had time to prepare the city for the siege.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Remain a strategos in Asia Minor","content.events[0].options[1].result":"The weak emperor would not have been able to defend the capital, leaving the city without a strong leader.","content.events[1].title":"Battle in the Strait","content.events[1].text":"The Arab fleet is entering the Bosporus. Should you sail out to meet it or wait until it reaches the chain across the Golden Horn?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Attack with fire ships","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Leo's fire ships set some of the enemy ships ablaze, weakening the Arab fleet.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"Wait by the chain without fighting","content.events[1].options[1].result":"As long as the enemy fleet remained intact, it could bring reinforcements to the besieging army.","content.events[2].title":"Help from the Bulgarians","content.events[2].text":"The walls are holding, but you have few soldiers. Khan Tervel, who has helped Byzantium before, lives nearby. Should you ask him for help?","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Ask the Bulgarians for help","content.events[2].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Khan Tervel's Bulgarians attacked the Arabs and, according to the chronicle of Theophanes, killed many enemy soldiers.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Rely only on your own forces","content.events[2].options[1].result":"Refusing an ally would have deprived the city of help when it needed it most.","content.events[3].title":"The Starving Arabs","content.events[3].text":"The winter of 717–718 AD is terrible. The Arabs are starving and freezing. Should you launch a major attack or stay behind the walls?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Stay behind the walls and let hunger do its work","content.events[3].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Leo protected his soldiers, while winter and hunger weakened the Arabs more than any assault could.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"March into the open field with the entire army","content.events[3].options[1].result":"The Arabs would have a better chance in the open field, and the defenders would risk losing the city.","content.events[4].title":"Egyptian Sailors","content.events[4].text":"Christian sailors from the Egyptian fleet secretly offer to join your side. Should you accept them?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Accept them and learn the fleet's plans","content.events[4].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Some Egyptian Christians joined the Byzantines, and the Arab fleet grew weaker.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Refuse because you do not trust them","content.events[4].options[1].result":"Refusing would mean missing a chance to learn the enemy's plans and weaken its fleet.","content.events[5].title":"The Arab Retreat","content.events[5].text":"The Arabs are loading their ships and leaving. Should you chase them with fire ships or let them go peacefully?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Send fire ships after them","content.events[5].options[0].result":"That is what happened. The retreating fleet was also caught in a storm. According to the chronicle of Theophanes, only a handful of thousands of ships returned.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Let the enemy leave without a fight","content.events[5].options[1].result":"Then the enemy might return with new forces.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Stratiot Militia","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"Stratiots were farmers who served in the army and received land in return. They bought their own weapons, so many had only javelins and a simple shield. They were cheap and quick to deploy when defending a city. Warriors like these made up much of the Byzantine militia.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Skutatoi","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"Skutatoi were Byzantine infantry soldiers armed with large shields and spears. They held their formation in the center and kept enemies away from the gates. They wore lamellar armor made of small overlapping plates. Their name comes from 'scutum,' meaning shield.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Cataphracts","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"Cataphracts were heavily armored horsemen riding protected horses. The Byzantines adopted this type of cavalry from the Persians and Romans. Cataphracts were especially dangerous against enemy cavalry. Light Bedouin horsemen had trouble withstanding their charge.","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Toxotai Archers","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"Toxotai were Byzantine archers who shot from walls and towers. The bow was one of the empire's most important weapons, and children learned to use it from an early age. Archers were effective against infantry without armor, but their arrows were less powerful against heavy armor.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Dromon with Greek Fire","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"A dromon was a fast warship powered by oars and a sail. A tube on its bow shot a stream of burning Greek fire. The fire kept burning on water and terrified enemies. Byzantium guarded the secret of its formula as a state secret.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Theodosian Walls Engineers","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"Engineers and builders repaired walls and towers during the siege. They closed gaps and held enemies back at the walls. The Theodosian Walls were built under Emperor Theodosius II in the 5th century. Strong walls often decided the outcome of a siege.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Arab Infantry","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"The caliph's army was mainly made up of foot soldiers recruited from across the caliphate. They advanced in large groups, lightly equipped and fast. They carried swords, spears, and round shields. They were weak alone but dangerous together.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Syrian Spearmen","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"Much of the Umayyad army came from Syria, the lands around Damascus. The spearmen were medium infantry: tough and well trained. They fought shoulder to shoulder, protecting themselves with shields. Many were experienced warriors.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Arab Archers","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"Arab archers were fast and accurate. They shot at the defenders on the walls and covered the attacking soldiers. Their light clothing helped them move quickly. But they were weaker than infantry in close combat.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Bedouin Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"The Bedouins were nomads from Arabia and excellent horsemen. They raided villages and supply trains on fast horses. Their light cavalry appeared and disappeared quickly. But they had a hard time fighting heavy cataphracts.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Camels","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"Camels carried supplies and soldiers across deserts and mountains. Horses were frightened by their smell, so camels could also help in battle. But during the winter outside Constantinople, many died from the cold. According to chroniclers, the Arabs lost many animals that winter.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"Ghulams","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"Ghulams were heavy Arab cavalry made up of professional warriors. They wore chain mail and helmets and carried round shields. These horsemen were the caliph's main striking force. Cataphracts were effective against them.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Sappers and Miners","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"Sappers dug tunnels beneath walls and towers to make them collapse. They worked under the protection of woven shields. Defenders dug their own tunnels to stop them. This kind of underground warfare was common during sieges.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Siege Tower","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"A siege tower was a tall wooden structure on wheels. Soldiers rolled it toward a wall so warriors could climb directly onto the wall. The tower was slow but sturdy. Fire was the best weapon against it.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Manjaniq","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"A manjaniq was an Arab siege engine that launched heavy stones. It was placed far from the walls and fired at towers. It fired slowly but with great force. The Theodosian Walls could withstand this bombardment.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Sulayman ibn Muad","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Sulayman ibn Muad was an Arab commander who led advance units through Asia Minor. He was a fast and brave horseman. His arrival meant that the caliph's entire army was close behind. Stopping his attack would buy the city valuable time.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Commander of the Arab Fleet","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"The Arab fleet had hundreds of ships and was supposed to surround the city from the sea. Chronicles give different names for its commander, so here he is identified by his position. Leo sent fire ships against him. Greek fire burned many vessels and disrupted the naval blockade.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Umar ibn Hubayra","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"According to Arab accounts, Umar ibn Hubayra was one of the commanders of this campaign. His warriors advanced in tight formation and protected one another. He was a determined and experienced commander. The defeat of his unit weakened the caliph's army.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Maslama ibn Abd al-Malik","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Maslama was the brother of two caliphs and commanded the land army outside Constantinople. He remained outside the city for more than a year, hoping to starve its defenders. Winter, hunger, and attacks by the Bulgarians weakened his army. In August 718 AD, he had to retreat.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Leo III the Isaurian","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Leo III was the strategos of the Anatolic Theme before becoming emperor in 717 AD. He prepared the city for the siege and skillfully used Greek fire and his allies. Later, he began iconoclasm, banning the veneration of icons. His dynasty is known as the Isaurian dynasty.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Khan Tervel","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Tervel was the ruler of Bulgaria and an ally of Byzantium. Earlier, he had helped Emperor Justinian II regain his throne and received the title of Caesar. In 718 AD, according to Theophanes, the Bulgarians defeated the Arabs in Thrace. This help made life easier for the besieged city.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Caliph Sulayman","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Sulayman was an Arab caliph of the Umayyad dynasty. He sent an enormous army and fleet against Constantinople and dreamed of capturing the great city. He died in September 717 AD, before the siege ended. The new caliph, Umar II, continued the war but no longer had the same confidence in victory.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Callinicus","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Callinicus is said to have been an engineer from Syria who invented Greek fire. According to tradition, he fled from the Arabs to Byzantium in the 670s AD. The Byzantines kept the exact formula secret. Historians still debate how it worked.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Caliph Umar II","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"Umar II became caliph after Sulayman in 717 AD. He saw that the siege was failing and ordered a retreat in 718 AD. He was considered a devout and just ruler. His reign was short, lasting only about three years.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"The Arabs March Through Asia Minor","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"In 716 AD, Arab armies marched through Asia Minor toward Constantinople. Leo the Isaurian, a strategos, used clever tricks against the enemy and prevented them from capturing Amorium. This success later helped him become emperor.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Leo III Takes Power","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"In March 717 AD, Leo arrived in Constantinople and became emperor. He immediately began gathering food supplies and repairing the walls. The city was preparing for a long siege.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"The Siege and Greek Fire","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"The Arabs besieged the city by land starting on August 15, 717 AD, and their fleet arrived in early September. Leo attacked the ships with fire vessels. Part of the Arab fleet burned, and the remaining ships no longer dared to enter the strait.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"The Terrible Winter","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"The winter of 717–718 AD was extremely harsh, and snow covered the ground for many weeks. Food ran short in the Arab camp, and horses and camels died. The besieged people suffered but held out behind the walls.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"Tervel's Bulgarians","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"In spring 718 AD, a new Arab fleet arrived from Egypt and Africa. Some Egyptian Christians switched to the Byzantine side. Around the same time, Khan Tervel's Bulgarians attacked the Arabs in Thrace.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"Retreat and Storm","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"On August 15, 718 AD, the Arabs lifted the siege. On their way home, their fleet was caught in a storm, and many ships were lost. Byzantium had successfully defended its capital and its lands.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"The Theodosian Walls","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"The Theodosian Walls were built under Emperor Theodosius II in the 5th century. They consisted of a moat and two walls, an outer wall and an inner wall, with towers. They protected Constantinople for more than a thousand years. Only in 1453 did enormous cannons finally break through them.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"Greek Fire","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"Greek fire was a flammable mixture launched through bronze tubes. Water could not put it out, and it set ships ablaze. The Byzantines kept its formula secret, and we still do not know its exact ingredients. Greek fire saved the empire more than once.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"The Byzantine Army and Themes","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"The empire was divided into themes, military districts governed by a strategos. Stratiot soldiers lived in these districts and received land in return for their service. Professional units of cataphracts and archers also served in the army. This system helped the empire gather troops quickly.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"Constantinople — The City","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"Constantinople stood between the Black Sea and the Sea of Marmara, on the Bosporus Strait. Trade routes between Europe and Asia passed through it. The city was one of the largest and richest in the world. The Golden Horn inlet played an important role in its defense.","content.encyclopedia[34].title":"What Happened Next","content.encyclopedia[34].text":"After 718 AD, the Arabs never again tried to capture Constantinople with such a large army. In 732 AD, Charles Martel's Franks stopped the Arabs at Poitiers. In this way, the Arab advance into Europe was stopped from two directions. Byzantium survived for about seven more centuries."},"gaugamela":{"defenders[5].name":"War Elephants","locked[1].name":"Crossbow","locked[2].name":"Knight","locked[3].name":"Cannon","enemies[2].name":"Cretan Archers","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"War Elephants","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Cretan Archers","title":"Gaugamela","year":"331 BC","side":"The Persians of Darius III","foe":"The Macedonians of Alexander","intro[0]":"You are Darius III, King of Kings. The vast Persian Empire still stands, but the Macedonians have already defeated you at the Granicus River and at Issus.","intro[1]":"Alexander is marching deep into your empire. Defend Darius's Camp against his phalanx, cavalry, and siege engines!","victoryText":"In history, Darius III lost. He fled at Gaugamela, and in 330 BC, the satrap Bessus killed him. But in this game, Darius's Camp held out, and you saw how history might have turned out differently.","defeatText":"The Macedonians broke through to the camp. That is how this war ended for Persia.","theme.spawnSign":"From Macedonia","fortress.name":"DARIUS'S CAMP","features[0].text":"Plain of Gaugamela","features[1].text":"Bumodus River (a tributary of the Tigris)","defenders[0].name":"Cardaces","defenders[0].note":"Cheap and fast; throw javelins","defenders[0].fact":"The Cardaces were Persian militia warriors. Historians disagree about whether they were light infantry or young soldiers from training units.","defenders[1].name":"Immortals","defenders[1].note":"Main fighter: spear and wicker shield","defenders[1].fact":"There were always ten thousand Immortals. If a warrior died or became ill, another immediately took his place.","defenders[2].name":"Greek Mercenaries","defenders[2].note":"Heavy hoplites; strong against infantry","defenders[2].fact":"Greek mercenaries stood close to Darius himself. They were the only soldiers who could face the Macedonian phalanx on equal terms.","defenders[3].name":"Persian Archers","defenders[3].note":"Ranged attacks: a cloud of arrows","defenders[3].fact":"Persian archers fired such thick volleys that, according to the Greeks, their arrows blocked out the sun.","defenders[4].name":"Scythed Chariots","defenders[4].note":"Charge through crowds: cut down everyone in their path","defenders[4].fact":"Sharp, curved blades were attached to the wheels and pole. The chariots charged straight into enemy formations, cutting down soldiers.","defenders[5].note":"Living wall: holds enemies back","defenders[5].fact":"Darius had 15 elephants from India, but according to some accounts, they barely took part in the battle and were captured by Alexander.","locked[0].name":"Stirrups","locked[0].line":"Horsemen still ride without stirrups. They will not be invented for many centuries.","locked[1].line":"Military crossbows will not reach Europe for more than a thousand years.","locked[2].line":"Knights in plate armor will not appear for another thousand years.","locked[3].line":"Gunpowder will reach Europe in about 1,600 years.","abilities[0].name":"Scythed Chariots","abilities[0].fact":"At Gaugamela, Darius sent about two hundred scythed chariots forward. He had ordered the battlefield leveled especially for them.","abilities[1].name":"The Immortals Rise Again","abilities[1].fact":"They were called Immortals because every fallen warrior was immediately replaced, keeping the unit at full strength.","enemies[0].name":"Thracians","enemies[0].fact":"Thracians served in the Macedonian army as light infantry armed with javelins and small shields.","enemies[1].name":"Agrianian Javelin Throwers","enemies[1].fact":"The Agrianians were mountain warriors and expert javelin throwers. They helped stop the chariots at Gaugamela.","enemies[2].fact":"Cretans were famous for their accurate archery, and the Macedonians gladly recruited them.","enemies[3].name":"Hypaspists","enemies[3].fact":"Hypaspists, or 'shield-bearers,' were elite infantry who fought near the king and could move quickly across the battlefield.","enemies[4].name":"Pezhetairoi","enemies[4].fact":"The pezhetairoi, or 'foot companions' of the king, fought in a tight phalanx with 16-foot spears called sarissas.","enemies[5].name":"Thessalian Cavalry","enemies[5].fact":"The Thessalians were considered the best horsemen in Greece. At Gaugamela, they held the left flank alongside Parmenion.","enemies[6].name":"Companion Cavalry","enemies[6].fact":"The Companions were the king's main striking force. Alexander personally led them in wedge-shaped charges.","enemies[7].name":"Alexander's Catapults","enemies[7].fact":"The Macedonians brought siege engines along with their army, mainly to attack fortified cities such as Tyre.","bosses[0].name":"Cleitus the Black","bosses[0].fact":"Cleitus commanded the royal cavalry squadron. At the Granicus River, he saved Alexander from a Persian nobleman's blow.","bosses[1].name":"Parmenion","bosses[1].fact":"Parmenion, Philip II's most experienced general, commanded the Macedonian left wing and held off the strongest Persian attacks.","bosses[2].name":"Hephaestion","bosses[2].fact":"Hephaestion had been Alexander's closest friend since childhood. He led troops and encouraged the soldiers.","bosses[3].name":"Alexander on Bucephalus","bosses[3].fact":"Alexander rode his black horse, Bucephalus. In battle, he led the Companion Cavalry in a wedge straight toward the king, and Darius lost his nerve.","chapters[0].title":"Granicus","chapters[0].year":"334 BC","chapters[0].fact":"In spring 334 BC, Alexander crossed the Granicus River and defeated the armies of the Persian satraps in Asia Minor.","chapters[1].title":"Issus","chapters[1].year":"333 BC","chapters[1].fact":"In the narrow valley near Issus, the Persian army could not spread out, and Darius III fled, leaving his family behind.","chapters[2].title":"Tyre and Egypt","chapters[2].year":"332 BC","chapters[2].fact":"Tyre held out for seven months, but Alexander built a causeway and captured the city. Egypt then surrendered without a fight.","chapters[3].title":"Gaugamela","chapters[3].year":"331 BC","chapters[3].fact":"On a flat plain, Darius waited for the Macedonians with chariots and elephants, but Alexander broke through the Persian lines and reached the king himself.","chapters[4].title":"Persepolis","chapters[4].year":"330 BC","chapters[4].fact":"The Macedonians entered Persepolis, captured the royal treasury, and the great palace burned down.","chapters[5].title":"The Flight of Darius and Bessus","chapters[5].year":"330 BC","chapters[5].fact":"Darius fled east, but the satrap Bessus captured him. In 330 BC, the king died from his wounds.","waves[0].title":"Scouts at the Granicus","waves[1].title":"Agrianian Javelin Throwers","waves[2].title":"Cretan Archers","waves[3].title":"Shield-Bearers","waves[4].title":"Cavalry Across the River","waves[5].title":"Issus: The Narrow Valley","waves[6].title":"Rain of Arrows","waves[7].title":"The Phalanx Advances","waves[8].title":"Thessalian Charge","waves[9].title":"Companions Strike the Center","waves[10].title":"Siege Engines Against Tyre","waves[11].title":"Formation of Sarissas","waves[12].title":"Through Egypt","waves[13].title":"The Causeway and Siege Engines","waves[14].title":"Cleitus the Black","waves[15].title":"Plain of Gaugamela","waves[16].title":"Cavalry on the Flank","waves[17].title":"Battering Rams","waves[18].title":"The Companion Wedge","waves[19].title":"Parmenion Holds the Flank","waves[20].title":"Road to Persepolis","waves[21].title":"Persian Gates","waves[22].title":"Fire Above the Palace","waves[23].title":"Chasing the King","waves[24].title":"Hephaestion","waves[25].title":"Flight to the East","waves[26].title":"Raid on the Unit","waves[27].title":"The Last Line of Defense","waves[28].title":"Pursuit at the Caspian Gates","waves[29].title":"Alexander on Bucephalus","content.waveFacts[0]":"In 334 BC, Alexander crossed the Hellespont with an army of about forty thousand men and marched against the Persian satraps.","content.waveFacts[1]":"Light Thracian and Agrianian troops led the Macedonians, entering battle first with their javelins.","content.waveFacts[2]":"Cretan archers fired from behind the infantry and showered the Persian satraps' forces with arrows at the Granicus.","content.waveFacts[3]":"The hypaspists were the first to enter the waters of the Granicus, while the Persians threw javelins at them from the high bank.","content.waveFacts[4]":"The Thessalian cavalry crossed the Granicus at a ford, and Cleitus saved Alexander from a deadly blow.","content.waveFacts[5]":"In fall 333 BC, near the city of Issus, the Macedonians turned back and found Darius and his army in a narrow valley.","content.waveFacts[6]":"In the crowded valley at Issus, the Persian archers could not spread out across a wide front, even though they had plenty of arrows.","content.waveFacts[7]":"The Macedonian phalanx, armed with long sarissas, advanced slowly but steadily, like a wall of pikes.","content.waveFacts[8]":"The Thessalians fought off the Persian cavalry on the banks of the Pinarus River while Alexander fought in the center.","content.waveFacts[9]":"Alexander led the Companion Cavalry toward Darius, who fled the battlefield in panic, leaving his family behind.","content.waveFacts[10]":"In 332 BC, Alexander besieged the island city of Tyre and brought siege engines to break down its walls.","content.waveFacts[11]":"The Macedonians built a causeway to the island. The siege of Tyre lasted seven months.","content.waveFacts[12]":"After Tyre, Alexander entered Egypt, where the Persian satrap surrendered without a fight.","content.waveFacts[13]":"In Egypt, Alexander founded the coastal city of Alexandria, where a famous library later grew.","content.waveFacts[14]":"Cleitus the Black, the king's friend and warrior, led the Companion Cavalry and inflicted heavy losses on the Persian horsemen.","content.waveFacts[15]":"Darius ordered the plain near Gaugamela to be leveled so his scythed chariots could race across it.","content.waveFacts[16]":"The Macedonians formed their lines to protect their flanks. They placed a second line behind the first to defend against Persian cavalry.","content.waveFacts[17]":"Alexander's siege engines followed behind, while a forest of sarissas moved forward.","content.waveFacts[18]":"The Companion Cavalry formed a wedge and struck a gap that had opened between the Persian lines.","content.waveFacts[19]":"Parmenion held off a powerful Persian attack on the left wing while Alexander broke through toward Darius.","content.waveFacts[20]":"After Gaugamela, Alexander captured Babylon and Susa, then marched toward Persepolis, where the royal treasury was kept.","content.waveFacts[21]":"At the Persian Gates, the satrap Ariobarzanes defended a mountain pass, but the Macedonians found a path around his position.","content.waveFacts[22]":"The Macedonians entered Persepolis and captured its treasures. Around that time, the great royal palace reportedly burned down.","content.waveFacts[23]":"Alexander pursued Darius closely to prevent him from raising another army in the east.","content.waveFacts[24]":"Hephaestion, Alexander's friend, accompanied him on his campaigns and commanded part of the army.","content.waveFacts[25]":"Darius retreated toward Bactria, but the Persian nobles were losing faith in their defeated king.","content.waveFacts[26]":"Macedonian horsemen caught up with Darius's troops, and many Persians abandoned their supply wagons and fled.","content.waveFacts[27]":"Bessus, the satrap of Bactria, and other nobles captured Darius and carried him away in chains.","content.waveFacts[28]":"Bessus went into hiding, hoping to keep control of the eastern lands, but his army was growing smaller.","content.waveFacts[29]":"Alexander caught up with the group. Darius lay wounded and soon died. In 330 BC, the Achaemenid Empire came to an end.","content.quiz[0].q":"On which river did Alexander first defeat the Persians in 334 BC?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"Granicus","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"Nile","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"Euphrates","content.quiz[0].explain":"This was Alexander's first major victory over the satraps of Asia Minor.","content.quiz[1].q":"Who founded the Persian Empire in the 6th century BC?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"Darius I","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"Cyrus the Great","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"Xerxes","content.quiz[1].explain":"Cyrus the Great defeated the Medes and King Croesus of Lydia, and captured Babylon.","content.quiz[2].q":"What were the Persian king's provincial governors called?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Strategoi","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Archons","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Satraps","content.quiz[2].explain":"A province was called a satrapy. Its satrap collected tribute and commanded troops.","content.quiz[3].q":"Which king built a strong Macedonian army with a phalanx?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"Alexander the Great","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"Darius I","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"Philip II","content.quiz[3].explain":"Philip II, Alexander's father, turned Macedonia into the strongest state in Greece.","content.quiz[4].q":"What was the great road across the empire of Darius I called?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"Royal Road","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"Silk Road","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"Appian Way","content.quiz[4].explain":"The Royal Road stretched from Sardis to Susa and had stations for messengers.","content.quiz[5].q":"Where was Darius III defeated in 333 BC, fleeing and leaving his family behind?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"At the Granicus","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"At Issus","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"At Marathon","content.quiz[5].explain":"Darius's mother and wife were captured, but Alexander treated them with respect.","content.quiz[6].q":"Who taught Alexander the Great when he was a child?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"Socrates","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"Aristotle","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"Demosthenes","content.quiz[6].explain":"The great philosopher Aristotle taught Alexander in the Macedonian town of Mieza.","content.quiz[7].q":"What was the long spear used by Macedonian phalanx soldiers called?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Javelin","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Battle-axe","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Sarissa","content.quiz[7].explain":"The sarissa was about 16–20 feet long and helped soldiers form a wall of spears.","content.quiz[8].q":"What was the name of the good god of light in Zoroastrianism?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"Ahura Mazda","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"Zeus","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"Amun-Ra","content.quiz[8].explain":"Zoroaster taught that the good god Ahura Mazda fought against the evil spirit Ahriman.","content.quiz[9].q":"What did Alexander do with the tangled knot at Gordium?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"Cut it with his sword","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"Untied it with his hands","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"Burned it","content.quiz[9].explain":"A prophecy said that whoever untied the knot would rule Asia. Alexander cut it with his sword.","content.quiz[10].q":"How many months did Alexander besiege the island city of Tyre?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"One month","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"Three months","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"Seven months","content.quiz[10].explain":"Tyre stood on an island, so the Macedonians built a causeway to reach its walls.","content.quiz[11].q":"Which city did Alexander found in Egypt?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Antioch","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Alexandria","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Pergamon","content.quiz[11].explain":"Alexandria, on the Mediterranean coast, became a famous center of learning.","content.quiz[12].q":"What was the gold coin of Darius I called?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Denarius","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Daric","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Obol","content.quiz[12].explain":"Gold darics were minted throughout the empire and were known even in Greece.","content.quiz[13].q":"What was the name of Alexander the Great's favorite horse?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"Bucephalus","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"Pegasus","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"Rocinante","content.quiz[13].explain":"According to legend, Alexander tamed the spirited Bucephalus when he was a boy.","content.quiz[14].q":"Whom did Philip II defeat at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BC?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"The Spartans","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"The Romans","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"Athens and Thebes","content.quiz[14].explain":"After Chaeronea, the Greek city-states accepted Philip II's leadership.","content.quiz[15].q":"Where did the decisive battle between Alexander and Darius III take place in 331 BC?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"At Issus","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"At Thermopylae","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"At Gaugamela","content.quiz[15].explain":"Gaugamela was on a plain in what is now Iraq. There, Darius's power collapsed.","content.quiz[16].q":"What weapons did Darius hope would break the phalanx at Gaugamela?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"Catapults","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"Scythed chariots","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"Battering rams","content.quiz[16].explain":"The chariots had blades attached to their wheels and poles, but the Macedonians opened gaps in their formation and let the chariots pass through.","content.quiz[17].q":"How many warriors were in the Immortals?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"Ten thousand","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"One hundred","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"One thousand","content.quiz[17].explain":"Their numbers were always kept at full strength, which is why they were called the Immortals.","content.quiz[18].q":"What was a satrapy?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"A province ruled by a satrap","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"A Persian coin","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"A fire temple","content.quiz[18].explain":"Darius I divided the empire into about twenty satrapies.","content.quiz[19].q":"Who commanded the Macedonian left wing at Gaugamela?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"Cleitus","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"Parmenion","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"Hephaestion","content.quiz[19].explain":"The experienced Parmenion held off the strongest Persian attack.","content.quiz[20].q":"What happened to the royal palace at Persepolis in 330 BC?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"It was left untouched","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"It was destroyed by an earthquake","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"It burned down during a feast","content.quiz[20].explain":"The palace burned down. Ancient writers say it happened after a feast.","content.quiz[21].q":"Which Persian noble captured Darius III in 330 BC?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"Memnon","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"Parmenion","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"Bessus, satrap of Bactria","content.quiz[21].explain":"Bessus killed Darius and declared himself king, but Alexander captured and executed him.","content.quiz[22].q":"About how many kilometers long was the Royal Road?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"About 2,500","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"About 250","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"About 25,000","content.quiz[22].explain":"Messengers raced along the Royal Road from Sardis to Susa.","content.quiz[23].q":"In which country did Alexander fight King Porus and his elephants in 326 BC?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"Egypt","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"India","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"Scythia","content.quiz[23].explain":"This was the Battle of the Hydaspes River, where Porus's elephants frightened the Macedonian horses.","content.quiz[24].q":"What did Alexander's soldiers refuse to do at the Hyphasis River?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Fight the Greeks","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"March farther east","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Drink water","content.quiz[24].explain":"The exhausted soldiers wanted to go home, so Alexander turned back.","content.quiz[25].q":"In which city did Alexander the Great die in 323 BC?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"Athens","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"Pella","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Babylon","content.quiz[25].explain":"Alexander fell ill in Babylon and died before his 33rd birthday.","content.quiz[26].q":"What is the period when Greek culture spread across the East called?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Hellenism","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"The Renaissance","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"The Middle Ages","content.quiz[26].explain":"Hellenism began after Alexander's campaigns and ended when Rome conquered the Hellenistic kingdoms.","content.quiz[27].q":"How old was Alexander when he died?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"Almost 33","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"About 60","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"About 20","content.quiz[27].explain":"He was born in 356 BC and died in 323 BC.","content.quiz[28].q":"What happened to Alexander's empire after his death?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"His son ruled it until old age","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"It remained united for another hundred years","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"His generals, the Diadochi, divided it","content.quiz[28].explain":"The Diadochi, or 'successors,' divided the empire into kingdoms. The best known were Egypt, Syria, and Macedonia.","content.quiz[29].q":"Which general took control of Egypt after Alexander's death?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"Seleucus","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"Ptolemy","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"Antigonus","content.quiz[29].explain":"Ptolemy founded a dynasty that ruled Egypt for almost three centuries.","content.events[0].title":"Memnon's Advice","content.events[0].text":"334 BC. Memnon of Rhodes, a Greek commander serving Darius, advises the Persians to avoid battle, burn their fields, and retreat so the Macedonians will run out of food. The satraps do not want to burn their own lands. What will you choose?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Burn the fields and avoid battle","content.events[0].options[0].result":"That was Memnon's advice, and many consider it a wise plan. But the satraps refused, fought at the Granicus, and lost.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Meet the Macedonians at the river","content.events[0].options[1].result":"That is what the satraps did. The Macedonians crossed the river and defeated them, while nearly all the Greek mercenaries were killed or captured.","content.events[1].title":"Where Should We Wait for the Enemy?","content.events[1].text":"Fall 333 BC. Darius stands with an enormous army on a wide plain. Alexander is delayed, and the courtiers say he is afraid. Should you wait here or march toward him through narrow passes?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Wait on the wide plain","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That was the advice of Amyntas, a Macedonian defector. But Darius hurried into the narrow valley near Issus, where his greater numbers could not help him.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"March toward Alexander","content.events[1].options[1].result":"That is what Darius actually did. In the narrow space between the mountains and the sea, his army could not spread out, and he lost.","content.events[2].title":"A Letter to Alexander","content.events[2].text":"332 BC. Tyre has fallen, and Egypt is surrendering. Darius can offer Alexander peace: an enormous ransom, his daughter's hand in marriage, and all lands up to the Euphrates River. What should he do?","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Offer peace and the lands up to the Euphrates","content.events[2].options[0].result":"According to ancient accounts, Darius made such offers after both Issus and Tyre. Parmenion said he would accept if he were Alexander, but Alexander refused.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Refuse to negotiate","content.events[2].options[1].result":"Then Darius would miss his chance to save at least part of his empire and would have to gather an army for a decisive battle.","content.events[3].title":"The Night Before the Battle","content.events[3].text":"September 30, 331 BC. The plain near Gaugamela has already been leveled for the chariots, and the Macedonians are camped a few miles away. You expect a night attack. Parmenion reportedly suggested one to Alexander. What will you do?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Keep the army under arms all night","content.events[3].options[0].result":"That is what Darius did. Alexander did not attack at night, and the Persians faced the morning tired, although they were ready for battle.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Let the army get some sleep","content.events[3].options[1].result":"The risk would be great: a night attack could catch the camp by surprise. Darius chose not to take that risk, although the sleepless night exhausted his soldiers.","content.events[4].title":"Flee or Stand Your Ground?","content.events[4].text":"Winter 331–330 BC. You fled from Gaugamela to Ecbatana. Alexander has captured Babylon and Susa and is marching toward Persepolis. Should you wait here with the remaining troops or retreat east to Bactria?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Retreat east to Bactria","content.events[4].options[0].result":"That is what Darius did when Alexander marched toward Ecbatana. Few soldiers followed him, and the Persian nobles were already losing faith in their king.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Wait for Alexander at Ecbatana","content.events[4].options[1].result":"At first, Darius waited, hoping Alexander would get stuck at Persepolis. But eventually he fled anyway, losing time and soldiers.","content.events[5].title":"Who Will Guard the King?","content.events[5].text":"Summer 330 BC. The satrap Bessus and other nobles are whispering about a plot. Patron, the leader of the Greek mercenaries, asks permission to guard the king. What will you say?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Agree and let the Greeks guard you","content.events[5].options[0].result":"In history, Darius refused because he did not want to offend the nobles, according to Curtius Rufus. The Greek guards might have prevented the plot, but we cannot know for sure.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Refuse and trust the nobles","content.events[5].options[1].result":"That is what Darius actually did. Soon Bessus captured him and put him in chains, then dealt him a fatal wound. The king died in 330 BC.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Cardaces","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"The Cardaces were Persian warriors mentioned only briefly by ancient writers. Historians disagree about whether they were light infantry or young soldiers from special units. Little is known about their weapons, so they throw javelins in the game. They are the cheapest and fastest defenders.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Immortals","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"The Immortals were an elite unit of ten thousand warriors who served as the king's personal guard. Whenever one died, another took his place, making the unit seem as if it never lost soldiers. They wore fine clothing and carried wicker shields and spears. Some reportedly had golden ornaments shaped like fruit on the ends of their spears. They were the backbone of the Persian infantry.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Greek Mercenaries","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"Greek hoplites served Persian kings in exchange for pay. They carried heavy equipment: a spear, a round shield, body armor, and a helmet. At Gaugamela, they stood near Darius and were the only troops who could stand up to the Macedonian phalanx. Many remained loyal to him until his final days.","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Persian Archers","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"Persians learned archery from childhood and fired their bows in volleys. Archers stood behind wicker shields and showered enemies with arrows. The Greeks said there were so many arrows that they blocked out the sun. But arrows were less effective against armor.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Scythed Chariots","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"Sharp, curved blades were attached to the wheels and poles of these chariots. The chariots raced straight into enemy formations, cutting down anyone in their path. At Gaugamela, Darius had about two hundred of them and ordered the battlefield leveled beforehand. But the Macedonians opened gaps in their formation, while the Agrianians threw javelins at the drivers and horses.","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"Darius had 15 elephants from India at Gaugamela. Ancient writers say they barely took part in the battle and were captured by Alexander along with the camp. An elephant is a living wall: horses fear it, and infantry hesitate to approach. In the game, elephants slow enemies on their way to the camp.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Thracians","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"Thrace lay north of Macedonia, and its warriors served in Alexander's army. Thracians wore fox-fur caps and cloaks, threw javelins, and fought with swords. They were brave but lightly protected. That is why they appear in the game as the largest groups of enemies.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Agrianian Javelin Throwers","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"The Agrianians lived in the mountains north of Macedonia and were excellent javelin throwers. Alexander valued their speed and kept them close to him. At Gaugamela, the Agrianians stopped the scythed chariots by throwing javelins at the horses and drivers.","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"The island of Crete was famous for its archers. Cretans shot their bows quickly and accurately, and the Macedonians gladly recruited them. They wore no heavy armor and stayed behind the infantry. That made Cretan arrows dangerous from a distance.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Hypaspists","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"The name means 'shield-bearers.' These were elite infantry soldiers in the Macedonian army who marched beside the king. Hypaspists could move quickly across the battlefield and fight in the mountains. To stop them, you need strong fighters as well as archers.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Pezhetairoi","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"The word means 'foot companions.' These soldiers formed the Macedonian phalanx: a tight formation of warriors carrying very long spears called sarissas. The front rank was protected by a forest of spear points, with several more ranks behind it. The phalanx was hard to stop, but it moved slowly.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"Thessalian Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"Thessaly was a region of Greece with rich pastures, and its cavalry was famous throughout the ancient world. At Gaugamela, the Thessalians fought on the left wing under Parmenion. They were fast and dangerous, but vulnerable to accurate archers.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Companion Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"The Companions were the king's heavy Macedonian cavalry. Alexander personally led them into battle in a wedge formation, shaped like an arrowhead. The horsemen wore armor and helmets and carried long spears. Spearmen and a wall of elephants are effective against them.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Alexander's Catapults","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"The Macedonians knew how to build siege engines and brought them along with their army. They were especially useful during the siege of the island city of Tyre. These machines are slow but sturdy, and they will cause serious damage if they reach the camp. Stop them on the road.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Cleitus the Black","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"Cleitus commanded the royal cavalry squadron and was Alexander's friend. At the Granicus River, he saved the king by cutting off an attacking Persian's arm. Later, in 328 BC, they argued at a feast, and Alexander killed him in anger. Historians believe the king deeply regretted it afterward.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Parmenion","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Parmenion was Philip II's most experienced general and later served Alexander. At Gaugamela, he commanded the left wing and held off the strongest enemy attacks. Alexander did not always follow his advice. Parmenion knew how to keep his soldiers in formation even under the heaviest attacks.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Hephaestion","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Hephaestion had been Alexander's closest friend since childhood. He commanded part of the army and encouraged the soldiers. When he died in 324 BC, Alexander was overcome with grief. In the game, Hephaestion makes nearby warriors move faster.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Alexander on Bucephalus","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"Alexander the Great became king at the age of twenty and reached India during his twelve-year reign. His black horse, Bucephalus, was known throughout the army. In battle, Alexander led the Companion Cavalry in a wedge and personally charged the enemy. He never lost a major battle.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Darius III","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Darius III became king of Persia in 336 BC and ruled for only a short time. He lost to Alexander at the Granicus, Issus, and Gaugamela, then fled east. In 330 BC, Bessus captured him, and Darius died from his wounds. Alexander buried him with honors.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Alexander the Great","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Alexander was born in 356 BC in Pella, the son of King Philip II. Aristotle was his teacher. He conquered Persia and Egypt, reached India, and died in Babylon in 323 BC. After his death, his empire broke apart.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Philip II","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Philip II ruled Macedonia from 359 BC. He built a powerful army with a phalanx and conquered Greece after winning the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BC. In 336 BC, he was assassinated at his daughter's wedding. His son Alexander continued his work.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Parmenion: Adviser to Kings","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Parmenion served Philip II and then Alexander, becoming his right-hand man. He gave cautious advice and often disagreed with the king. According to ancient accounts, he suggested a night attack before Gaugamela, but Alexander refused. In 330 BC, Parmenion was killed on the king's orders.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Bessus","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Bessus was the satrap of Bactria and a relative of Darius III. In 330 BC, he and other nobles captured and wounded the king, then Bessus declared himself king. Alexander caught and executed Bessus. This marked the end of the Achaemenid dynasty's rule.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Battle of the Granicus, 334 BC","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"In spring 334 BC, the Macedonians reached the Granicus River. The Persian satraps stood on the high riverbank. Alexander crossed the river and attacked the center, while Cleitus saved the king during the fighting. The Persians fled, and the Greek mercenaries were killed or captured.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"Battle of Issus, 333 BC","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"In fall 333 BC, Darius moved behind the Macedonian army, forcing Alexander to turn around and fight in a narrow valley between the mountains and the sea. Alexander sent the Companion Cavalry against the Persian left flank and advanced straight toward the king. Darius fled, and his mother and wife were captured.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Siege of Tyre, 332 BC","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"Tyre stood on an island, so the Macedonians could not reach its walls. They built a causeway and constructed siege engines. The siege lasted seven months before the city was captured. Egypt then surrendered without a fight, and Alexander founded Alexandria.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"Battle of Gaugamela, 331 BC","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"Darius chose a flat plain and assembled chariots, elephants, and an enormous army. Alexander arranged his troops to protect their flanks, while the Agrianians met the chariots with javelins. Then the Companion Cavalry charged in a wedge through a gap in the Persian lines, and Darius fled. This decided the fate of the empire.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"Persepolis, 330 BC","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"In winter 330 BC, the Macedonians entered Persepolis, one of Persia's most important cities. They captured an enormous royal treasury. The great royal palace burned down. Some say it happened after a feast, but historians still debate the cause. For the Persians, it was a sign of the end.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"The Flight of Darius, 330 BC","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"Darius fled east, but the Persian nobles had lost faith in him. The satrap Bessus captured the king and carried him away in chains. When the Macedonians caught up, Bessus wounded Darius and escaped, and the king died. In history, Darius was defeated, and this was the final struggle of the Achaemenid dynasty.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"The Macedonian Phalanx","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"A phalanx was a tight formation of warriors carrying long spears called sarissas, about 16 feet long. The front ranks lowered their spears, creating a wall of spear points. The Greeks invented the phalanx, and Philip II made it especially powerful. Its weaknesses were attacks from the sides and uneven ground, so cavalry protected its flanks.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"The Persian Empire and Its Satrapies","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"The Achaemenid Persian Empire stretched from Egypt and Asia Minor to the borders of India. King Darius I divided it into provinces called satrapies and appointed a satrap to govern each one. Satraps collected tribute and commanded troops. The Royal Road crossed the empire, with stations and messengers along the way.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"Bucephalus","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"Bucephalus was Alexander's black horse. According to legend, young Alexander calmed him after realizing the horse was afraid of his own shadow. Alexander turned him toward the sun. His name means 'ox head.' The horse traveled across Asia with the king and reportedly died in India, where Alexander founded a city in his honor.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Babylon","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"Babylon was a huge city on the Euphrates River and the capital of an ancient kingdom. It had the famous Ishtar Gate and a great ziggurat tower. The Persians conquered it under Cyrus in 539 BC. Alexander made Babylon one of the centers of his empire and died there in 323 BC.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"Hellenism: What Happened Next","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"After Alexander's death, his generals, called the Diadochi, divided his empire. New kingdoms appeared: Ptolemaic Egypt, Seleucid Syria, and Macedonia. Greek language and culture spread as far as India. Rome later conquered these lands, but Alexander's legacy lived on."},"hannibal":{"side":"Romans","intro[0]":"218 BC. The Carthaginian general Hannibal crossed the Alps with an army and 37 war elephants.","victoryText":"The war ended with the Battle of Zama in 202 BC: Publius Cornelius Scipio defeated Hannibal, and Carthage asked for peace.","ui.subtitle":"Second Punic War","ui.startButton":"Defend Rome","ui.coinTitle":"Denarii","ui.livesTitle":"Walls of Rome","ui.camTop":"Alps","ui.camBottom":"Rome","ui.encUnits":"Romans","ui.encFoes":"Enemies of Rome","ui.bossLabel":"General of Carthage","ui.winTitle":"Rome stood firm!","ui.winSub":"202 BC","ui.loseTitle":"Hannibal at the gates! Rome has fallen","theme.spawnSign":"From the Alps","fortress.name":"ROME","water[2].sign":"Lake Trasimene","defenders[0].name":"Velites","defenders[0].note":"Fast javelins, ×2 damage to elephants","defenders[0].fact":"Velites were the youngest and poorest soldiers, armed with javelins. Some wore wolf skins over their helmets.","defenders[1].name":"Legionaries","defenders[1].note":"Pila, strong against infantry","defenders[1].fact":"The pilum was a throwing spear that bent on impact, so the enemy couldn’t throw it back.","defenders[2].name":"Triarii","defenders[2].note":"Slow spears, ×2 damage to cavalry","defenders[2].fact":"Triarii were veteran soldiers who stood in the last line. The saying “it has come down to the triarii” meant that things were getting desperate.","defenders[3].name":"Archers","defenders[3].note":"Long range, rapid attacks, effective against fast enemies","defenders[3].fact":"The Romans hired archers from Crete — they had almost no archers of their own.","defenders[4].name":"Ballista","defenders[4].note":"Very long range, heavy area damage","defenders[4].fact":"A ballista hurled stones and bolts up to 400 paces.","defenders[5].name":"Trumpeters","defenders[5].note":"No damage; nearby elephants panic","defenders[5].fact":"At the Battle of Zama, Roman trumpets helped frighten Hannibal’s elephants. Some ran back into their own army.","locked[0].name":"Cannon","locked[0].line":"Gunpowder will reach Europe 1,500 years later.","locked[1].name":"Knight","locked[1].line":"Stirrups and full plate armor belong to a much later era, not ancient Rome.","locked[2].name":"Crossbow","locked[2].line":"It will appear in Europe only a thousand years later.","locked[3].name":"Machine gun","locked[3].line":"That’s a completely different era.","abilities[0].name":"Fabius’s tactics","abilities[0].fact":"Fabius Maximus, known as “the Delayer,” avoided major battles and wore down Hannibal’s army. All enemies are slowed for 6 seconds.","abilities[1].name":"Scipio’s legions","abilities[1].fact":"Scipio trained his legions in new tactics and defeated Carthage. Defenders deal 50% more damage for 6 seconds.","enemies[0].name":"Gallic Allies","enemies[0].fact":"The Gallic tribes of northern Italy went over to Hannibal’s side.","enemies[1].name":"Iberian Swordsmen","enemies[1].fact":"Iberians from Spain fought with a short, sharp sword — later the Romans adopted this sword themselves.","enemies[2].name":"Libyan Infantry","enemies[2].fact":"Hannibal’s Libyan foot soldiers wore armor taken from defeated Romans.","enemies[3].name":"Balearic Slingers","enemies[3].fact":"Slingers from the Balearic Islands could hit a target from 100 paces.","enemies[4].name":"Numidian Cavalry","enemies[4].fact":"The Numidians were the best riders of ancient times — they rode without saddles or bridles.","enemies[5].name":"War Elephants","enemies[5].fact":"Of Hannibal’s 37 elephants, only one survived the winter after crossing the Alps.","enemies[6].name":"Celtiberians","enemies[6].fact":"Celtiberians from central Spain wore black cloaks, and javelins got stuck in their sturdy shields.","enemies[7].name":"Gallic Cavalry","enemies[7].fact":"At Cannae, Gallic and Iberian horsemen beat the Roman cavalry by the river.","enemies[8].name":"Sacred Band","enemies[8].fact":"The Sacred Band was elite infantry made of noble citizens of Carthage dressed in white.","bosses[0].name":"Maharbal","bosses[0].fact":"Maharbal commanded the Numidian cavalry — Hannibal’s fastest horsemen.","bosses[1].name":"Hasdrubal","bosses[1].fact":"Hasdrubal was Hannibal’s brother; enemies near him stand firmer and take less damage.","bosses[2].name":"Mago","bosses[2].fact":"Mago was Hannibal’s younger brother; from time to time he vanishes from sight, and your towers can’t see him.","bosses[3].name":"Hannibal","bosses[3].fact":"Hannibal lost an eye in the marshes of Etruria in 217 BC, but kept leading his army.","chapters[0].title":"Trebia","chapters[0].year":"218 BC","chapters[0].fact":"At the Trebia, Hannibal lured the Romans into an icy river and then struck from an ambush.","chapters[1].title":"Lake Trasimene","chapters[1].year":"217 BC","chapters[1].fact":"At Lake Trasimene, Hannibal hid his army in the fog on the hills and attacked from above.","chapters[2].title":"Cannae","chapters[2].year":"216 BC","chapters[2].fact":"At Cannae, Hannibal surrounded 80,000 Romans; it is said that after the battle Maharbal told him: “You know how to win a victory, but not how to use it.”","chapters[3].title":"War of attrition","chapters[3].year":"215–208 BC","chapters[3].fact":"Hasdrubal led a second army across the Alps, but the Romans intercepted his letter to his brother.","chapters[4].title":"Metaurus","chapters[4].year":"207 BC","chapters[4].fact":"At the Metaurus, the Romans defeated Hasdrubal and threw his head into Hannibal’s camp.","chapters[5].title":"Zama","chapters[5].year":"202 BC","chapters[5].fact":"The war ended with the Battle of Zama in 202 BC: Publius Cornelius Scipio defeated Hannibal, and Carthage asked for peace.","waves[0].title":"Gallic Allies","waves[1].title":"Gallic Allies","waves[2].title":"Iberian Swordsmen","waves[3].title":"Slingers and Gauls","waves[4].title":"Ambush by the river","waves[5].title":"Libyan Infantry","waves[6].title":"Numidian Cavalry","waves[7].title":"Fog over the hills","waves[8].title":"Cavalry and slingers","waves[9].title":"The first elephant","waves[10].title":"Celtiberians","waves[11].title":"Hannibal’s crescent","waves[12].title":"Elephants and slingers","waves[13].title":"Cavalry avalanche","waves[14].title":"Maharbal","waves[15].title":"Hordes of Gauls","waves[16].title":"Gallic Cavalry","waves[17].title":"Iberians and Celtiberians","waves[18].title":"Cavalry raid","waves[19].title":"Hasdrubal","waves[20].title":"Sacred Band","waves[21].title":"White shields","waves[22].title":"Four elephants","waves[23].title":"Five elephants","waves[24].title":"Mago","waves[25].title":"The whole army of Carthage","waves[26].title":"Cavalry from both sides","waves[27].title":"Eight elephants","waves[28].title":"The final assault","waves[29].title":"Hannibal and his guard","content.waveFacts[0]":"In autumn 218 BC, Hannibal led his army into Italy over the Alps. The Romans did not expect him to come from that direction at all.","content.waveFacts[1]":"Many Gauls in northern Italy were unhappy with Rome. After the first battles, they joined Hannibal and fought on his side.","content.waveFacts[2]":"Numidian horsemen would charge the Romans and then pretend to run away. This is how Hannibal lured the enemy to the Trebia River.","content.waveFacts[3]":"Hannibal brought about 37 elephants over the Alps (so writes Polybius), but almost all of them died from the cold in Italy that winter.","content.waveFacts[4]":"The Balearic slingers threw stones and lead pellets so far and so accurately that many armies hired them.","content.waveFacts[5]":"In spring 217 BC, Hannibal's army marched for four days through the marshes by the Arno River. There Hannibal fell very ill and lost the sight in one eye.","content.waveFacts[6]":"Consul Gaius Flaminius was hot-headed and stubborn. He followed right behind Hannibal without scouting the road.","content.waveFacts[7]":"Iberian swordsmen used short swords that worked well for both stabbing and slashing in close combat.","content.waveFacts[8]":"At dawn, thick fog lay over Lake Trasimene. The Romans could not see the enemy on the hills until the enemy struck from above.","content.waveFacts[9]":"At Lake Trasimene, according to Polybius, about 15,000 Romans died, and Consul Flaminius fell in battle.","content.waveFacts[10]":"In 216 BC, Rome gathered a huge army: about 80,000 men, counting its allies.","content.waveFacts[11]":"Hannibal's Gallic and Iberian cavalry stood on the left wing by the river and were the first to break the Roman horsemen.","content.waveFacts[12]":"At Cannae, Hannibal placed Gauls and Iberians in the center and Libyan soldiers on the flanks. The Romans were surrounded.","content.waveFacts[13]":"The consuls Varro and Aemilius Paullus took turns in command, one every other day. Hannibal picked a day and a place that suited him for the battle.","content.waveFacts[14]":"It is said that after Cannae, the cavalry commander Maharbal asked Hannibal to march on Rome, but Hannibal did not dare.","content.waveFacts[15]":"After Cannae, Rome did not ask for peace. It recruited even very young men and others who had not been allowed to serve before.","content.waveFacts[16]":"Fabius Maximus was nicknamed “Cunctator,” the “Delayer.” He avoided big battles and wore the enemy down instead.","content.waveFacts[17]":"After Cannae, many cities in southern Italy went over to Hannibal, including rich Capua.","content.waveFacts[18]":"The Celtiberians were warlike tribes of central Spain. Both the Carthaginians and the Romans hired them.","content.waveFacts[19]":"Hannibal's brother Hasdrubal stayed in Spain. In 208 BC, Scipio defeated him, but Hasdrubal escaped and set out for Italy.","content.waveFacts[20]":"In 207 BC, Hasdrubal crossed the Alps and entered Italy to join Hannibal.","content.waveFacts[21]":"The Sacred Band was made up of chosen citizens of Carthage itself. Most of the Carthaginian army, however, was made up of hired soldiers.","content.waveFacts[22]":"The Romans caught Hasdrubal's messengers carrying a letter to Hannibal, and so they learned where and when the brothers planned to meet.","content.waveFacts[23]":"Consul Nero secretly led about 7,000 of his best soldiers north. At the battle by the Metaurus River, the Romans had twice as many men.","content.waveFacts[24]":"The Metaurus: Hasdrubal was killed, and the brothers never met. Later, their younger brother Mago tried to keep fighting in Italy.","content.waveFacts[25]":"In 204 BC, Scipio sailed his army to Africa, and Carthage had to defend its own land.","content.waveFacts[26]":"The Numidian prince Masinissa switched to Rome's side and brought excellent cavalry with him.","content.waveFacts[27]":"In 203 BC, Carthage called Hannibal home. He left Italy, where he had fought for about fifteen years.","content.waveFacts[28]":"At Zama, Hannibal had about 80 elephants. But Scipio left lanes open in his battle line, and many elephants ran right past the Romans.","content.waveFacts[29]":"The victory at Zama in 202 BC ended the war, and Scipio received the nickname Africanus.","content.quiz[0].q":"In what year, according to legend, was Rome founded?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"509 BC","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"264 BC","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"753 BC","content.quiz[0].explain":"According to Roman legend, Rome was founded in 753 BC. Historians cannot confirm this exact date.","content.quiz[1].q":"Who, according to legend, founded the city of Rome?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"Romulus","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"Aeneas","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"Hannibal","content.quiz[1].explain":"According to legend, a she-wolf nursed the twin brothers Romulus and Remus. Romulus founded the city and became its first king. Aeneas was only thought of as a distant ancestor of the Romans.","content.quiz[2].q":"What was the name of Hannibal's father, the great Carthaginian general?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Hasdrubal","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Hamilcar Barca","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Mago","content.quiz[2].explain":"Hamilcar Barca fought Rome in the First Punic War. According to Polybius, he made little Hannibal swear to be Rome's enemy.","content.quiz[3].q":"Which mountains did Hannibal cross to bring his army and elephants into Italy?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"The Pyrenees","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"The Apennines","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"The Alps","content.quiz[3].explain":"Hannibal crossed the Alps in autumn 218 BC. The journey was hard: there was cold, there were rockslides, and mountain tribes attacked.","content.quiz[4].q":"What trick did Hannibal use at the Trebia?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"He lured the hungry Romans across an icy river","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"He sent burning boats down the river","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"He dug under the walls of the Roman camp","content.quiz[4].explain":"The Numidians drew the Romans out, and the Romans waded across the cold winter river without eating breakfast. The tired soldiers were then met by rested Carthaginians and an ambush.","content.quiz[5].q":"How many consuls were elected each year in the Roman Republic?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"One","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"Two","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Ten","content.quiz[5].explain":"There were two consuls, and their power was equal. This way the Romans wanted to stop any one man from becoming king.","content.quiz[6].q":"What helped Hannibal set an ambush at Lake Trasimene?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"Heavy rain and a storm","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"A night attack on the camp","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"Thick morning fog and the hills along the road","content.quiz[6].explain":"The Roman army was marching in a column between the lake and the hills, and the fog hid the Carthaginians. They struck from all sides, and the Romans had no time to form a battle line.","content.quiz[7].q":"Who were the plebeians in Ancient Rome?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Ordinary free citizens: farmers, craftsmen, and traders","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Nobles who came from the first great families","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Slaves brought back from wars","content.quiz[7].explain":"Patricians belonged to Rome’s old noble families. Plebeians were other free Roman citizens. For a long time, only patricians could hold the highest offices.","content.quiz[8].q":"What was the senate in Ancient Rome?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"The Roman army","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"A council of noble and experienced men that decided important matters for the state","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"A temple of the chief gods","content.quiz[8].explain":"The senate was made up of former officials and noble Romans. It decided questions of war, peace, money, and relations with other countries.","content.quiz[9].q":"In what year BC did the Romans drive out their last king and create a republic?","content.quiz[9].explain":"The last king was Tarquin the Proud. After he was driven out in 509 BC, power passed to elected officials.","content.quiz[10].q":"What happened to the Roman army at the Battle of Cannae?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"The Romans were surrounded and suffered a terrible defeat","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"The Romans broke through the center and chased the enemy","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"Both armies walked away without fighting","content.quiz[10].explain":"Hannibal let the Romans push into his center, and then the Libyans and the cavalry struck from the sides. The Romans were surrounded, and tens of thousands of men died.","content.quiz[11].q":"What were the small units that a Roman legion was divided into called?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Phalanxes","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Maniples","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Elephant cohorts","content.quiz[11].explain":"Maniples could move more easily than a solid phalanx. This helped a legion change formation and fight on uneven ground. The maniples formed three lines.","content.quiz[12].q":"What does the expression “Pyrrhic victory” mean?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"A victory without a single loss","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"A victory won by trickery","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"A victory won at such a high cost that it is almost the same as a defeat","content.quiz[12].explain":"King Pyrrhus beat the Romans at Heraclea and Asculum, but he lost so many soldiers that it became hard for him to keep fighting.","content.quiz[13].q":"Which island did Rome and Carthage mainly fight over in the First Punic War?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"Sicily","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"Sardinia","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"Crete","content.quiz[13].explain":"The First Punic War lasted from 264 to 241 BC. Rome won, and Sicily became its first province.","content.quiz[14].q":"What is Maharbal said to have advised Hannibal to do after the victory at Cannae?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"Ask Rome for peace","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"March on Rome at once","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"Go back to Carthage","content.quiz[14].explain":"Livy writes that Maharbal told Hannibal: “You know how to win a victory, but you do not know how to use one.” Hannibal did not march on Rome. He had no siege machines and not enough men.","content.quiz[15].q":"What was the tactic of Fabius Maximus?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"Attack Hannibal's camp every day","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"Ask Egypt for help","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"Avoid big battles, wear the enemy down, and cut off his supplies","content.quiz[15].explain":"Fabius followed Hannibal through the hills, where the enemy cavalry was weaker. That is why he was nicknamed Cunctator, which means the Delayer.","content.quiz[16].q":"Who was a dictator in ancient Rome?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"A man given special power for a short time in a dangerous period","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"The richest citizen","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"The winner of the games","content.quiz[16].explain":"A dictator was appointed in a hard hour, usually for no more than six months. Fabius Maximus became dictator after Lake Trasimene.","content.quiz[17].q":"Which big city of Italy went over to Hannibal after Cannae?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"Ostia","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"Capua","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"Antium","content.quiz[17].explain":"Capua was one of the largest cities in Italy. The Romans besieged it and captured it in 211 BC, and Hannibal could not save it.","content.quiz[18].q":"Which famous scientist helped defend Syracuse in Sicily against the Romans?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"Euclid","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"Pythagoras","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"Archimedes","content.quiz[18].explain":"Archimedes built throwing machines and lifting devices. The Roman general Marcellus took Syracuse in 212 BC, and Archimedes was killed when the city was captured.","content.quiz[19].q":"How was Hasdrubal related to Hannibal?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"He was his brother","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"He was his son","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"He was his uncle on his mother's side","content.quiz[19].explain":"Hasdrubal was Hannibal's younger brother. He stayed in Spain, and in 207 BC he led an army to Italy to help Hannibal.","content.quiz[20].q":"What did the Roman consul Nero do before the battle at the Metaurus?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"Made peace with Hannibal","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"Secretly led part of his army north to strike Hasdrubal","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"Retreated to Rome","content.quiz[20].explain":"Nero left his camp facing Hannibal and quickly marched north with his best soldiers. After joining the other consul, he attacked Hasdrubal.","content.quiz[21].q":"Who were the triarii in a Roman legion?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"The youngest and poorest soldiers","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"Archers and slingers","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"The most experienced soldiers, who stood in the third line","content.quiz[21].explain":"The triarii joined the fight when the first two lines were in trouble. This gave the Romans a saying: “It has come down to the triarii,” meaning the situation was desperate.","content.quiz[22].q":"Why did the Roman plebeians demand that the laws be written on tablets?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"So that judges could not twist the laws to suit themselves","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"To decorate the temples","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"To hand power to a king","content.quiz[22].explain":"The Law of the Twelve Tables was written down around 450 BC and put on display in the public square so that everyone could read it.","content.quiz[23].q":"How did the battle at the Metaurus River in 207 BC end?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"With a victory for Hasdrubal","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"With the destruction of Hasdrubal's army and his death","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"With a peace treaty","content.quiz[23].explain":"The Romans defeated Hasdrubal's army, and he himself was killed. The brothers Hannibal and Hasdrubal never joined their armies.","content.quiz[24].q":"Which of Hannibal's brothers still tried to fight in northern Italy after the Metaurus?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Hamilcar","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"Masinissa","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Mago","content.quiz[24].explain":"Mago landed in northern Italy in 205 BC and recruited Gauls and Ligurians. Two years later, he was defeated and wounded. He died on his way home. Masinissa was a Numidian prince, not Hannibal's brother.","content.quiz[25].q":"What bold plan did Scipio come up with to end the war?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"Take the war to Africa, to Carthage itself","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"Surround Rome with a wall","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Give Sicily to Hannibal","content.quiz[25].explain":"Scipio landed in Africa in 204 BC. The senator Fabius was against it because he thought it was too risky. But the plan worked: Carthage called Hannibal back to help.","content.quiz[26].q":"What did the plebeians do in 494 BC, according to legend, when they demanded protection from the nobles?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Set fire to the Capitol","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Left the city for the Sacred Mount and won the right to have tribunes of the people","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Drove the senators into exile","content.quiz[26].explain":"The tribunes of the people could stop an unfair decision by an official. This greatly strengthened the position of the plebeians.","content.quiz[27].q":"Who was Rome's ally in Africa and commanded the Numidian cavalry at Zama?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"Hanno","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"Pyrrhus","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Masinissa","content.quiz[27].explain":"Masinissa was first an ally of Carthage, and then he went over to Rome. His cavalry helped Scipio at Zama.","content.quiz[28].q":"How did Scipio deal with Hannibal's war elephants at Zama?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"He left lanes between his units, and the elephants ran right through","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"He dug a deep ditch","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"He set the field on fire","content.quiz[28].explain":"Scipio lined up his maniples so that there were wide lanes between them. With the noise of trumpeters and with spears, the Romans drove the elephants into these lanes or back onto their own soldiers.","content.quiz[29].q":"What honorary nickname did Scipio receive after defeating Carthage?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"Cunctator","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"Africanus","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"The Proud","content.quiz[29].explain":"The victory at Zama in 202 BC ended the Second Punic War. Scipio became Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus, while Fabius was the one called Cunctator.","content.events[0].title":"The Trebia: The Consuls' Council","content.events[0].text":"December 218 BC. You are Consul Sempronius. Hannibal is camped beyond the Trebia River. Your fellow consul Scipio is wounded and lying in camp, and your soldiers are eager to fight. New consuls will be elected soon, and the glory of victory would go to you.","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Accept battle and cross the river","content.events[0].options[0].result":"This is what Sempronius did. The Romans waded into the icy water on an empty stomach and were surrounded. Only part of the army escaped.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Wait for Scipio to recover and for spring to arrive","content.events[0].options[1].result":"This was Scipio's advice, but Sempronius did not listen. Rested and well-fed Romans on their own bank would have fought much better, and Hannibal would have had to spend the winter in a foreign land.","content.events[1].title":"Trasimene: Chase Through the Hills","content.events[1].text":"Spring 217 BC. You are Consul Flaminius. Hannibal has marched around your army and is burning the nearby fields. People in Rome demand that you stop him. The road runs between the lake and the hills, and morning fog covers the water.","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Follow him in a column without delay","content.events[1].options[0].result":"This is what Flaminius did. The Romans fell into an ambush between the lake and the hills, and about 15,000 men died, including the consul himself.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"First send scouts up onto the hills","content.events[1].options[1].result":"Flaminius did not do this. Scouts would have spotted the soldiers on the slopes, and the Romans could have chosen their own place to fight instead of walking into a trap.","content.events[1].options[2].label":"Wait in camp for the other consul","content.events[1].options[2].result":"Flaminius did not wait. Together they would have been stronger, but in that time Hannibal could have ruined even more land, and the Romans wanted to protect their fields.","content.events[2].title":"Cannae: Plain or Hills","content.events[2].text":"Summer 216 BC. You are a consul, and you have a huge army of about 80,000 men. Hannibal is camped on an open plain, where he has more and stronger horsemen. Your fellow consul advises you not to fight on such ground, but your soldiers demand a battle.","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Accept battle on the plain","content.events[2].options[0].result":"This is what happened at Cannae. Hannibal surrounded the Roman army, and tens of thousands died, including Consul Aemilius Paullus.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Refuse and lead the army into the hills","content.events[2].options[1].result":"This is what Aemilius Paullus wanted. In the hills, Hannibal's cavalry could not have surrounded the Romans, and they would not have paid so terribly for the mistake.","content.events[3].title":"After Cannae: How to Go On","content.events[3].text":"Autumn 216 BC. Rome has lost almost its whole field army, and some of its allies have gone over to Hannibal. Some senators suggest asking for peace. Others suggest raising a new army and fighting another great battle. Fabius Maximus suggests something different.","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Fight one more great battle","content.events[3].options[0].result":"Rome tried this many times, and it kept losing armies. Hannibal is stronger in the open field, especially with his cavalry.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Follow Hannibal without fighting big battles, and take back cities one by one","content.events[3].options[1].result":"This is what Rome did. Over time, Hannibal struggled to find new allies or replace his lost soldiers, while the Romans recaptured Capua and many other cities.","content.events[3].options[2].label":"Ask Hannibal for peace","content.events[3].options[2].result":"The senate refused both peace and a ransom for the prisoners. Rome decided to fight to the end, and that saved it.","content.events[4].title":"The Metaurus: A Secret March","content.events[4].text":"207 BC. You are Consul Claudius Nero. You face Hannibal in southern Italy, while his brother Hasdrubal is marching through northern Italy. If their armies join forces, Rome will be in great danger. Your fellow consul in the north needs help.","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Hold your position and wait to see what happens","content.events[4].options[0].result":"Then Hasdrubal would have faced only one consul and could have joined his brother. The risk would have been too great.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Secretly lead part of your army north","content.events[4].options[1].result":"This is what Nero did. He moved very fast, and for a long time Hannibal did not know about it. The Romans attacked Hasdrubal with two armies and defeated him.","content.events[5].title":"Scipio: Where to Go","content.events[5].text":"205 BC. You are Scipio. Hannibal has been in Italy for many years, and no one can drive him out. Fabius thinks Italy must be defended. But you have won in Spain, and you know that Carthage has few troops at home.","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Defend Italy and wait","content.events[5].options[0].result":"This is what Fabius wanted. The war would have dragged on forever, because Hannibal would have stayed in Italy.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Land in Africa, next to Carthage","content.events[5].options[1].result":"This is what Scipio did in 204 BC. Carthage had to call Hannibal home, and in 202 BC Rome won the victory at Zama.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Velites","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"Velites were the light infantry of the Roman army. They were young and not rich, so they could not afford much armor. They carried a small round shield and threw javelins. They started the battle from a distance and then pulled back behind the heavy infantry.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Legionaries","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"Legionaries were the backbone of the Roman army. The first two lines, the hastati and the principes, threw heavy spears called pila and then fought with short swords. A pilum bent after it was thrown, so an enemy shield with a pilum stuck in it was hard to carry. Every legionary wore a helmet and armor and carried a large shield.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Triarii","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"The triarii were the legion's most experienced soldiers, usually older veterans. They stood in the third line and joined the fight when the first two lines were in trouble. They carried long spears. The Roman saying “It has come down to the triarii” meant that the situation was desperate.","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Cretan Archers","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"Crete was famous for its skilled archers. The Romans hired Cretan archers to fight alongside their troops. Arrows could travel farther than javelins, allowing archers to attack from a distance. Archers were vulnerable in close combat, so heavy infantry protected them.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Ballista","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"A ballista was a war machine that launched heavy bolts or stones. It used tightly twisted ropes or animal tendons to store energy. The Romans used these machines mainly during sieges. Archimedes of Syracuse was famous for designing powerful war machines.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Trumpeters","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"In the Roman army, orders were given by the sound of trumpets and horns. Soldiers knew the signals by ear, because a voice cannot be heard in the noise of battle. At a signal, units moved forward, pulled back, or changed formation. A good trumpeter helped the army act together.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Gallic Allies","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"The Gauls were Celtic peoples who lived in northern Italy and beyond the Alps. Some opposed Roman rule and joined Hannibal after his first victories. Many fought fiercely with long swords. At Cannae, Hannibal placed Gallic and Iberian soldiers in the center of his battle line.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Iberian Swordsmen","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"The Iberians were peoples of ancient Spain, and many served in Hannibal's army. They used short swords that worked well for both stabbing and slashing. According to Polybius, Iberian soldiers at Cannae wore white tunics with red borders. They were tough, experienced fighters.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Libyan Infantry","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"The Libyans were heavy infantry from the lands of Africa that were ruled by Carthage. They were Hannibal's most trusted soldiers. Polybius writes that at Cannae they were armed with captured Roman weapons. At the edges of the line, they turned and struck the Romans from the sides.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Balearic Slingers","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"The Balearic Islands lie off the coast of Spain. Their slingers were famous for their accuracy. Ancient writers say they learned to use slings as children. They launched stones and lead pellets at enemies before close combat began.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Numidian Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"The Numidians were people of North Africa and excellent horsemen. Livy writes that they hardly used bridles and steered their horses with their voices and knees. They would charge quickly, throw javelins, and ride away at once. With such tactics they lured the Romans to the Trebia.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"War Elephants","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"According to Polybius, Hannibal brought about 37 elephants across the Alps. Elephants frightened horses and could break enemy formations. But the cold winter in Italy killed almost all of them. Only one survived. According to ancient accounts, Hannibal later rode this last elephant through the marshes.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Celtiberians","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"The Celtiberians were peoples of central Spain. They were skilled warriors known for their well-made swords. Both Carthage and Rome hired Celtiberian soldiers to fight in their armies.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Gallic Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"Gallic horsemen were brave and strong. At Cannae, they fought alongside Iberian cavalry on Hannibal's left wing, near the river. They defeated the Roman horsemen facing them, then rode around to attack the Roman army from behind. This helped Hannibal surround the Romans.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"The Sacred Band of Carthage","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"The Sacred Band was made up of chosen soldiers who were citizens of Carthage itself. Most of the Carthaginian army was drawn from hired soldiers and subject peoples, and there were few citizens among the soldiers. That is why such a unit was considered especially honorable. It was strong and tough, but there were not many of them.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Maharbal","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Maharbal was the commander of Hannibal's cavalry. Livy writes that at Lake Trasimene his horsemen defeated a unit of Roman cavalry. After Cannae, it is said, he advised marching on Rome and told Hannibal that he knew how to win a victory but not how to use one.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Hasdrubal","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Hasdrubal was Hannibal's younger brother. He stayed in Spain and fought against Scipio. In 208 BC he was defeated, but he managed to lead his army away and then set out for Italy. He crossed the Alps faster than Hannibal had, but he was killed in battle at the Metaurus River in 207 BC.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Mago","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"Mago was Hannibal's younger brother. He was with Hannibal in Italy and took part in the ambush at the Trebia. After Cannae, he carried the news of the victory to Carthage. In 205 BC he landed in northern Italy and gathered Gauls and Ligurians, but in 203 BC he was defeated. He was wounded and died on the way home.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Hannibal","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Hannibal was born in 247 BC into the family of Hamilcar Barca. According to Polybius, his father made him swear as a boy to be Rome's enemy. In 218 BC he crossed the Alps and fought in Italy for about fifteen years. He lost at Zama in 202 BC, later fled to the East, and is believed to have died around 183 BC.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Scipio Africanus","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Publius Cornelius Scipio was a young Roman general. He defeated the Carthaginians in Spain and captured their main city, New Carthage. In 204 BC he took the war to Africa. In 202 BC he beat Hannibal at Zama and received the nickname Africanus.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Fabius Maximus Cunctator","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Quintus Fabius Maximus became dictator after Lake Trasimene. He did not rush into big battles. Instead he followed Hannibal, cut off his supplies, and saved his own strength. He was nicknamed Cunctator, which means the Delayer. He saved Rome from more defeats.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Marcus Claudius Marcellus","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Marcellus was a bold Roman general. Fabius was sometimes called the “shield” of Rome, and Marcellus its “sword.” In 212 BC he captured Syracuse, where the scientist Archimedes died. In 208 BC Marcellus was killed in an ambush.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Masinissa","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Masinissa was a prince of Numidia, a region of North Africa. At first he fought on the side of Carthage, including in Spain. Then he switched to Rome's side. At Zama in 202 BC, his cavalry helped Scipio defeat Hannibal.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Gaius Terentius Varro","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"Varro was consul in 216 BC and commanded the Roman army at Cannae alongside Aemilius Paullus. He was eager to fight Hannibal. Varro survived the defeat and gathered the remaining soldiers. According to Livy, the senate thanked him for not giving up hope of saving Rome.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"Gaius Claudius Nero","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"Nero was consul in 207 BC. He was facing Hannibal in southern Italy when he learned that Hasdrubal was approaching from the north. Nero secretly led about 7,000 of his best soldiers north. There, he joined the other consul and defeated Hasdrubal at the Metaurus River.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Battle of the Trebia, 218 BC","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"Hannibal's first big battle in Italy was fought in winter. The Numidians drew the Romans across the cold river, and the Romans went into battle hungry and frozen. Part of the Carthaginian army waited in ambush, and the Roman columns were surrounded. Many Romans died, but about 10,000 broke through to the city of Placentia.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"Battle of Lake Trasimene, 217 BC","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"Consul Flaminius followed right behind Hannibal. The road ran between the lake and the hills, and in the morning there was thick fog. The Carthaginians attacked from the hills on all sides, and the Romans had no time to form up. According to Polybius, about 15,000 Romans were killed and as many were taken prisoner. Flaminius was killed.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"Battle of Cannae, 216 BC","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"Hannibal had about 50,000 soldiers, and the Romans had many more. He put the Gauls and Iberians in the center and the Libyans on the sides. The Romans pushed into the center, the Libyans struck from the sides, and the cavalry got behind the Romans. Tens of thousands of Romans died, including Consul Aemilius Paullus. It was one of the worst defeats in Rome's history.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"The War of Attrition, 215–208 BC","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"After Cannae, Rome did not ask for peace and did not give Hannibal big battles. The Romans besieged Capua and captured it in 211 BC, and Syracuse in 212 BC. Hannibal took Tarentum in 212 BC, but Fabius won it back in 209 BC. Hannibal was short of men and supplies.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"Battle of the Metaurus, 207 BC","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"Hasdrubal was marching from Spain to join Hannibal. The Romans caught his messengers with a letter and learned where and when the brothers planned to meet. Consul Nero quickly led part of his army north and, together with the other consul, struck Hasdrubal. Hasdrubal's army was defeated, and he himself was killed.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"Battle of Zama, 202 BC","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"This was the deciding battle of the Second Punic War. Hannibal put about 80 elephants and three lines of soldiers in the field. Scipio left lanes open in his battle line, and the elephants ran past the Romans or back onto their own men. Masinissa's Numidian cavalry struck Hannibal from behind. Hannibal lost, and Carthage accepted a harsh peace.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"How a Legion Was Built","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"At that time, a Roman legion had about 4,200 foot soldiers and 300 horsemen. The infantry was divided into velites, hastati, principes, and triarii. The soldiers were split into small units called maniples. Maniples lined up in three rows in a checkerboard pattern, so fresh fighters could replace tired ones.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Consuls, the Senate, and the Dictator","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"In the Roman Republic, two consuls with equal power were elected every year. They led armies and managed government affairs. The senate was a council of influential Romans that advised leaders and guided important decisions. In an emergency, a dictator could be appointed with special powers, usually for no more than six months.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"What Was Carthage?","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"Carthage was a wealthy city-state in North Africa, in what is now Tunisia. It was founded by Phoenicians from the eastern Mediterranean. Carthage became a major trading power with a strong navy and controlled lands in Africa, Spain, and several islands. The Romans called the Carthaginians Poeni, which is why their wars are known as the Punic Wars.","content.encyclopedia[34].title":"Crossing the Alps","content.encyclopedia[34].text":"In autumn 218 BC, Hannibal led his army and elephants across the Alps. The crossing took about two weeks. Mountain tribes attacked, paths collapsed, and snow made the journey dangerous. Livy tells a story that Hannibal heated huge rocks with fire and poured vinegar on them to crack them. Hannibal lost many soldiers along the way and reached Italy with a much smaller army than the one that had left Spain.","content.encyclopedia[35].title":"The First Punic War","content.encyclopedia[35].text":"This war lasted from 264 to 241 BC, mostly over the island of Sicily. Rome had not been a sea power before, but it built a big fleet. It is said that the Romans used boarding bridges on their ships, which helped them to board enemy ships. In 241 BC Rome won, and it got Sicily and money from Carthage. The Carthaginians were commanded by Hamilcar Barca.","content.encyclopedia[36].title":"What Happened to Carthage","content.encyclopedia[36].text":"After Zama, Carthage lost its fleet and its lands overseas, and it promised to pay Rome a lot of money. Half a century passed, and the city grew rich again. Then the Romans started the Third Punic War. In 146 BC, Carthage was captured and destroyed. It is said that the Roman senator Cato kept repeating: “Carthage must be destroyed.”","title":"Hannibal at the Gates","year":"218–202 BC","foe":"Hannibal's Carthage","intro[1]":"Place Roman soldiers along the road and keep the enemy away from the walls of Rome. Survive 30 waves across six battles of the great war!","defeatText":"Hannibal is at the gates! Rome has fallen, but you can start the war again.","ui.coinWord":"denarii","ui.loseHint":"Try using trumpeters and velites against elephants, and triarii against cavalry.","ui.eventBonus":"Fabius and Scipio are ready again."},"ice":{"defenders[3].name":"Archers","locked[3].name":"Cannon","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Archers","title":"Battle on the Ice","year":"1242 AD","side":"The Novgorodians and Alexander Nevsky's troops from Vladimir","foe":"The Livonian Order, the Bishop of Dorpat, and their allies","intro[0]":"The Livonian Order has captured Izborsk and Pskov and approached the lands of Novgorod. Prince Alexander is only twenty-one years old, but he has already won a victory on the Neva River.","intro[1]":"Your mission is to meet the knights on the ice of Lake Peipus near Raven Stone and stop their 'iron wedge' from breaking through to the fortress.","intro[2]":"In the game, troops of the Order and its allies march along the road. This is how we show all the enemies of those years, including the Swedes of 1240.","victoryText":"That is what happened in history: on April 5, 1242, the Russians won, defeated the knights and their allies, and chased them across the ice. The Order asked for peace and gave up its claims to Russian lands. The famous words 'Whoever comes to us with a sword will die by the sword' were spoken in a 1938 movie, not in the chronicles. The Life of Alexander contains a similar idea: 'God is not in strength, but in truth.'","defeatText":"The knights' wedge broke through the formation, opening the road to Novgorod. But this did not happen in real history.","theme.spawnSign":"From Dorpat","fortress.name":"RAVEN STONE","features[0].text":"Uzmen Strait","features[1].text":"Lake Teploye","defenders[0].name":"Novgorod Militia","defenders[0].note":"Cheap units with heavy spears against horses","defenders[0].fact":"Craftsmen and merchants from Novgorod joined military campaigns. A rogatina, a heavy spear with a broad tip, helped stop charging horses.","defenders[1].name":"Foot Soldiers","defenders[1].note":"Main fighting force: spear and shield","defenders[1].fact":"Foot soldiers wore chain mail, pointed helmets, and almond-shaped shields that protected almost their entire bodies.","defenders[2].name":"Mounted Retainers","defenders[2].note":"Heavy cavalry, strong against horsemen","defenders[2].fact":"The prince's personal troops were few but well armed. Alexander led them into battle and fought at the front.","defenders[3].note":"Attack infantry from a distance","defenders[3].fact":"The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle says the Russians had many archers and that they were the first to face the knights' charge.","defenders[4].name":"Crossbowmen","defenders[4].note":"Bolts pierce armor","defenders[4].fact":"The Russians called a crossbow a samostrel. Crossbows were known by the 13th century, and their heavy bolts could pierce chain mail.","defenders[5].name":"Front Line","defenders[5].note":"Shield-bearers hold back enemies","defenders[5].fact":"The front line was the leading part of a Russian regiment. Shield-bearers formed a solid wall to withstand the first attack.","locked[0].name":"Hand Cannon","locked[0].line":"Gunpowder will reach Rus' in 140 years","locked[1].name":"Mobile Wooden Fortress","locked[1].line":"In 300 years","locked[2].name":"Armored Knight","locked[2].line":"Full plate armor will appear in 150 years","locked[3].line":"The first cannons will appear in Rus' in 140 years","abilities[0].name":"Right-Wing Regiment","abilities[0].fact":"The Russian army was divided into regiments: the main regiment, right and left wings, vanguard, and guard. A strike from the flank often decided a battle.","abilities[1].name":"Nevsky","abilities[1].fact":"The nickname 'Nevsky' became associated with the prince later. But he defeated the Swedes on the Neva River when he was twenty, and his warriors followed him without fear.","enemies[0].name":"Knechts","enemies[0].fact":"Knechts were ordinary foot soldiers of the Order, hired or recruited from conquered lands.","enemies[1].name":"The Order's Estonian Allies","enemies[1].fact":"The Russians called Estonians and other Baltic peoples 'Chud.' The Order forced them to fight, although some also fought on the Russian side.","enemies[2].name":"Order Crossbowmen","enemies[2].fact":"The Order's crossbowmen fired from a distance, then took cover behind the infantry's shields.","enemies[3].name":"Mounted Sergeants","enemies[3].fact":"Sergeants were members of the Order who were not knights by birth. They rode lighter horses and wore simpler armor.","enemies[4].name":"Squires","enemies[4].fact":"Squires helped knights in battle, handed them weapons, and fought on foot themselves.","enemies[5].name":"The Bishop of Dorpat's Soldiers","enemies[5].fact":"Bishop Hermann of Dorpat had his own army. The Rhymed Chronicle mentions it separately from the Order's knights.","enemies[6].name":"Brother Knights","enemies[6].fact":"The Order's knights wore white cloaks with black crosses and attacked in a wedge. Some say the ice cracked beneath their weight, but this is a later legend, not something found in the chronicles.","enemies[7].name":"Danish Knights","enemies[7].fact":"Denmark controlled Reval and northern Estonia. Danish knights may have joined the Order's campaign, although historians debate whether they actually fought on the ice.","bosses[0].name":"Commander of Fellin","bosses[0].fact":"Commanders led the Order's castles, and Fellin, now Viljandi, was one of them. The commander's name is not given in the sources, so this character represents several possible commanders.","bosses[1].name":"Bishop Hermann of Dorpat","bosses[1].fact":"Hermann von Buxhoeveden was Bishop of Dorpat from 1224 and sent his troops against the Russians. Whether he personally fought on the ice is unknown.","bosses[2].name":"Andreas von Velven","bosses[2].fact":"According to the Rhymed Chronicle, Andreas von Velven was one of the Order's leaders in Livonia at the time. Scholars debate whether he led the army onto the ice.","bosses[3].name":"Master of the Order","bosses[3].fact":"The Order's leader in Livonia was called the master. In the game, this is a fictional representative, since it is unknown whether the real master took part in the battle.","chapters[0].title":"Battle of the Neva","chapters[0].year":"1240 AD","chapters[0].fact":"The Swedes, led by Jarl Birger, landed near the mouth of the Izhora River. Twenty-year-old Alexander attacked quickly and unexpectedly. In the game, the Order's soldiers appear instead of the Swedes.","chapters[1].title":"The Order Captures Izborsk and Pskov","chapters[1].year":"1240–1241 AD","chapters[1].fact":"In fall 1240, the Order captured Izborsk and then Pskov, where traitors among the local boyars helped them.","chapters[2].title":"Alexander Returns and Captures Koporye","chapters[2].year":"1241 AD","chapters[2].fact":"The Novgorodians invited the prince back, and he captured Koporye, which the Order had turned into a fortress on Russian land.","chapters[3].title":"The Liberation of Pskov","chapters[3].year":"1242 AD","chapters[3].fact":"In March 1242, Alexander entered Pskov with his brother Andrey and sent two of the Order's governors, called vogts, to Novgorod in chains.","chapters[4].title":"Domash's Scouts and the Retreat onto the Ice","chapters[4].year":"1242 AD","chapters[4].fact":"Domash Tverdislavich's scouting party encountered the knights and was defeated. Alexander withdrew his army onto the ice of Lake Peipus.","chapters[5].title":"Battle on the Ice","chapters[5].year":"April 5, 1242","chapters[5].fact":"Near Raven Stone at Uzmen, the Russians stopped the knights' wedge, defeated it, and chased their enemies for seven versts to the shore.","waves[0].title":"First Units","waves[1].title":"Estonian Allies and Knechts","waves[2].title":"Attack at the Izhora River","waves[3].title":"Archers on the Shore","waves[4].title":"First Horsemen","waves[5].title":"Beneath the Walls of Izborsk","waves[6].title":"The Order Marches on Pskov","waves[7].title":"The Bishop's Soldiers","waves[8].title":"The First Knights","waves[9].title":"The Fall of Pskov","waves[10].title":"Raids on the Vozha Lands","waves[11].title":"The Danes March toward Koporye","waves[12].title":"The Siege of Koporye","waves[13].title":"The Iron Wall","waves[14].title":"Commander of Fellin","waves[15].title":"Toward the Walls of Pskov","waves[16].title":"The Order's Garrison","waves[17].title":"The Assault on Pskov","waves[18].title":"The Order's Counterattack","waves[19].title":"Bishop Hermann","waves[20].title":"Scouting near Hamaste","waves[21].title":"The Chase after Domash","waves[22].title":"The Order Follows the Trail","waves[23].title":"Toward Lake Peipus","waves[24].title":"Andreas von Velven","waves[25].title":"Onto the Ice","waves[26].title":"Near Raven Stone","waves[27].title":"The Iron Wedge","waves[28].title":"Battle at Uzmen","waves[29].title":"Master of the Order","content.waveFacts[0]":"In summer 1240, a Swedish army landed on the Neva River. Alexander marched against it quickly, without waiting for all his regiments.","content.waveFacts[1]":"The Swedes had allies from Finnish tribes, while the Russian force included Novgorodians and soldiers from Ladoga.","content.waveFacts[2]":"The Battle of the Neva lasted until evening. According to tradition, Alexander himself wounded Birger in the face with a spear.","content.waveFacts[3]":"The Swedes withdrew to their ships, and the chronicle says only about twenty Russians were killed.","content.waveFacts[4]":"The victory on the Neva made Alexander famous, but he soon quarreled with the Novgorod boyars.","content.waveFacts[5]":"In fall 1240, the Order approached Izborsk, a strong fortress on the western border of Rus'.","content.waveFacts[6]":"Izborsk fell. The people of Pskov marched out to fight but were defeated and retreated to their city.","content.waveFacts[7]":"The Bishop of Dorpat sent soldiers, and together with the knights, they became a threat to all the lands around Pskov.","content.waveFacts[8]":"Traitors in Pskov opened the city gates to the Order, and the Order left its vogts there.","content.waveFacts[9]":"The knights built fortresses in conquered lands and collected tribute from the people. Their troops raided Novgorod villages.","content.waveFacts[10]":"The enemy came close to Novgorod. The chronicle says they raided settlements just thirty versts from the city.","content.waveFacts[11]":"The Order built a fortress at Koporye, while the Danes from northern Estonia were also dangerous neighbors.","content.waveFacts[12]":"The Novgorodians realized their mistake and sent messengers to bring Alexander back. He returned to the city.","content.waveFacts[13]":"Alexander captured Koporye with Novgorodians, soldiers from Ladoga, Izhorians, and Karelians, then destroyed the fortress.","content.waveFacts[14]":"The commander of Fellin led heavy cavalry. The knights were used to foot soldiers fleeing from their charge.","content.waveFacts[15]":"In winter 1242, Alexander approached Pskov with his brother Andrey, who had brought troops from Vladimir.","content.waveFacts[16]":"Pskov was a strong fortress, but the Order's garrison was small. The city was liberated, and two of the Order's vogts were captured.","content.waveFacts[17]":"The Order decided to take revenge and sent the bishopric's troops and knights from nearby castles into battle.","content.waveFacts[18]":"Bishop Hermann of Dorpat gathered his soldiers, and the Order began preparing a major campaign against Novgorod.","content.waveFacts[19]":"Alexander marched into the Order's lands and sent Domash Tverdislavich ahead with a scouting party.","content.waveFacts[20]":"Domash's unit encountered knights near Hamaste, and Domash himself was killed in battle.","content.waveFacts[21]":"The surviving warriors returned to Alexander and reported that the Order was following them with a large army.","content.waveFacts[22]":"Alexander decided not to risk a battle on the shore. He withdrew onto the ice of Lake Peipus, where the narrow Uzmen Strait connects the lakes.","content.waveFacts[23]":"The Russians took their positions on the ice near Raven Stone. The knights pursuing them were forced to fight.","content.waveFacts[24]":"On the morning of April 5, 1242, the knights advanced onto the ice in a wedge formation, which Russian chronicles called a 'pig.'","content.waveFacts[25]":"Russian archers faced the first attack. The German chronicle confirms that the Russians had many archers.","content.waveFacts[26]":"The knights' wedge crashed into the Russian formation but could not break through it.","content.waveFacts[27]":"Some say the ice cracked under the knights' weight, but the chronicle mentions only cracking spears and breaking weapons.","content.waveFacts[28]":"The Russians struck from the flanks and surrounded the wedge. The knights and their allies fled.","content.waveFacts[29]":"The victors chased the enemy for seven versts to the Subolichye shore, and the Order soon asked for peace.","content.quiz[0].q":"According to the chronicle, who was invited to rule Novgorod in 862 AD?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"Rurik","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"Yaroslav the Wise","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"Vladimir Monomakh","content.quiz[0].explain":"The chronicle says the Novgorodians invited the Varangian Rurik to rule, and the Rurikid dynasty began with him.","content.quiz[1].q":"What was the name of the Swedish leader defeated on the Neva River in 1240?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"Olaf","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"Harald","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"Birger","content.quiz[1].explain":"The Swedish jarl Birger later became a ruler of Sweden, and his army was defeated on the Neva.","content.quiz[2].q":"Which prince united Novgorod and Kyiv in 882 AD?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Igor","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Oleg","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Sviatoslav","content.quiz[2].explain":"According to the chronicle, Oleg captured Kyiv and united the northern and southern lands into one state.","content.quiz[3].q":"Why was Alexander nicknamed Nevsky?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"For his victory on the Neva River","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"For his victory on Lake Peipus","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"For building a city on the Neva River","content.quiz[3].explain":"The nickname refers to his victory over the Swedes on the Neva River in 1240.","content.quiz[4].q":"Who introduced fixed tribute amounts and collection centers after Igor was killed by the Drevlians?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"Sviatoslav","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"Olga","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"Vladimir","content.quiz[4].explain":"Princess Olga established fixed tribute amounts, called 'uroki,' and collection centers, called 'pogosts.'","content.quiz[5].q":"Which Russian towns did the Livonian Order capture in 1240?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"Kyiv and Chernigov","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"Vladimir and Suzdal","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Izborsk and Pskov","content.quiz[5].explain":"The Order captured Izborsk first, then Pskov, putting Novgorod in danger.","content.quiz[6].q":"In what year did Rus' adopt Christianity under Prince Vladimir?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"862 AD","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"988 AD","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"1054 AD","content.quiz[6].explain":"Prince Vladimir Sviatoslavich baptized Rus' in 988 AD, and Christianity became the state's main religion.","content.quiz[7].q":"What was the Livonian Order?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"An organization of German crusader knights in the Baltic region","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"A band of Russian epic heroes","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"An alliance of trading cities","content.quiz[7].explain":"The Order was a branch of the Teutonic Order and answered to it. Its knights sought to conquer the Baltic lands.","content.quiz[8].q":"Which state did Prince Sviatoslav defeat in the 960s?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"The Byzantine Empire","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"Volga Bulgaria","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"The Khazar Khaganate","content.quiz[8].explain":"Sviatoslav crushed the Khazar Khaganate and opened the Volga trade route to Russian merchants.","content.quiz[9].q":"Where did Alexander Nevsky defeat the knights in 1242?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"At the Kalka River","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"On Lake Peipus","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"At Kulikovo Field","content.quiz[9].explain":"The battle took place on the ice of Lake Peipus, which is why it is called the Battle on the Ice.","content.quiz[10].q":"What was the law code created under Yaroslav the Wise called?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"Russkaya Pravda","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"The Tale of Igor's Campaign","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"The Primary Chronicle","content.quiz[10].explain":"Russkaya Pravda was the first Russian law code, begun under Yaroslav the Wise.","content.quiz[11].q":"What did Russian chronicles call the knights' wedge formation?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Square formation","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Phalanx","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Pig","content.quiz[11].explain":"Russian chronicles called the knights' wedge a 'pig' because the formation was narrow and pointed at the front.","content.quiz[12].q":"Which prince wrote Instruction to His Children?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Yaroslav the Wise","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Vladimir Monomakh","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Oleg Sviatoslavich","content.quiz[12].explain":"Vladimir Monomakh wrote Instruction, advising his children to be fair and hardworking.","content.quiz[13].q":"What did Alexander do to the fortress at Koporye in 1241?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"Left a garrison there","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"Gave it to the Order","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"Destroyed it","content.quiz[13].explain":"Alexander captured Koporye and destroyed the Order's fortress so it could no longer threaten the Novgorodians.","content.quiz[14].q":"What began in Rus' after Mstislav the Great died in 1132?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"Political fragmentation","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"The adoption of Christianity","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"The invitation of the Varangians","content.quiz[14].explain":"After 1132, the state centered on Kyiv split into independent principalities, beginning a period of political fragmentation.","content.quiz[15].q":"Which ruler conquered much of Rus' in 1237–1240?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"Genghis Khan","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"Batu Khan","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"Mamai","content.quiz[15].explain":"Batu Khan, a grandson of Genghis Khan, devastated the Russian principalities in 1237–1240.","content.quiz[16].q":"Where did the Russian princes first face the Mongols in a major battle in 1223?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"At the Kalka River","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"At the Neva River","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"On Lake Peipus","content.quiz[16].explain":"The Battle of the Kalka River ended in defeat for the Russians and Cumans.","content.quiz[17].q":"What was the landmark on the ice near which the Russians stood called?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"Bald Mountain","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"Kulikovo Field","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"Raven Stone","content.quiz[17].explain":"Russian chronicles mention Raven Stone at Uzmen, but scholars are still searching for its exact location.","content.quiz[18].q":"What happened at Lyubech in 1097?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"The Baptism of Rus'","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"A council of princes","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"A battle against the Cumans","content.quiz[18].explain":"The princes agreed that each would rule his own inherited lands, but their conflicts did not end.","content.quiz[19].q":"What was the name of Alexander's brother who brought troops from Vladimir?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"Andrey Yaroslavich","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"Daniel of Galicia","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"Yaroslav the Wise","content.quiz[19].explain":"Andrey Yaroslavich helped his brother in the campaign to Pskov and at Lake Peipus with troops from Suzdal and Vladimir.","content.quiz[20].q":"According to the chronicle, who nailed his shield to the gates of Constantinople in 907?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"Igor","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"Rurik","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"Oleg","content.quiz[20].explain":"According to the chronicle, Oleg hung his shield on the gates of Constantinople and made a favorable peace treaty with Byzantium.","content.quiz[21].q":"What was a yarlyk?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"A tax on merchants","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"A khan's charter granting the right to rule","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"A prince's seal","content.quiz[21].explain":"A yarlyk was a charter from a Golden Horde khan that allowed a Russian prince to rule.","content.quiz[22].q":"What was the name of Batu Khan's capital on the Volga River?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Sarai","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"Kyiv","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"Karakorum","content.quiz[22].explain":"Sarai became the capital of the Golden Horde, and Russian princes traveled there to receive their yarlyks.","content.quiz[23].q":"Which city did Batu Khan capture in December 1240?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"Novgorod","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"Pskov","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"Kyiv","content.quiz[23].explain":"After a long siege, the Mongols captured Kyiv, and the city suffered terrible destruction.","content.quiz[24].q":"In what year did the Battle on the Ice take place?","content.quiz[24].explain":"The Battle on the Ice took place on April 5, 1242, while the Battle of the Neva was fought in 1240.","content.quiz[25].q":"Who was baptized in Constantinople and became the first Christian among the rulers of Rus'?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"Olga","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"Yaroslav the Wise","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Igor","content.quiz[25].explain":"Princess Olga was baptized in Constantinople around 957 AD, more than 30 years before the Baptism of Rus'.","content.quiz[26].q":"In which town did Alexander Nevsky die in 1263?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Novgorod","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Vladimir","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Gorodets","content.quiz[26].explain":"Alexander was returning from the Golden Horde when he died in Gorodets on the Volga River on November 14, 1263.","content.quiz[27].q":"Under which prince was Saint Sophia Cathedral built in Kyiv?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"Vladimir the Saint","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"Yaroslav the Wise","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Vladimir Monomakh","content.quiz[27].explain":"Yaroslav the Wise began building Saint Sophia Cathedral around 1037, and it became a landmark of the capital.","content.quiz[28].q":"What title did the Church give Alexander Nevsky?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"Holy Right-Believing Prince","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"Metropolitan","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"Khan","content.quiz[28].explain":"Alexander was officially recognized as a saint in the 16th century for defending Rus' and caring for its people.","content.quiz[29].q":"Alexander Nevsky's youngest son became the first prince of which city and principality?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"Tver","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"Vladimir","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"Moscow","content.quiz[29].explain":"Daniel Alexandrovich founded the Moscow dynasty, and Moscow later united the lands of Rus' around itself.","content.events[0].title":"The Swedes on the Neva","content.events[0].text":"The Swedish force led by Jarl Birger has camped near the mouth of the Izhora River. Alexander has not gathered all his troops yet. Should he wait or attack immediately?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Attack immediately","content.events[0].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Alexander launched a surprise attack with a small force and soldiers from Ladoga, catching the Swedes before they could form up.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Wait for reinforcements from Vladimir","content.events[0].options[1].result":"They did not wait. Reinforcements from Vladimir would not have arrived in time, and the Swedes might have advanced toward Ladoga or Novgorod.","content.events[1].title":"Should We Call Alexander Back?","content.events[1].text":"The Novgorod boyars quarreled with Alexander, and he left. The Order has already captured Pskov and is raiding Novgorod villages. Should they invite the prince back?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Yes, send messengers for him","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That is what happened. In 1241, the Novgorodians asked Alexander to return, and he took command of the army.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"No, manage without him","content.events[1].options[1].result":"Without Alexander, the Novgorodians would have struggled. The Order was already close to their city.","content.events[2].title":"What Should We Do with Koporye?","content.events[2].text":"The Order's fortress at Koporye has been captured. Should we leave a Russian garrison there or destroy it?","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Destroy the fortress","content.events[2].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Alexander destroyed Koporye and took the captured Germans to Novgorod.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Leave a garrison","content.events[2].options[1].result":"A small garrison would have been left right on the border, where the Order could easily recapture the fortress.","content.events[3].title":"Peace or Another Campaign?","content.events[3].text":"Pskov has been liberated, and two vogts have been captured. Should we make peace with the Order or march into its lands?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"March into the Order's lands","content.events[3].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Alexander entered the lands of the Estonians and sent out detachments, but they encountered knights near Hamaste.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Make peace now","content.events[3].options[1].result":"The Order was not ready to give up, and a peace treaty was signed only after the victory on the ice.","content.events[4].title":"Where Should We Fight?","content.events[4].text":"The Order is close behind us. Should we face it on the shore or move onto the ice near Raven Stone?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Fight on the ice near Raven Stone","content.events[4].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Alexander chose a position at Uzmen, where the knights' wedge had little room to maneuver.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Meet them on the shore","content.events[4].options[1].result":"The open shore would have given the knights plenty of room to build up speed, which Alexander wanted to avoid.","content.events[5].title":"Center or Wings?","content.events[5].text":"The knights are advancing in a wedge. Should we strengthen the center or the wings on both sides?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Strengthen the wings","content.events[5].options[0].result":"According to tradition, that is what happened. The Russians stopped the wedge in the center and attacked from the flanks, although scholars debate exactly how the troops were arranged.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Strengthen the center","content.events[5].options[1].result":"The knights' wedge could have broken through even a strong center, while a flank attack was Alexander's greatest advantage.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Novgorod Militia","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"The Novgorodians marched to war armed for battle: merchants, craftsmen, and people from nearby villages. They carried heavy spears, axes, and simple shields. They had few expensive weapons, but there were many militia fighters, and they knew their land well.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Foot Soldiers","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"Foot soldiers formed the backbone of a Russian regiment. They wore chain mail or padded armor and pointed helmets. Their almond-shaped shields protected them from shoulder to knee. The spear was their main weapon when fighting in close formation.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Mounted Retainers","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"A prince's personal troops were professional warriors who served him and followed him into battle. They were heavily armed with chain mail, helmets, spears, and swords. They were the strongest part of the armies of the Russian principalities.","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"The bow remained a favorite weapon of hunters and warriors. Arrows could strike infantry from a distance, but they were less effective against chain mail. The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle specifically mentions that the Russians had many archers.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Crossbowmen","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"In Rus', a crossbow was called a samostrel. It fired short, heavy arrows called bolts. Reloading took a long time, but a bolt could pierce armor. By the 13th century, Russian warriors were already familiar with this weapon.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Front Line","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"The front line was the leading part of a Russian regiment. Shield-bearers stood there to withstand the enemy's first attack. By protecting one another with their shields, they held back the knights while archers and spearmen attacked from the sides.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Knechts","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"Knechts were ordinary soldiers of the Order. They fought on foot, wore light armor, and used spears and axes. In battle, knechts followed the knights and finished off fallen enemies.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"The Order's Estonian Allies","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"The Russians called Estonians, Livonians, and other Baltic peoples 'Chud.' The Order conquered many of them and forced them to fight for it. These warriors moved easily across snow and ice but had poorer weapons than the knights. Some fought alongside the Russians instead.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Order Crossbowmen","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"The Order had both hired crossbowmen and crossbowmen of its own. Crossbows had a long range and were accurate, but they took a long time to reload, so the crossbowmen sheltered behind infantry shields.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Mounted Sergeants","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"Sergeants were members of the Order who came from less noble families. They rode horses, but their armor was lighter than the knights' armor. In battle, they protected the knights and chased fleeing enemies.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Squires","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"A squire served a knight by handing him weapons and caring for his horse. Squires could also fight. On the ice, they fought on foot and protected the knights from spearmen.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"The Bishop of Dorpat's Soldiers","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"The Bishopric of Dorpat was an independent territory with its own army. Its soldiers went on campaigns alongside the Order but answered to the bishop. The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle mentions them separately from the Order's knights.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Brother Knights","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"The Order's knights wore white cloaks with black crosses and charged in a heavy wedge formation. They wore chain mail and steel armor, and their horses were also protected. Some say the ice cracked beneath the knights, but that is a legend. The chronicle mentions only the cracking of spears.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Danish Knights","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"The Danes controlled Reval and northern Estonia and were neighbors of the Order. Danish knights wore heavy iron armor, just like the German knights. Whether they fought on the ice of Lake Peipus is uncertain because the sources say little about it.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Commander of Fellin","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"A commander was the leader of an Order castle. He governed the surrounding lands and led its knights into battle. Fellin was a castle in Estonia. The sources do not name its commander, so this character represents several possible commanders.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Bishop Hermann of Dorpat","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Hermann von Buxhoeveden had been Bishop of Dorpat since 1224. He ruled the city and surrounding lands, maintained an army, and took part in military campaigns. His troops fought against Alexander alongside the Order, but whether he personally fought on the ice is unknown.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Andreas von Velven","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Andreas von Velven was a knight of the Order in Livonia and is said to have later become its master. The Livonian Rhymed Chronicle connects his name with the war against Rus'. Scholars debate whether he commanded the Order's army on the ice.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Master of the Order","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"The leader of the Order in Livonia was called the master. The game deliberately leaves his name unspecified. Many scholars believe that Landmeister Dietrich von Grüningen may not have taken part in the battle, but nobody knows for certain. The final enemy therefore represents the Order's leadership rather than one specific person.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Alexander Nevsky","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Alexander Yaroslavich was born around 1220 and became Prince of Novgorod while still young. In 1240, he defeated the Swedes on the Neva River, and in 1242, he defeated the knights on the ice of Lake Peipus. Later, he traveled to the Golden Horde and worked to secure peace for Rus'. He died in 1263 and was recognized as a saint by the Church.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Birger","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Jarl Birger was a Swedish nobleman and a relative of the king. In summer 1240, his troops landed on the Neva River. According to tradition, Alexander wounded him in the face with a spear. Birger later became the ruler of Sweden.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Hermann of Dorpat","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Hermann von Buxhoeveden was the brother of Bishop Albert, the founder of Riga, and had been Bishop of Dorpat since 1224. He wanted to extend the Catholic Church's authority into Russian lands. His soldiers were among the forces that marched against Pskov and Novgorod.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Andrey Yaroslavich","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Andrey was Alexander's younger brother and a prince in Suzdal. He brought troops from the Vladimir-Suzdal lands to help Novgorod and took part in the liberation of Pskov and the Battle on the Ice. Later, he became Grand Prince of Vladimir.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Batu Khan","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Batu Khan, a grandson of Genghis Khan, led the Mongols through Rus' in 1237–1240, and many cities fell. He later founded the Golden Horde, with its capital at Sarai on the Volga River. Russian princes had to travel to him to receive a yarlyk, a charter granting the right to rule. Alexander Nevsky also visited him.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Battle of the Neva, 1240","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"The Swedish army of Jarl Birger landed near the mouth of the Izhora River, a tributary of the Neva. Alexander quickly attacked with his personal troops and soldiers from Ladoga. The Russians won, and the Swedes withdrew on their ships. Russian losses were small.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"Izborsk and Pskov, 1240–1241","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"In fall 1240, the Order captured Izborsk and then advanced toward Pskov. Traitors among the local nobles opened the city, and the Order installed its governors there. The Order built fortresses and raided lands near Novgorod.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Koporye, 1241","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"The Order built a fortress at Koporye to control the lands of the Vod people. Alexander returned to Novgorod, gathered an army, captured Koporye, and destroyed the fortress. According to the chronicle, he punished traitors among the Estonians and the Vod.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"The Liberation of Pskov, 1242","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"In March 1242, Alexander and Andrey approached Pskov. The Order's garrison was small, and they managed to liberate the city. Two vogts, the Order's governors, were sent to Novgorod as prisoners.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"Domash's Scouting Mission, 1242","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"Alexander marched into the Order's lands and sent a scouting party led by Domash Tverdislavich and Kerbet ahead. Near Hamaste, the scouts encountered knights and were defeated. Domash was killed. The survivors returned to Alexander, who withdrew onto the ice of Lake Peipus.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"Battle on the Ice, April 5, 1242","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"The Russians took their positions on the ice near Raven Stone at Uzmen, and the knights attacked in a wedge formation. Russian chronicles say the wedge broke through the regiment but was surrounded and defeated. The enemy was chased for seven versts. Sources give very different casualty figures: the Novgorod Chronicle reports 400 Germans killed, while the German chronicle reports twenty knights killed.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"The Prince's Retinue and the Militia","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"The Russian army consisted of the prince's personal troops, called the druzhina, and the militia. The druzhina were professional warriors, while the militia was recruited from townspeople and villagers. In Novgorod, the veche assembly decided whom to call to war. Together, the druzhina and militia formed regiments: the vanguard, the main regiment, and the right and left wings.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"The Novgorod Republic and the Veche","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"In Novgorod, the veche, an assembly of townspeople, held great power. It invited princes to rule and could also dismiss them. The prince commanded the army and acted as a judge, but the city was governed by officials called the posadnik and tysyatsky. That is why Alexander, even after defeating the Swedes, could quarrel with the boyars and leave.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"The Livonian Order","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"The Livonian Order was established in 1237 as a branch of the Teutonic Order. Its knights wore white cloaks with black crosses, lived in castles, and fought campaigns in the Baltic region. The Order conquered lands inhabited by non-Christian peoples and tried to bring Rus' under its control, but it was defeated in 1242.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Lake Peipus and the Ice","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"Lake Peipus lies between Novgorod and Livonia and is connected to Lake Pskov by the narrow Uzmen Strait. In early April, the ice can still support people. Scholars are still searching for the exact site of the battle. The story of knights falling through the ice is a later legend and does not appear in the chronicle.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"What Happened Next","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"After the victory, the Order made peace and gave up its claims to the lands of the Vod people, Luga, and Pskov. But Rus' had suffered Batu Khan's invasion in 1237–1240, and Alexander had to travel to the Golden Horde to receive a yarlyk granting him the right to rule. His descendants became princes of Moscow, and over time, Moscow united the Russian lands."},"kulikovo":{"defenders[3].name":"Archers","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Archers","title":"Kulikovo Field","year":"1380 AD","side":"Dmitry Donskoy and the Russian Princes","foe":"Mamai's Horde and Mercenaries","intro[0]":"On September 8, 1380, the Moscow army and Mamai's forces met at Kulikovo Field near the Don River.","intro[1]":"For the first time, Russian regiments marched into an open field to face the Horde.","intro[2]":"Build defenses near Dmitry's camp, hold back the enemies, and wait for the Ambush Regiment to strike.","victoryText":"Mamai was defeated and fled, and Dmitry earned the nickname Donskoy. But the victory did not immediately free the country: in 1382, Khan Tokhtamysh burned Moscow, tribute payments resumed, and Mongol rule ended only in 1480.","defeatText":"The Horde broke through your defenses—but in history, Dmitry held his ground, so try again.","theme.spawnSign":"From the Horde","fortress.name":"DMITRY'S CAMP","features[0].text":"Don River","features[1].text":"Nepryadva River","features[2].text":"Kulikovo Field","defenders[0].name":"Militia Spearmen","defenders[0].note":"Cheap and quick; heavy spears against cavalry","defenders[0].fact":"A rogatina was a sturdy spear with a broad blade, used by peasants even when hunting bears.","defenders[1].name":"Foot Soldiers","defenders[1].note":"Main fighting force: spear, chain mail, shield","defenders[1].fact":"Foot soldiers formed the main strength of the Russian army. They stood in tight formations with spears and almond-shaped shields.","defenders[2].name":"Mounted Retainers","defenders[2].note":"Heavy cavalry; powerful attacks against horsemen","defenders[2].fact":"A prince's retinue consisted of professional warriors who were always ready for battle. They delivered powerful blows during combat.","defenders[3].note":"Shoot far and frequently","defenders[3].fact":"The bow was the most common ranged weapon in Rus'. Townspeople and hunters alike knew how to use it.","defenders[4].name":"Crossbowmen","defenders[4].note":"Slow but accurate bolts pierce armor","defenders[4].fact":"In Rus', a crossbow was called a samostrel. Its bolts could pierce chain mail, but reloading took a long time.","defenders[5].name":"Guard Regiment","defenders[5].note":"Does not attack, but delays enemies for 1.5 seconds","defenders[5].fact":"The Guard Regiment was sent ahead to protect the army and delay the enemy. According to legend, it was commanded by Semyon Melik.","locked[0].name":"Early Cannons","locked[0].line":"The first cannons will appear in Moscow in two years, in 1382","locked[1].name":"Mobile Wooden Fortress","locked[1].line":"Mobile fortifications made of shields will not be invented until the 16th century","locked[2].name":"Streltsy","locked[2].line":"They will appear in 170 years","locked[3].name":"Armored Knight","locked[3].line":"Warriors in the Horde and Rus' still wear chain mail, not full plate armor","abilities[0].name":"Ambush Regiment","abilities[0].fact":"According to legend, the Ambush Regiment led by Bobrok and Vladimir of Serpukhov emerged from an oak grove and attacked the Horde from behind.","abilities[1].name":"Sergius of Radonezh Gives His Blessing","abilities[1].fact":"Before the battle, Dmitry visited Sergius of Radonezh. According to tradition, Sergius blessed him and sent two warrior monks with him.","enemies[0].name":"Brodniks","enemies[0].fact":"Brodniks were free people living along the steppe frontier. At different times, they served either Russian princes or the Horde.","enemies[1].name":"Alans and Kasogs","enemies[1].fact":"The Russians called the Alans 'Yases' and the ancestors of the Circassians 'Kasogs.' These mountain peoples of the North Caucasus served in the Horde's army.","enemies[2].name":"Circassians","enemies[2].fact":"In Rus', the mountain peoples of the North Caucasus were called Circassians. According to legends, some fought in Mamai's army.","enemies[3].name":"Horde Horse Archers","enemies[3].fact":"Horde horse archers fired while riding, showered enemies with arrows, and quickly escaped counterattacks.","enemies[4].name":"Horde Heavy Cavalry","enemies[4].fact":"Noble warriors of the Horde wore chain mail and lamellar armor. Such horsemen were difficult to stop with arrows.","enemies[5].name":"Genoese Mercenaries (Fryagi)","enemies[5].fact":"The Russians called Italians 'Fryagi.' According to The Tale of Mamai's Battle, Mamai had mercenary infantry from Italy, although historians debate this.","enemies[6].name":"Supply Camels","enemies[6].fact":"The Horde transported supplies on camels. Horses unfamiliar with their smell and appearance sometimes became frightened and shied away.","enemies[7].name":"Murzas","enemies[7].fact":"Murzas were nobles of the Horde. They led their units in battle, while their followers protected their leaders with shields.","bosses[0].name":"Chelubey (Temir-Murza)","bosses[0].fact":"According to legend, a giant warrior of the Horde named Chelubey, also called Temir-Murza, challenged a Russian warrior to a duel and was killed by Peresvet.","bosses[1].name":"Right-Wing Tumen Commander","bosses[1].fact":"A temnik commanded a tumen, a unit of ten thousand warriors. Historical sources have not preserved the name of the Horde commander who led the right wing.","bosses[2].name":"Genoese Commander","bosses[2].fact":"The name of the Fryagi leader is unknown, so the game identifies him by his rank. Such heavy infantry fighting in a foreign army was unusual in steppe warfare.","bosses[3].name":"Mamai","bosses[3].fact":"Mamai was the Golden Horde's beklyarbek, its chief military commander. He wanted to rule through a puppet khan, but he was not a khan himself.","chapters[0].title":"Battle of the Vozha River","chapters[0].year":"1378 AD","chapters[0].fact":"On August 11, 1378, Dmitry defeated the Horde army of Murza Begich at the Vozha River. It was the first major victory over the Horde in an open-field battle.","chapters[1].title":"Gathering at Kolomna","chapters[1].year":"August 1380","chapters[1].fact":"After learning of Mamai's campaign, Dmitry summoned regiments from the Russian lands to Moscow and Kolomna.","chapters[2].title":"Crossing the Don","chapters[2].year":"September 7, 1380","chapters[2].fact":"The Russian army crossed the Don and took its position at Kulikovo Field, leaving the river behind it.","chapters[3].title":"A Foggy Morning","chapters[3].year":"September 8, 1380","chapters[3].fact":"The morning of September 8 was foggy. When the fog lifted, the two armies stood facing each other.","chapters[4].title":"The Fierce Battle","chapters[4].year":"September 8, 1380","chapters[4].fact":"The battle raged for several hours, and Dmitry fought among the ordinary soldiers.","chapters[5].title":"The Ambush Regiment","chapters[5].year":"September 8, 1380","chapters[5].fact":"The Ambush Regiment led by Bobrok and Vladimir of Serpukhov charged out of an oak grove and sent the Horde fleeing.","waves[0].title":"Brodniks","waves[1].title":"Scouting at the Vozha River","waves[2].title":"Begich's Archers","waves[3].title":"Alans","waves[4].title":"Attack from Both Sides","waves[5].title":"Circassians","waves[6].title":"Army Review at Kolomna","waves[7].title":"Horde Supply Wagons","waves[8].title":"Heavy Cavalry","waves[9].title":"Camel Caravan","waves[10].title":"Fryagi","waves[11].title":"Horse Archers","waves[12].title":"Murzas","waves[13].title":"The Night Before Battle","waves[14].title":"Chelubey's Challenge","waves[15].title":"Fog over the Field","waves[16].title":"Russian Regiments in Formation","waves[17].title":"An Ordinary Soldier","waves[18].title":"Waves of Cavalry","waves[19].title":"The Horde's Right Wing","waves[20].title":"The Battle Begins","waves[21].title":"Fighting at the Banner","waves[22].title":"The Left Wing Holds","waves[23].title":"Fryagi in the Center","waves[24].title":"Leader of the Fryagi","waves[25].title":"Attack from the Oak Grove","waves[26].title":"The Horde Falters","waves[27].title":"Abandoned Supply Wagons","waves[28].title":"The Retreat","waves[29].title":"Mamai","content.waveFacts[0]":"Brodniks were free people living along the steppe frontier. At different times, they served either Russian princes or the Horde.","content.waveFacts[1]":"In August 1378, Murza Begich approached the Vozha River in the Ryazan lands, and Dmitry led his regiments out to meet him.","content.waveFacts[2]":"Horde horse archers showered the Russian riverbank with arrows, but Dmitry did not rush. He waited for the enemy to begin crossing.","content.waveFacts[3]":"The Russians called the Alans 'Yases.' They were mountain people from the North Caucasus who often served in the Horde's army.","content.waveFacts[4]":"When the Horde troops crossed the river, the Russians attacked from several directions and defeated Begich.","content.waveFacts[5]":"In Rus', the mountain peoples of the North Caucasus were called Circassians. According to legends, some fought in Mamai's army.","content.waveFacts[6]":"In August 1380, regiments from many Russian lands marched toward Moscow and Kolomna.","content.waveFacts[7]":"The Horde's supply wagons carried provisions on camels, and horses unfamiliar with them shied away in fear.","content.waveFacts[8]":"Noble cavalrymen of the Horde wore chain mail and lamellar armor. Arrows alone could not stop them.","content.waveFacts[9]":"At Kolomna, Dmitry divided his army into regiments, but historians still debate how many warriors he had.","content.waveFacts[10]":"On September 7, the Russian regiments began crossing the Don. According to The Tale of Mamai's Battle, Mamai's army included Italian mercenaries called Fryagi.","content.waveFacts[11]":"Horse archers were considered the best marksmen of the steppe. They fired while riding and immediately escaped pursuit.","content.waveFacts[12]":"Murzas were nobles of the Horde who led their units into battle and protected their leaders with shields.","content.waveFacts[13]":"By nightfall, the regiments had taken their positions at Kulikovo Field between the Don and Nepryadva rivers, while the Horde's camel caravans approached from the south.","content.waveFacts[14]":"According to legend, before the battle, a giant warrior named Chelubey rode out from the Horde's ranks, and the monk Peresvet came forward to fight him.","content.waveFacts[15]":"The morning of September 8 was foggy, and both armies formed their lines in a blanket of white mist.","content.waveFacts[16]":"The Russian regiments took their positions: the Vanguard Regiment in front, the Main Regiment behind it, and the Right-Wing and Left-Wing Regiments on either side.","content.waveFacts[17]":"According to tradition, Dmitry exchanged armor with the boyar Mikhail Brenk and joined the ranks of ordinary soldiers.","content.waveFacts[18]":"The Horde's cavalry repeatedly charged into the Russian formation, but the narrow field between the rivers gave them little room.","content.waveFacts[19]":"Mamai commanded from a distance and entrusted his army's wings to temniks, commanders of tumens containing ten thousand warriors.","content.waveFacts[20]":"The battle at Kulikovo Field lasted several hours, with enormous losses on both sides.","content.waveFacts[21]":"Mikhail Brenk fought beneath the prince's banner wearing Dmitry's armor, and he was killed there.","content.waveFacts[22]":"According to accounts, the Horde pushed back the Russian Left-Wing Regiment, but the decisive blow had not yet been struck.","content.waveFacts[23]":"Vladimir of Serpukhov and Dmitry Bobrok waited for the signal in an oak grove. According to tradition, Bobrok held back the impatient prince.","content.waveFacts[24]":"The name of the Fryagi leader is unknown, so the game identifies him by his rank: 'Commander.'","content.waveFacts[25]":"The Ambush Regiment emerged from the oak grove and attacked the Horde just as its warriors believed victory was near.","content.waveFacts[26]":"Seeing fresh Russian regiments, the Horde troops faltered and began retreating toward the river.","content.waveFacts[27]":"The Russians captured the abandoned Horde supply wagons and all their camels.","content.waveFacts[28]":"The murzas tried to stop the fleeing warriors, but the Horde army was already falling apart.","content.waveFacts[29]":"Mamai watched the battle from a distance. When he saw the defeat, he fled into the steppe and later reached Crimea.","content.quiz[0].q":"On which river did Dmitry defeat the Horde commander Begich in 1378?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"The Vozha River","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"The Kalka River","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"The Ugra River","content.quiz[0].explain":"The Battle of the Vozha River was the first major open-field victory over the Horde. The Russians were defeated at the Kalka River in 1223, while the Great Stand on the Ugra River took place in 1480.","content.quiz[1].q":"Who were the baskaks?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"Russian boyars serving a prince","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"Horde officials who supervised tribute collection","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"Merchants from Novgorod","content.quiz[1].explain":"Baskaks represented the khan in the Russian lands and supervised the collection of tribute.","content.quiz[2].q":"What was the name of a khan's charter granting a prince the right to rule?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Chronicle","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Birch-bark letter","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Yarlyk","content.quiz[2].explain":"A yarlyk gave a prince the right to rule as grand prince, so princes traveled to the Horde to receive one.","content.quiz[3].q":"Who devastated the Russian lands in 1237–1240?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"Batu Khan","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"Khan Mamai","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"Khan Tokhtamysh","content.quiz[3].explain":"Batu Khan, a grandson of Genghis Khan, conquered Rus' in 1237–1240.","content.quiz[4].q":"Which of these cities did Batu Khan NOT capture?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"Ryazan","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"Novgorod","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"Vladimir","content.quiz[4].explain":"Ryazan and Vladimir were captured in 1237–1238. Batu Khan turned back about one hundred versts before reaching Novgorod.","content.quiz[5].q":"Which Moscow prince was nicknamed Kalita ('Moneybag')?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"Daniel Alexandrovich","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"Yuri Dolgoruky","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Ivan Kalita","content.quiz[5].explain":"Ivan Kalita ruled Moscow from 1325 to 1340 and was skilled at collecting money, including tribute for the Horde.","content.quiz[6].q":"Who were called murzas in Rus'?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"Noblemen of the Horde","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"Ordinary shepherds","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"Russian boyars","content.quiz[6].explain":"Murza was a title for a nobleman in the Horde.","content.quiz[7].q":"Which metropolitan made Moscow his residence in 1325?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Photius","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Peter","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Hilarion","content.quiz[7].explain":"Metropolitan Peter settled in Moscow, making it a religious center of Rus'. Photius and Hilarion lived in different centuries.","content.quiz[8].q":"What happened in Tver in 1327?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"The city was founded","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"Batu Khan captured it","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"An uprising against a Horde envoy","content.quiz[8].explain":"The people of Tver rebelled against the envoy Shevkal. Moscow helped the Horde crush the uprising, weakening Tver.","content.quiz[9].q":"Who was the first prince of Moscow and a son of Alexander Nevsky?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"Daniel Alexandrovich","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"Ivan Kalita","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"Dmitry Donskoy","content.quiz[9].explain":"Daniel began ruling Moscow in 1276 and expanded its territory.","content.quiz[10].q":"What was built in Moscow in 1367 under Dmitry?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"The brick walls of today's Kremlin","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"The White-Stone Kremlin","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"The Kremlin's Dormition Cathedral","content.quiz[10].explain":"The wooden walls were replaced with white stone, and Moscow became known as the White-Stone City.","content.quiz[11].q":"Which abbot of the Trinity Monastery blessed Dmitry before the battle?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Seraphim of Sarov","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Andrei Rublev","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Sergius of Radonezh","content.quiz[11].explain":"According to tradition, Sergius of Radonezh blessed the prince and sent two warrior monks with him.","content.quiz[12].q":"What was the name of the Horde commander who marched against Rus' in 1380?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Mamai","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Tokhtamysh","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Edigu","content.quiz[12].explain":"Mamai was the Golden Horde's beklyarbek. Tokhtamysh defeated him later, while Edigu lived in a later period.","content.quiz[13].q":"Who was Jogaila?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"A prince of Ryazan","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"The Grand Duke of Lithuania","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"A khan of the Horde","content.quiz[13].explain":"Jogaila hurried to help Mamai, but when he learned of the defeat, he turned back.","content.quiz[14].q":"According to legend, whom did Peresvet fight before the battle?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"Mamai","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"Begich","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"Chelubey","content.quiz[14].explain":"According to legend, Chelubey (Temir-Murza) and Peresvet struck each other down. Begich had already died at the Vozha River.","content.quiz[15].q":"Between which rivers is Kulikovo Field located?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"The Don and Nepryadva","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"The Volga and Oka","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"The Neva and Ladoga","content.quiz[15].explain":"Kulikovo Field lies near the place where the Nepryadva River flows into the Don.","content.quiz[16].q":"Which regiment, according to tradition, waited for its moment in an oak grove?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"The Vanguard Regiment","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"The Ambush Regiment","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"The Guard Regiment","content.quiz[16].explain":"The Ambush Regiment hid in the forest and attacked the enemy at the decisive moment.","content.quiz[17].q":"Who commanded the Ambush Regiment alongside Vladimir of Serpukhov?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"Daniil Kholmsky","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"Alexander Nevsky","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"Dmitry Bobrok-Volynsky","content.quiz[17].explain":"Bobrok-Volynsky was an experienced commander and Dmitry's brother-in-law. Kholmsky lived later, and Nevsky lived earlier.","content.quiz[18].q":"Why was Dmitry later nicknamed Donskoy?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"For his victory near the Don River","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"Because he was born on the Don River","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"For building a fortress called Don","content.quiz[18].explain":"The nickname 'Donskoy' became associated with Dmitry after his victory at Kulikovo Field near the Don River.","content.quiz[19].q":"What were Horde horsemen famous for in battle?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"Fighting in a deep phalanx","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"Shooting arrows and pretending to retreat","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"Fighting only on foot","content.quiz[19].explain":"Steppe archers often lured enemies into ambushes by pretending to retreat.","content.quiz[20].q":"Who captured and burned Moscow in 1382?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"Mamai","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"Begich","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"Tokhtamysh","content.quiz[20].explain":"Khan Tokhtamysh entered Moscow through trickery and burned it. Rus' had to pay tribute again.","content.quiz[21].q":"What happened to Mamai after his defeat?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"He fled to Crimea and died in Caffa","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"He became a monk","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"He was captured and taken to Moscow","content.quiz[21].explain":"Tokhtamysh defeated Mamai at the Kalka River in 1381. Mamai fled to Crimea, where he was killed.","content.quiz[22].q":"What helped Moscow become more powerful than other cities?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Gold mines","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"A favorable location on trade routes and the metropolitan's support","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"A powerful navy","content.quiz[22].explain":"Moscow stood on trade routes and became a religious center. Its princes were also skilled at negotiating with the Horde.","content.quiz[23].q":"What was an elected official in Novgorod called?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"Baskak","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"Temnik","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"Posadnik","content.quiz[23].explain":"The posadnik was elected by the veche assembly and governed Novgorod alongside the prince.","content.quiz[24].q":"Which principality competed with Moscow for power over Rus' in the 14th century?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Tver","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"Bulgaria","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Byzantium","content.quiz[24].explain":"Tver competed with Moscow for the title of grand prince for many years but eventually lost.","content.quiz[25].q":"In what year did Rus' finally throw off Horde rule?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"1380","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"1480","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"1240","content.quiz[25].explain":"In 1480, Khan Akhmat retreated from the Ugra River, ending Rus' dependence on the Horde.","content.quiz[26].q":"Who was Grand Prince of Moscow during the Great Stand on the Ugra River?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Ivan Kalita","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Dmitry Donskoy","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Ivan III","content.quiz[26].explain":"Ivan III did not engage Akhmat in battle at the river, and the khan withdrew.","content.quiz[27].q":"What was the tribute that Rus' paid to the Horde called?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"Vykhod","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"Obrok","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Tithe","content.quiz[27].explain":"Vykhod was the tribute paid to the Horde, while the tithe went to the Church.","content.quiz[28].q":"What was the nickname of Dmitry's cousin, the hero of the Ambush Regiment?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"Kalita","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"The Brave","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"The Wise","content.quiz[28].explain":"Prince Vladimir Andreyevich of Serpukhov was known as Vladimir the Brave.","content.quiz[29].q":"Why didn't the victory at Kulikovo Field immediately free Rus'?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"Rus' asked to pay tribute again","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"Dmitry gave up his throne","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"Tokhtamysh burned Moscow in 1382 and restored tribute payments","content.quiz[29].explain":"The Horde was still powerful. Tokhtamysh captured Moscow in 1382, and Horde rule continued until 1480.","content.events[0].title":"News of Mamai and Jogaila","content.events[0].text":"The Battle of the Vozha River is behind us, but the Horde has not given up. Two years later, in summer 1380, news arrives: Mamai is marching against Rus', and the Lithuanian prince Jogaila and Prince Oleg of Ryazan are hurrying to help him. Should we wait for all our regiments to arrive or strike Mamai before his allies join him?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Wait until all the regiments have gathered","content.events[0].options[0].result":"While Dmitry was gathering his army, Mamai, Jogaila, and Oleg could have joined forces, making the enemy much stronger.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"March against Mamai before his allies arrive","content.events[0].options[1].result":"That is what happened. Dmitry hurried to meet Mamai. When Jogaila learned of the battle, he turned back, only about a day's march away. Oleg of Ryazan did not send his regiments into battle.","content.events[1].title":"Should We Cross the Don?","content.events[1].text":"The Russian regiments have reached the Don River. The commanders disagree: should they cross to the bank where Mamai is waiting or stay on their own side? Crossing would leave the river behind them, with no easy way to retreat.","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Wait on our side of the river","content.events[1].options[0].result":"Mamai could have waited for Jogaila, forcing the Russians to fight two armies at once.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"Cross the river and take positions at Kulikovo Field","content.events[1].options[1].result":"Dmitry led his army across the Don. According to tradition, the bridges were then dismantled so nobody could flee. The river protected their rear, while the narrow battlefield limited the Horde's room to maneuver.","content.events[2].title":"The Warrior's Challenge","content.events[2].text":"At dawn, according to legend, an enormous Horde warrior named Chelubey rides out and challenges any Russian warrior to a duel. Peresvet, a monk who was once a soldier, steps forward. Should we accept the challenge or refuse?","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Accept the challenge: Peresvet will fight","content.events[2].options[0].result":"According to legend, Peresvet and Chelubey struck each other with their spears, and both fell dead. Some say Peresvet remained in his saddle. This encouraged the Russians.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Refuse and wait for the attack","content.events[2].options[1].result":"According to the customs of the time, refusing a duel was considered a sign of weakness. The legend says the challenge was accepted.","content.events[3].title":"Which Armor Should Dmitry Wear?","content.events[3].text":"Before the battle, Dmitry must decide where to stand. If the prince wears his shining armor beneath the banner, the Horde will focus its attack on him. What should he do?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Wear the prince's armor and stand beneath the banner","content.events[3].options[0].result":"The enemy could recognize and kill the prince from a distance. In those days, the death of a leader often decided the outcome of a battle.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Exchange armor with a boyar and fight as an ordinary soldier","content.events[3].options[1].result":"According to tradition, Dmitry gave his armor and banner to the boyar Mikhail Brenk and fought among the ordinary soldiers. Brenk was killed, while the wounded prince was found after the battle beneath a cut-down birch tree.","content.events[4].title":"When Should the Ambush Regiment Attack?","content.events[4].text":"Hundreds of Russian soldiers are falling. Vladimir of Serpukhov wants to attack now, but Commander Bobrok advises waiting until the wind changes and the enemy grows tired. When should we send in the Ambush Regiment?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Attack now without wasting time","content.events[4].options[0].result":"An early attack could have wasted the Ambush Regiment's strength while the Horde warriors were still fresh.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Wait for the right moment, as Bobrok advises","content.events[4].options[1].result":"According to legend, Bobrok waited until the Horde warriors were deeply engaged in battle. Then he sent the Ambush Regiment forward at just the right moment. Its attack on the enemy's flank and rear decided the battle.","content.events[5].title":"Should We Pursue Mamai?","content.events[5].text":"The Horde warriors are fleeing. Mamai has escaped into the steppe. Should we chase the fugitives and capture their supply wagons, or stay on the battlefield and avoid taking risks?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Stay on the battlefield and pursue nobody","content.events[5].options[0].result":"The Horde might have recovered. Instead, the Russians pursued it, captured the supply wagons, and then spent several days burying their fallen warriors.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Pursue the enemy and capture the supply wagons","content.events[5].options[1].result":"The Russians chased the fleeing enemy as far as the Krasivaya Mecha River and captured the supply wagons. Mamai escaped into the steppe and later reached Crimea, where he was killed.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Militia Spearmen","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"The militia was recruited from townspeople and peasants whenever a prince needed a large army. A rogatina was a sturdy spear with a broad blade, used for hunting bears and stopping charging horsemen. Militia fighters had little armor, but there were many of them. Dmitry gathered warriors from across the Russian lands.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Foot Soldiers","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"Foot soldiers formed the main strength of the Russian army in the 14th century. They wore chain mail and helmets and carried spears and almond-shaped shields. In battle, they stood shoulder to shoulder in a tight formation. This formation could withstand a cavalry charge.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Mounted Retainers","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"Retainers served the prince permanently and were his best warriors. They rode strong horses, wore chain mail, and carried swords and spears. In battle, they delivered powerful attacks and pursued fleeing enemies. Moscow's mounted retainers formed the core of Dmitry's army.","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"The bow was a simple and common weapon used by both hunters and townspeople. Archers fired quickly from a distance, disrupting enemy formations. Arrows were often ineffective against heavy armor, but they worked well against unarmored cavalry. At Kulikovo Field, archers opened the battle by exchanging volleys.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Crossbowmen","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"In Rus', a crossbow was called a samostrel, and chronicles mention it as early as the 12th century. A crossbow bolt could pierce chain mail, but reloading took a long time. Crossbowmen therefore fired slowly but accurately, aiming at the most dangerous enemies. They were more effective against armor than ordinary archers.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Guard Regiment","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"The Guard Regiment was sent ahead to protect the army and scout the enemy. According to tradition, Semyon Melik commanded it at Kulikovo Field, and it was the first to face the Horde's attack. This regiment delayed the enemy, giving the main forces time to prepare. In the game, it deals no damage but stops enemies from advancing.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Brodniks","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"Brodniks were free people who lived along the steppe frontier near the Don River and the Sea of Azov. They did not answer to princes and served either the Russians or the Horde at different times. They fought with light weapons, axes, and shields. Historians debate whether they fought in Mamai's army, but in the game, the Brodniks are a large group of lightly armed fighters.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Alans and Kasogs","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"The Russians called the Alans 'Yases.' They were descendants of ancient nomads from the North Caucasus. The Kasogs were ancestors of the Circassians. Many mountain warriors served in the Horde's army as infantry and cavalry. They wore chain mail and threw javelins.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Circassians","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"The Russians called the mountain peoples of the North Caucasus Circassians. According to legends, some fought in Mamai's army. They wore dark clothing and were experienced fighters with sabers. Mountain warriors moved quickly along narrow trails and knew how to set ambushes. Their exact number and role in Mamai's army are unknown.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Horde Horse Archers","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"Horse archers formed the backbone of the Horde's army. They fired arrows while riding, showered enemies with arrows, and retreated. If the enemy chased them, they lured the pursuers into an ambush. Children in the steppe learned to ride horses and shoot bows from an early age. Fast archers and spearmen were effective against them.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Horde Heavy Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"Noble and wealthy warriors of the Horde wore chain mail and lamellar armor made of small plates. These horsemen were dangerous in close combat, breaking through enemy formations with sabers and spears. Arrows from light bows were rarely effective against them. Crossbows and spears were needed to stop them.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"Genoese Mercenaries (Fryagi)","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"In Rus', Italians were called 'Fryagi.' The Genoese controlled trading cities in Crimea, including Caffa. The Tale of Mamai's Battle mentions mercenary infantry from Italy in Mamai's army, but historians debate whether they were really there. If they fought, they used long spears. In the game, they are heavy infantry.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Supply Camels","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"The Horde used camels to carry supplies and tents, especially on long campaigns. Horses unfamiliar with these animals were frightened by their appearance and smell, sometimes causing cavalry formations to break apart. Camels are slow but have great endurance. In the game, they are large, strong animals traveling with the supply wagons.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Murzas","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"A murza was a nobleman of the Horde who commanded his own warriors. Murzas led troops into battle and were wealthier than ordinary soldiers. They had better horses, armor, and sabers. Their bodyguards protected them with shields. In the game, murzas use their shields to protect nearby allies.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Chelubey (Temir-Murza)","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"The Tale of Mamai's Battle tells how a giant warrior named Chelubey, also called Temir-Murza, rode out from the Horde's ranks before the battle. A monk named Peresvet came forward to fight him. According to the tale, they struck each other with their spears, and both fell. This is a legend, not an exact historical record, so historians treat its details with caution.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Right-Wing Tumen Commander","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"A temnik commanded a tumen, a unit of ten thousand warriors. At Kulikovo Field, as in other Horde battles, each wing of the army had its own commander. Historical sources have not preserved this commander's name, so he is identified by his rank. He encouraged his soldiers and urged them into battle.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Genoese Commander","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Accounts of the Battle of Kulikovo do not name the leader of the Fryagi. In the game, he is simply called the Commander, showing that the Fryagi fought under a single leader. If such a unit existed, it was heavy infantry armed with long spears. Historians still debate whether these soldiers fought at Kulikovo Field.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Mamai","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"Mamai was the Golden Horde's beklyarbek, its chief military commander. He wanted to rule through a khan descended from Genghis Khan. In 1378, his commander Begich was defeated at the Vozha River. In 1380, Mamai personally led an army against Rus'. After losing at Kulikovo Field, he retreated into the steppe. In 1381, Khan Tokhtamysh defeated him, and Mamai fled to Crimea, where he was killed.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Dmitry Donskoy","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Dmitry Ivanovich was born in 1350 and became Prince of Moscow at the age of nine. He built the White-Stone Kremlin in 1367 and became the first Russian prince to defeat a Horde army in a major open-field battle. He earned the nickname 'Donskoy' for his victory near the Don River. He died in 1389 at the age of thirty-nine.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Who Was Mamai?","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Mamai was not a khan. He ruled through a puppet khan descended from Genghis Khan. He controlled the western part of the Golden Horde and wanted to restore its former power and collect tribute from Rus'. His campaign in 1380 was his greatest gamble. After losing, he was left without an army or political power.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Sergius of Radonezh","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Sergius founded the Trinity Monastery and was one of the most respected people in Rus'. According to tradition, he blessed Dmitry before the Battle of Kulikovo and sent two warrior monks, Peresvet and Oslyabya, with him. He taught princes to live in peace and unite against their enemies. The Orthodox Church honors him as a saint.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Dmitry Bobrok-Volynsky","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Bobrok-Volynsky was an experienced military commander from Volhynia and Dmitry Donskoy's brother-in-law. According to tradition, he commanded the Ambush Regiment alongside Vladimir of Serpukhov and advised against attacking too early. He knew how to read the battlefield and choose the right moment for a decisive strike.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Vladimir the Brave","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Vladimir Andreyevich, Prince of Serpukhov, was Dmitry's cousin and loyal ally. At Kulikovo Field, he led the Ambush Regiment alongside Bobrok. He earned the nickname 'the Brave' for his courage. Later, he helped defend Moscow against its enemies.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Battle of the Vozha River, 1378","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"Murza Begich marched toward Moscow, and Dmitry met him at the Vozha River in the Ryazan lands. The Russians waited until the Horde warriors began crossing the river, then attacked from both sides. Begich was killed, and the Horde fled. This was the first major open-field victory over the Horde.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"Gathering at Kolomna, 1380","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"In August 1380, Dmitry summoned regiments from the Russian lands and gathered them in Moscow and Kolomna. There, he inspected the army and divided it into regiments. Moscow boyars and allied princes helped him. Historians still debate how many warriors took part.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Crossing the Don","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"On September 7, 1380, the Russian regiments reached the Don River. The commanders decided to cross because Dmitry wanted to face Mamai before Jogaila arrived to help him. According to tradition, the bridges were dismantled afterward so nobody would think of fleeing. At dawn, the regiments took their positions at Kulikovo Field.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"Morning of September 8","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"The morning was foggy. When the fog lifted, the two armies stood facing each other. According to legend, Peresvet and Chelubey fought a duel first, and both were killed. Then the main battle began.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"The Battle at Kulikovo Field","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"According to tradition, Dmitry fought as an ordinary soldier while the boyar Mikhail Brenk wore his armor. The Horde pushed back the Russians, especially the Left-Wing Regiment. The fierce battle continued for several hours. The decisive attack had not yet begun.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"The Ambush Regiment Strikes","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"The Ambush Regiment led by Bobrok and Vladimir of Serpukhov emerged from the oak grove and attacked the Horde from behind and from the flank. The Horde warriors faltered and fled, and the Russians chased them as far as the Krasivaya Mecha River. Mamai escaped into the steppe. The victory was a turning point, but it did not end Horde rule.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"The Russian Army in the 14th Century","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"The Russian army was divided into regiments: the Vanguard Regiment, Main Regiment, Right-Wing Regiment, and Left-Wing Regiment. Sometimes there were also Guard and Ambush Regiments. Most warriors were foot soldiers or members of a prince's mounted retinue. Princes brought their own regiments, and Dmitry managed to unite many of them.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"The Horde and the Yarlyk","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"Russian princes depended on the Golden Horde. They traveled to the khan to receive a yarlyk, a charter granting the right to rule, and paid tribute called 'vykhod.' Baskaks supervised tribute collection. Princes often competed with one another at the Horde's court. Powerful princes, such as Ivan Kalita, knew how to negotiate with the khans.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"Moscow under Ivan Kalita","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"Ivan Kalita ruled from 1325 to 1340. He received the right to collect tribute for the Horde, which made him wealthy. During his reign, Metropolitan Peter moved to Moscow, making it a center of the Church. Moscow began bringing the surrounding lands under its control.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Kulikovo Field","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"Kulikovo Field lies in the southern part of today's Tula Oblast, where the Nepryadva River flows into the Don. The battlefield is surrounded by rivers and ravines, leaving little room for a large cavalry force. Today, it is a museum and nature reserve. A monument to the warriors who fell in battle stands at its center.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"What Happened Next","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"In 1381, Khan Tokhtamysh defeated Mamai. In 1382, he captured Moscow through trickery and burned it. Rus' had to pay tribute again. Horde rule finally ended in 1480, when Ivan III faced Khan Akhmat at the Ugra River. After a long standoff, Akhmat withdrew."},"poitiers":{"defenders[3].name":"Archers","locked[1].name":"Crossbow","locked[2].name":"Cannon","waves[3].title":"Archers","waves[5].title":"The Pyrenees","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Rome","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"Shield","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"War Elephants","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Legionaries","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"War Elephants","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Archers","title":"Poitiers","year":"732 AD","side":"Charles Martel's Franks","foe":"Abd al-Rahman’s Arabs and Berbers","intro[0]":"An Arab army is marching north to plunder the wealthy Abbey of Saint Martin in Tours.","intro[1]":"You are Charles Martel. Stop the enemy near Poitiers: the Frankish infantry must stand firm like a wall.","victoryText":"In history, the Franks won: the Arabs retreated beyond the Pyrenees, and Charles Martel became a hero of Christian Europe. His son Pepin later became king, and his grandson Charlemagne became emperor.","defeatText":"The enemy reached Tours and plundered the Abbey of Saint Martin. Charles failed to hold the line.","theme.spawnSign":"From Spain","fortress.name":"TOURS","features[0].text":"Clain River","features[1].text":"Toward Poitiers","defenders[0].name":"Frankish Militia","defenders[0].note":"Cheap and fast; effective against infantry","defenders[0].fact":"Every free Frank was required to come to war with a spear and shield, and these militiamen made up most of Charles's army.","defenders[1].name":"Shield Wall Infantry","defenders[1].note":"Main fighter: round shield and spear; strong against cavalry","defenders[1].fact":"Frankish infantry stood in a tight formation, shield to shield, holding their spears toward charging horsemen.","defenders[2].name":"Francisca Throwers","defenders[2].note":"Throw axes quickly, breaking shields and armor","defenders[2].fact":"A francisca was a short throwing axe. The Franks threw these axes before close combat to smash enemy shields.","defenders[3].note":"Attack from a distance; effective against camels","defenders[3].fact":"Many Frankish hunters used bows, but at the Battle of Poitiers, infantry was still the main fighting force.","defenders[4].name":"Charles's Scara","defenders[4].note":"Heavy horsemen; deal twice as much damage to cavalry","defenders[4].fact":"A scara was Charles's mounted retinue of noble warriors. However, most historians believe the Franks fought mainly on foot at Poitiers.","defenders[5].name":"Shield Wall","defenders[5].note":"Does not attack, but holds enemies back for 1.5 seconds","defenders[5].fact":"The warriors stood shoulder to shoulder and blocked the road. A chronicler later compared them to an unmoving wall.","locked[0].name":"Knight in Plate Armor","locked[0].line":"Plate armor will not appear for another 600 years","locked[1].line":"Crossbows will not appear in European armies for another 300 years","locked[2].line":"Gunpowder will not reach Europe for at least another 500 years","locked[3].name":"Trebuchet","locked[3].line":"Siege engines were not brought onto the battlefield: Charles fought in open terrain","abilities[0].name":"Wall of Ice","abilities[0].fact":"The Mozarabic Chronicle of 754 described the northerners as standing motionless 'like a wall' and 'like ice.'","abilities[1].name":"The Hammer","abilities[1].fact":"Charles was nicknamed Martel, meaning 'the Hammer.' People said he crushed his enemies like a hammer.","enemies[0].name":"Arab Infantry","enemies[0].fact":"Foot soldiers armed with spears and swords formed the backbone of the army. Many were Berbers who had recently converted to Islam.","enemies[1].name":"Berber Cavalry","enemies[1].fact":"The Berbers of North Africa were light horsemen who threw javelins and quickly moved out of danger.","enemies[2].name":"Slingers","enemies[2].fact":"Slings were used to throw stones and lead pellets. These projectiles could injure even a warrior wearing a helmet.","enemies[3].name":"Arab Archers","enemies[3].fact":"Archers fired at enemy formations from a distance to disrupt them before the cavalry attacked.","enemies[4].name":"Syrian Spearmen","enemies[4].fact":"Spearmen wearing chain mail and carrying round shields moved slowly but could withstand many blows.","enemies[5].name":"Arab Heavy Cavalry","enemies[5].fact":"Horsemen wearing chain mail and carrying spears and swords were the Arabs' main striking force.","enemies[6].name":"Pack Camels","enemies[6].fact":"Camels carried tents, supplies, and loot from Aquitaine. This was the army's 'loot convoy.'","enemies[7].name":"Ghulams","enemies[7].fact":"Ghulams were elite warriors from the commander's personal guard, equipped with strong armor.","bosses[0].name":"Vanguard Emir","bosses[0].fact":"An emir was a military commander. The vanguard marched ahead of the army and was the first to meet the enemy.","bosses[1].name":"Supply Convoy Commander","bosses[1].fact":"He was responsible for the pack animals and the loot, protecting the stolen goods and refusing to abandon them even in danger.","bosses[2].name":"Army Qadi","bosses[2].fact":"A qadi was a judge and expert in religious law. On campaign, he made sure the warriors followed the rules of their faith and encouraged them.","bosses[3].name":"Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi","bosses[3].fact":"The governor of Al-Andalus led his army across the Pyrenees and was killed in battle near Poitiers.","chapters[0].title":"The Arabs Conquer Spain","chapters[0].year":"711 AD","chapters[0].fact":"In 711, Arabs and Berbers crossed into Spain, defeated the Visigoths, and conquered almost the entire peninsula within a few years.","chapters[1].title":"Raid on Aquitaine","chapters[1].year":"732 AD","chapters[1].fact":"In 732, Governor Abd al-Rahman crossed the Pyrenees, defeated Duke Odo near the Garonne River, and plundered Bordeaux.","chapters[2].title":"Odo Asks Charles for Help","chapters[2].year":"732 AD","chapters[2].fact":"After his defeat, Odo fled north to Charles Martel, who had previously been his enemy. Charles gathered an army.","chapters[3].title":"Seven Days near Poitiers","chapters[3].year":"732 AD","chapters[3].fact":"The Franks took positions on high ground between Poitiers and Tours, and the two armies faced each other for about a week.","chapters[4].title":"The Battle: 'Wall of Ice'","chapters[4].year":"732 AD","chapters[4].fact":"Arab cavalry attacked the Franks again and again, but a chronicler wrote that they stood like a wall of ice.","chapters[5].title":"The Arab Retreat","chapters[5].year":"732 AD","chapters[5].fact":"Abd al-Rahman was killed, and the Arabs withdrew during the night, abandoning their camp and loot. Charles did not pursue them.","waves[0].title":"First Infantry","waves[1].title":"Slingers","waves[2].title":"Berber Cavalry","waves[4].title":"The Army Grows","waves[6].title":"Spearmen","waves[7].title":"Supply Convoy","waves[8].title":"Battle at the Garonne","waves[9].title":"Bordeaux in Flames","waves[10].title":"Odo the Fugitive","waves[11].title":"Charles's March","waves[12].title":"Loot from Aquitaine","waves[13].title":"Elite Warriors","waves[14].title":"Vanguard Emir","waves[15].title":"Wooded Hill","waves[16].title":"The First Week","waves[17].title":"Skirmishes","waves[18].title":"The Seventh Day","waves[19].title":"Supply Convoy Commander","waves[20].title":"Cavalry Attack","waves[21].title":"Wall of Ice","waves[22].title":"Wave after Wave","waves[23].title":"The Line Holds","waves[24].title":"Army Qadi","waves[25].title":"Evening","waves[26].title":"Shadows of the Night","waves[27].title":"The Convoy Withdraws","waves[28].title":"Covering the Retreat","waves[29].title":"Abd al-Rahman","content.waveFacts[0]":"In 711, Berbers and Arabs crossed the strait near Gibraltar, and infantry units were the first to enter Spain.","content.waveFacts[1]":"Slingers marched with the infantry. In Spain, the conquerors' army grew quickly.","content.waveFacts[2]":"Light Berber cavalry launched sudden attacks and retreated just as quickly.","content.waveFacts[3]":"Archers showered the enemy with arrows while the infantry advanced.","content.waveFacts[4]":"By 718, the Arabs had conquered almost all of Spain and reached the Pyrenees.","content.waveFacts[5]":"In spring 732, Governor Abd al-Rahman led his army across the Pyrenees into Aquitaine.","content.waveFacts[6]":"Spearmen wearing chain mail marched with the army. They moved slowly but held their ground.","content.waveFacts[7]":"A supply convoy followed the army, with camels carrying tents, provisions, and stolen goods.","content.waveFacts[8]":"Near the Garonne River, Arab heavy cavalry defeated Duke Odo's army.","content.waveFacts[9]":"Bordeaux was plundered and burned, and the army continued north toward Tours.","content.waveFacts[10]":"Odo fled north to Charles Martel, even though they had previously been enemies.","content.waveFacts[11]":"Charles gathered the Franks, mostly foot soldiers, and marched to meet the enemy.","content.waveFacts[12]":"Camels carried loot from Aquitaine, slowing the Arab army's advance.","content.waveFacts[13]":"Elite ghulam warriors marched with the army. They were among the enemy's most disciplined soldiers.","content.waveFacts[14]":"The vanguard raced ahead of the main army under an emir's command.","content.waveFacts[15]":"The Franks took positions on a high, wooded plain where cavalry would have trouble building up speed.","content.waveFacts[16]":"For about a week, the two armies faced each other and fought small skirmishes.","content.waveFacts[17]":"The Arabs waited for all their forces to arrive, while Charles continued gathering his warriors.","content.waveFacts[18]":"By the seventh day, impatient heavy cavalry began testing the wooded slope.","content.waveFacts[19]":"The supply convoy commander guarded the stolen goods and refused to abandon the loot.","content.waveFacts[20]":"At last, the Arab horsemen charged the Frankish formation.","content.waveFacts[21]":"A chronicler compared the Franks to a wall of ice: they stood motionless under the attacks.","content.waveFacts[22]":"The attacks broke against shields and spears, while throwing axes smashed enemy shields.","content.waveFacts[23]":"Archers and throwers attacked anyone trying to break through the formation.","content.waveFacts[24]":"The army qadi tried to encourage the warriors, but order in their ranks was already breaking down.","content.waveFacts[25]":"By evening, the Arabs were beginning to lose formation, while the Franks still held their ground.","content.waveFacts[26]":"During the night, panic spread through the Arab camp. Rumors of an attack on the loot threw the ranks into confusion.","content.waveFacts[27]":"The Arabs tried to move their supply convoy, but the camels were too slow.","content.waveFacts[28]":"The remaining warriors launched one final attack to cover the retreat.","content.waveFacts[29]":"Abd al-Rahman fell in battle, and the Arabs withdrew during the night, abandoning their camp.","content.quiz[0].q":"What was the religion preached by the Prophet Muhammad called?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"Islam","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"Christianity","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"Judaism","content.quiz[0].explain":"Muhammad preached Islam, and his followers are called Muslims.","content.quiz[1].q":"What was the first royal dynasty of the Franks called?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"Carolingians","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"Merovingians","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"Umayyads","content.quiz[1].explain":"The dynasty was named after Merovech, an ancestor of Clovis.","content.quiz[2].q":"Which country did the Arabs and Berbers begin conquering in 711?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Britain","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Egypt","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Spain","content.quiz[2].explain":"They crossed the strait near Gibraltar and defeated the Visigoths.","content.quiz[3].q":"What is the holy book of Islam called?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"Quran","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"Bible","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"Gospel","content.quiz[3].explain":"The Quran is a book containing the words revealed to the Prophet Muhammad.","content.quiz[4].q":"Which Frankish king converted to Christianity around 496?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"Charlemagne","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"Clovis","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"Pepin the Short","content.quiz[4].explain":"Clovis was baptized, bringing the Franks closer to the Roman population of Gaul.","content.quiz[5].q":"What was the chief administrator of the royal household under the Merovingians called?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"Consul","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"Chancellor","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Mayor of the Palace","content.quiz[5].explain":"The mayors of the palace gradually took power from the weak kings.","content.quiz[6].q":"What was the ruler of the Arab Caliphate called?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"Caliph","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"Emperor","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"Consul","content.quiz[6].explain":"The caliph was both a ruler and a religious leader.","content.quiz[7].q":"Which city became the center of Arab Spain?","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Córdoba","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Paris","content.quiz[7].explain":"Córdoba was the main city of Al-Andalus.","content.quiz[8].q":"Who effectively ruled the Franks as Mayor of the Palace in 732?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"Clovis","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"Charlemagne","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"Charles Martel","content.quiz[8].explain":"Charles Martel ruled on behalf of a king from the Merovingian dynasty.","content.quiz[9].q":"What does the nickname 'Martel' mean?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"Hammer","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"Lion","content.quiz[9].explain":"Charles's nickname is linked to the way he crushed his enemies like a hammer.","content.quiz[10].q":"What is the name for the migration of Germanic and other tribes in the 4th–7th centuries?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"The Crusades","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"The Migration Period","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"The Greco-Persian Wars","content.quiz[10].explain":"The Franks, who settled in Gaul, were among these peoples.","content.quiz[11].q":"What was the Eastern Roman Empire called?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Aquitaine","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"The Caliphate","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"The Byzantine Empire","content.quiz[11].explain":"The Byzantine Empire outlasted the Western Roman Empire by almost a thousand years.","content.quiz[12].q":"What was the Frankish throwing axe called?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Francisca","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Catapult","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Mace","content.quiz[12].explain":"The name 'francisca' is related to the word 'Frank.'","content.quiz[13].q":"Which wealthy abbey attracted the Arab army in 732?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"A monastery in Kyiv","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"The Abbey of Saint Martin in Tours","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"The cathedral in Aachen","content.quiz[13].explain":"The Arabs marched toward Tours, hoping to capture valuable treasure.","content.quiz[14].q":"Who were the Berbers?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"A Frankish tribe","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"People from Arabia","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"The indigenous people of North Africa","content.quiz[14].explain":"The Berbers adopted Islam and made up much of the army that invaded Spain.","content.quiz[15].q":"Who was Duke of Aquitaine in 732?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"Odo","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"Charlemagne","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"Pepin the Short","content.quiz[15].explain":"Odo defeated the Arabs near Toulouse in 721, but he was defeated near the Garonne River in 732.","content.quiz[16].q":"What was a benefice?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"A tax on merchants","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"Land granted to a warrior in exchange for service","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"A monastery school","content.quiz[16].explain":"A benefice helped a warrior afford a horse and armor. Knights later developed from such warriors.","content.quiz[17].q":"What did a chronicler describe as a 'wall of ice'?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"The Clain River","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"The Arab camp","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"The Franks standing motionless","content.quiz[17].explain":"The chronicler described how the Franks stood firm without moving under attack.","content.quiz[18].q":"About how many days did the armies reportedly face each other near Poitiers before the main battle?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"About seven","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"One","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"Forty","content.quiz[18].explain":"The armies spent seven days waiting and fighting small skirmishes.","content.quiz[19].q":"Which dynasty of Arab caliphs ruled from Damascus in 732?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"The Carolingians","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"The Umayyads","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"The Merovingians","content.quiz[19].explain":"The Umayyads ruled the Caliphate, and Spain was one of their territories.","content.quiz[20].q":"What was feudalism?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"The caliph's rule over cities","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"Government by a people's assembly","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"A system in which people received land in exchange for serving a lord","content.quiz[20].explain":"Under feudalism, warriors called vassals served their lords in exchange for land.","content.quiz[21].q":"Which warriors formed the backbone of Charles Martel's army at the Battle of Poitiers?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"Foot soldiers","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"Chariots","content.quiz[21].explain":"Most historians believe the Franks fought on foot in a tight formation.","content.quiz[22].q":"What did Charles do when the Arabs retreated?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Chased them all the way to Córdoba","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"Did not pursue them far","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"Made an alliance with them","content.quiz[22].explain":"He was cautious about a possible trap and did not march beyond the Pyrenees.","content.quiz[23].q":"What was the name of the dynasty of Charles Martel, Pepin the Short, and Charlemagne?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"The Merovingians","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"The Umayyads","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"The Carolingians","content.quiz[23].explain":"The name comes from Charles.","content.quiz[24].q":"Who became the first Carolingian king in 751?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Pepin the Short","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"Charles Martel","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Clovis","content.quiz[24].explain":"Pepin, the son of Charles Martel, removed the last Merovingian king from power.","content.quiz[25].q":"What were heavily armored horsemen who served a lord in exchange for land called?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"Gladiators","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"Knights","content.quiz[25].explain":"Knighthood developed from mounted warriors who received land in exchange for service.","content.quiz[26].q":"Which city was the main center of Charlemagne's empire?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Córdoba","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Tours","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Aachen","content.quiz[26].explain":"Charlemagne often lived in Aachen.","content.quiz[27].q":"What helped the Franks win, according to historians?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"A tight formation of foot soldiers on a wooded hill","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Cannons","content.quiz[27].explain":"The Arab cavalry could not build up speed, and its attacks broke against the Frankish shields.","content.quiz[28].q":"What happened in 800?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"The Battle of Poitiers","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"The pope crowned Charlemagne emperor","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"The baptism of Clovis","content.quiz[28].explain":"This brought an empire back to Western Europe.","content.quiz[29].q":"What happened to Charlemagne's empire after his death?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"It conquered the Byzantine Empire","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"It remained united for a thousand years","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"It was divided by a treaty in 843","content.quiz[29].explain":"The Treaty of Verdun divided the lands among Charlemagne's grandsons.","content.events[0].title":"How Should We Pay the Warriors?","content.events[0].text":"You are Charles Martel, Mayor of the Palace of the Franks. You need strong horsemen to fight your enemies, but horses and armor are expensive. How can you recruit these warriors?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Grant land in exchange for military service","content.events[0].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Land granted for service was called a benefice, and a warrior could use it to afford a horse and armor. Charles is said to have taken some land from the Church. Knights later developed from such warriors.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Pay them money from the treasury","content.events[0].options[1].result":"There was not always enough money because the Frankish treasury was not wealthy. That is why Charles paid warriors with land instead of coins.","content.events[1].title":"Should We Ask for Help?","content.events[1].text":"Duke Odo has been defeated by the Arabs near the Garonne River. He has long been Charles's enemy, but the Arab army is marching north. What should Odo do?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Ask Charles for help","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Odo fled north and accepted Charles's authority. Charles then led the Frankish army to meet the Arabs.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"Refuse help and fight alone","content.events[1].options[1].result":"Odo had almost no forces left. Without Charles's help, the Arabs might have reached Tours.","content.events[2].title":"Where Should We Face the Enemy?","content.events[2].text":"Charles is leading his army to meet the Arabs. The Arabs have powerful cavalry. Where should the Franks take their positions?","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Take positions on a wooded hill","content.events[2].options[0].result":"That is what happened. According to accounts, Charles chose high ground with trees, where the cavalry could not build up speed or break through the formation.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Move into an open field","content.events[2].options[1].result":"The Arab horsemen would have been stronger in an open field. Charles did not allow that to happen.","content.events[3].title":"Attack or Wait?","content.events[3].text":"The armies have been facing each other for several days. Should we attack the Arabs with our cavalry or wait?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Wait for the enemy to attack first","content.events[3].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Skirmishes continued for about a week. Charles waited and gathered his strength, and the Arabs had to attack uphill.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Attack first with our cavalry","content.events[3].options[1].result":"The Frankish cavalry was weaker than the Arab cavalry, and the attack could have ended in disaster.","content.events[4].title":"How Should We Stand Against the Attacks?","content.events[4].text":"Arab horsemen charge again and again. What should the Franks do?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Hold a tight formation","content.events[4].options[0].result":"That is what happened. A chronicler wrote that the northerners stood motionless like a wall and did not let the enemy break through.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Chase the retreating horsemen","content.events[4].options[1].result":"The formation would have fallen apart, allowing the cavalry to attack isolated warriors. Charles kept his soldiers in formation.","content.events[5].title":"The Camp Is Empty","content.events[5].text":"In the morning, the Franks discover that the Arabs have left, abandoning their tents and loot. Should they chase them?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Search the camp, collect the loot, and do not pursue","content.events[5].options[0].result":"That is what happened. At first, the Franks feared a trap, but then they collected the abandoned goods. Charles did not advance farther because his army was tired and a pursuit in unfamiliar territory was dangerous.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Immediately chase them to the Pyrenees","content.events[5].options[1].result":"That could have led to an ambush. Charles decided not to take the risk and did not pursue the retreating army.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Frankish Militia","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"Every free Frank was required to answer the call to war. Militia fighters carried spears and round shields, but they had almost no armor. There were many of them, and they formed the backbone of Charles's army.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Shield Wall Infantry","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"Frankish foot soldiers stood in a tight formation, shield to shield. They pointed their spears forward, making it difficult for horsemen to break through. At Poitiers, this formation stopped the Arab cavalry.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Francisca Throwers","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"A francisca was a small axe thrown at the enemy. It could split a shield or wound an enemy at close range. Its name is linked to the word 'Frank.'","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"Many Frankish hunters owned bows. Arrows could strike from a distance, but they were weak against armor. Archers helped stop camels and infantry.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Charles's Scara","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"A 'scara' was a group of noble warriors who served a ruler. They had better weapons, chain mail, and horses. At Poitiers, however, most historians believe the Franks fought mainly on foot.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Shield Wall","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"The warriors stood shoulder to shoulder and blocked the road. The enemy had to stop or fight its way through the formation. A chronicler later compared the Franks to a wall.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Arab Infantry","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"Infantry formed the backbone of the army, and most of the warriors were Berbers. They carried spears, swords, and small shields. They advanced in a tight mass but quickly lost their formation.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Berber Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"The Berbers are the indigenous people of North Africa. They adopted Islam and helped the Arabs conquer Spain. Their light horsemen threw javelins and quickly escaped pursuit.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Slingers","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"A sling was a strap used to throw stones. An experienced slinger could injure an enemy from a great distance. This weapon was cheap and simple.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Arab Archers","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"Arab warriors were skilled with bows. Archers fired at enemy formations to disrupt them before an attack. But the Frankish shields and the hill made their task difficult.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Syrian Spearmen","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"Warriors from many lands served in Arab armies, including soldiers from Syria. They wore chain mail and carried round shields. They moved slowly but could withstand many blows.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"Arab Heavy Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"Horsemen wearing chain mail and carrying spears and swords were the army's main striking force. In open terrain, they could defeat infantry. But in forests and on slopes, they had trouble building up speed.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Pack Camels","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"Camels were hardy animals that needed little care, so they carried supplies and tents. The supply convoy also carried loot taken in Aquitaine. After the battle, the Arabs abandoned the convoy.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Ghulams","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"The word 'ghulam' meant 'young man' or a servant who became a warrior. It was used for elite soldiers in a commander's personal guard. They were better armed and more disciplined than ordinary fighters.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Vanguard Emir","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"An emir was a military commander or the ruler of a region or military unit. The vanguard marched ahead of the army, scouted the route, and was the first to enter battle. The commander's name is not recorded in historical sources.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Supply Convoy Commander","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"On every campaign, someone had to take care of the pack animals, supplies, and loot. The Arab army needed such a person too. His name is unknown, but losing the supply convoy at Poitiers was a serious blow to the army.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Army Qadi","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"A qadi was a judge who knew Islamic law. On campaign, he maintained order and helped settle disputes. Historical sources do not record the name of this army's qadi.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"He was the governor of Al-Andalus, or Arab Spain. In 732, he led an army across the Pyrenees, defeated Odo, and reached the area near Poitiers. He was killed in battle against the Franks, and his army then withdrew.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Charles Martel","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Charles was the Mayor of the Palace of the Franks and ruled on behalf of the Merovingian kings. His nickname, Martel, means 'the Hammer.' After his victory at Poitiers, he was praised as a defender of Christian Europe. He died in 741, and power passed to his sons.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"He was an experienced military commander and governor of Al-Andalus. In 732, he led a large army across the Pyrenees in search of loot and glory. He was killed at the Battle of Poitiers, which decided the fate of the campaign.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Odo of Aquitaine","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Odo was the Duke of Aquitaine, which was independent of the Franks. In 721, he defeated the Arabs near Toulouse. In 732, the Arabs defeated him near the Garonne River, and he asked his longtime enemy Charles for help.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Pepin the Short","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Pepin was the son of Charles Martel. In 751, with the pope's support, he became King of the Franks and founded the Carolingian dynasty. Later, he helped the pope fight the Lombards, leading to the creation of the Papal States.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Charlemagne","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Charlemagne was the grandson of Charles Martel and the son of Pepin the Short. He conquered many lands and was crowned emperor in Rome in 800. After his death, his empire soon broke apart.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"The Arabs Conquer Spain","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"In 711, Arabs and Berbers crossed into Spain and defeated the Visigoths. By 718, they had conquered almost the entire peninsula. Beyond it lay the Pyrenees and the lands of the Franks.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"Raid on Aquitaine","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"In 732, Abd al-Rahman crossed the Pyrenees and entered Aquitaine. He defeated Odo near the Garonne River and plundered Bordeaux. The campaign was more of a raid for loot than an attempt to conquer the country.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Odo Asks Charles for Help","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"Odo had long been Charles's enemy, but after his defeat, he went to Charles for help. Charles gathered the Franks and marched to meet the Arabs. Faced with a common danger, the two enemies became allies.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"Seven Days near Poitiers","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"The armies took positions between Poitiers and Tours, near the Clain River. They faced each other for about a week. The Franks occupied high ground while the Arabs gathered their forces.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"Wall of Ice","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"The Chronicle of 754 says that the northerners stood motionless and held together like ice. The Arab cavalry charged their formation again and again without breaking through. The Arabs called the place the 'Pavement of the Martyrs.'","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"The Arab Retreat","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"Abd al-Rahman fell in battle, and the Arabs withdrew during the night, abandoning their camp. At first, the Franks suspected a trick, but then they found the tents empty. Charles did not pursue the enemy far. Historians debate the exact year of the battle: 732 or 733.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"The Frankish Army and Shield Wall","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"Most of the army consisted of free Frankish militiamen armed with spears and shields. Noble warriors had chain mail, swords, and horses. In formation, the soldiers stood close together and moved slowly forward on command without breaking their line.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"Benefice","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"Charles Martel and other rulers granted warriors land in exchange for military service. This land was called a benefice. A warrior lived off the land and was required to come to war with a horse and weapons. This helped lead to the rise of knighthood and feudalism.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"Córdoba and Al-Andalus","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"Arab Spain was called Al-Andalus, and its main city was Córdoba. In 732, it was an emirate within the Umayyad Caliphate. The Emirate of Córdoba became independent later, in 756.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Tours and Saint Martin","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"Martin of Tours lived in the 4th century and was considered a patron saint of the Franks. A wealthy abbey named after him stood in Tours. The Arabs were heading there but never reached it.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"What Happened Next: The Carolingians","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"Charles's son Pepin became king, and his grandson Charlemagne became emperor. The Carolingian Empire later broke apart into several states. France and Germany eventually developed from these lands."},"spartacus":{"locked[0].name":"Crossbow","locked[1].name":"Knight","locked[2].name":"Cannon","enemies[1].name":"Legionaries","waves[28].title":"The final assault","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Hannibal","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"Hannibal","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Legionaries","title":"Spartacus's Revolt","year":"73–71 BC","side":"Spartacus's Rebel Slaves","foe":"Roman Republic","intro[0]":"You are Spartacus, a gladiator who escaped from a school in Capua. Slaves and shepherds are joining you by the thousands.","intro[1]":"Roman legions are advancing from every direction. Hold the camp and save your people.","victoryText":"In history, Spartacus died in battle near the Silarus River in 71 BC, and about six thousand of his fighters were crucified along the Appian Way from Capua to Rome. But here, you held the camp! Spartacus is remembered because a former slave fought Roman legions for almost three years and became a symbol of the struggle for freedom.","defeatText":"The Roman legions broke into the camp, and Spartacus's revolt was crushed.","theme.spawnSign":"From Rome","fortress.name":"SPARTACUS'S CAMP","features[0].text":"Slope of Mount Vesuvius","features[1].text":"Road South","defenders[0].name":"Shepherd Slingers","defenders[0].note":"Cheap and fast: sling stones","defenders[0].fact":"The shepherds of southern Italy were skilled at using slings to protect their flocks from wolves, and many joined the rebels.","defenders[1].name":"Murmillo Gladiators","defenders[1].note":"Main fighter: sword and large shield","defenders[1].fact":"A murmillo was a gladiator armed with a short sword called a gladius, a large shield, and a helmet with a tall crest. These fighters formed the core of Spartacus's force.","defenders[2].name":"Crixus's Germans and Gauls","defenders[2].note":"Heavy fighters with powerful blows; deadly against infantry","defenders[2].fact":"The Gauls and Germans under Crixus formed the largest and fiercest part of Spartacus's army.","defenders[3].name":"Thracian Javelin Throwers","defenders[3].note":"Fast javelins, especially effective against cavalry","defenders[3].fact":"Thrace, Spartacus's homeland, was famous for its light infantry, who skillfully threw javelins.","defenders[4].name":"Captured Ballistae","defenders[4].note":"Siege weapon: area damage, pierces armor","defenders[4].fact":"The rebels armed themselves with weapons captured from defeated Roman units, and throwing machines such as ballistae could have fallen into their hands.","defenders[5].name":"Vines and Traps","defenders[5].note":"Support: slows enemies","defenders[5].fact":"On Mount Vesuvius, the rebels made ropes from wild grapevines and used them to climb down a cliff behind the Romans.","locked[0].line":"Crossbows will not reach Europe for more than a thousand years","locked[1].line":"Knights will not appear until the Middle Ages, a thousand years later","locked[2].line":"Gunpowder will not reach Europe for another fifteen hundred years","locked[3].name":"Cataphract","locked[3].line":"Heavy armored cavalry belongs to Persia and Byzantium, not Italy","abilities[0].name":"Descent from Vesuvius","abilities[0].fact":"The rebels climbed down the cliff on ropes made from vines and launched a surprise attack on Glaber's camp from behind.","abilities[1].name":"Crixus's Battle Cry","abilities[1].fact":"Crixus's Gauls and Germans charged into battle first and fought so fiercely that they frightened the Romans.","enemies[0].name":"Militia","enemies[0].fact":"A hastily assembled town militia from Campania: poorly armed men who fled from experienced fighters.","enemies[1].fact":"A Roman legionary was a trained soldier armed with a gladius sword, two pila javelins, and a large scutum shield.","enemies[2].name":"Heavy Legionaries","enemies[2].fact":"Legionaries wearing chain mail made of metal rings were well protected against arrows and stones.","enemies[3].name":"Mercenary Slingers","enemies[3].fact":"Rome hired skilled slingers from the Balearic Islands, whose stones could strike from far away.","enemies[4].name":"Roman Cavalry","enemies[4].fact":"The Roman cavalry was small, but it could quickly catch fleeing enemies and scout ahead.","enemies[5].name":"Centurions","enemies[5].fact":"A centurion commanded about a hundred soldiers and kept them in formation, making nearby soldiers fight more bravely.","enemies[6].name":"Runaway Slave Hunters","enemies[6].fact":"According to some accounts, runaway slaves in Rome were tracked with dogs, and these hunting parties could follow a trail quickly.","enemies[7].name":"Praetorian Cohort","enemies[7].fact":"A praetorian cohort was a Roman commander's personal guard, made up of elite soldiers wearing good armor.","bosses[0].name":"Claudius Glaber","bosses[0].fact":"Praetor Claudius Glaber trapped the fugitives on Mount Vesuvius with a militia force but left the cliff unguarded.","bosses[1].name":"Consul Gellius","bosses[1].fact":"Consul Lucius Gellius Publicola defeated Crixus's force near Mount Garganus but was later defeated by Spartacus.","bosses[2].name":"Pompey","bosses[2].fact":"Gnaeus Pompey returned from Spain near the end of the war, intercepted five thousand fleeing rebels, and claimed the victory for himself.","bosses[3].name":"Marcus Crassus","bosses[3].fact":"Crassus, the richest man in Rome, received six new legions, ordered a huge trench to be dug, and crushed the revolt through strict military discipline.","chapters[0].title":"Escape from Capua","chapters[0].year":"73 BC","chapters[0].fact":"About seventy gladiators escaped from a school in Capua with kitchen knives and roasting spits, then armed themselves with captured weapons.","chapters[1].title":"Mount Vesuvius","chapters[1].year":"73 BC","chapters[1].fact":"The fugitives fortified a position on the slopes of Mount Vesuvius, which was a dormant volcano at the time, while slaves and shepherds flocked to join them.","chapters[2].title":"Victory over Glaber","chapters[2].year":"73 BC","chapters[2].fact":"The rebels climbed down a cliff using vines and attacked Praetor Glaber's camp from behind.","chapters[3].title":"Defeating the Consuls and Marching toward the Alps","chapters[3].year":"72 BC","chapters[3].fact":"Crixus split away and was killed, while Spartacus defeated both consuls, reached the Alps, but then turned back.","chapters[4].title":"Crassus's Trench in Bruttium","chapters[4].year":"71 BC","chapters[4].fact":"Crassus trapped Spartacus's army behind a trench in southern Italy, while pirates failed to keep their promise to carry the rebels to Sicily.","chapters[5].title":"Final Battle at the Silarus","chapters[5].year":"71 BC","chapters[5].fact":"Spartacus decided to attack Crassus before Pompey arrived and died fighting near the Silarus River.","waves[0].title":"Capua City Guards","waves[1].title":"Pursuit from Capua","waves[2].title":"Campanian Militia","waves[3].title":"Troops from Nearby Towns","waves[4].title":"Cavalry Scouts","waves[5].title":"Camp at the Foot of the Mountain","waves[6].title":"Runaway Slave Hunters","waves[7].title":"Blockade of the Only Path","waves[8].title":"Night Patrol","waves[9].title":"Hunger on the Mountain","waves[10].title":"Glaber's Camp","waves[11].title":"Three Thousand Militiamen","waves[12].title":"Attack from Behind","waves[13].title":"Flight from the Camp","waves[14].title":"Praetor Glaber","waves[15].title":"Varinius's Legates","waves[16].title":"Consular Legions","waves[17].title":"Mount Garganus","waves[18].title":"Road to Mutina","waves[19].title":"Consul Gellius","waves[20].title":"Crassus Takes Command","waves[21].title":"Decimation","waves[22].title":"Trench Across Bruttium","waves[23].title":"Snowy Night at the Trench","waves[24].title":"Pompey Marches South","waves[25].title":"Rome Gathers All Its Forces","waves[26].title":"Camp by the Silarus River","waves[27].title":"Crassus's Heavy Infantry","waves[29].title":"Crassus at the Silarus","content.waveFacts[0]":"Gladiators escaped from the school in Capua, and the city guards rushed after them.","content.waveFacts[1]":"The pursuit was weak: the fugitives defeated the force sent from Capua and captured real weapons.","content.waveFacts[2]":"News of the escape spread through Campania, and a militia with its first legionaries was assembled to fight the fugitives.","content.waveFacts[3]":"The authorities of nearby towns sent slingers to stop the fugitives, who were still poorly organized.","content.waveFacts[4]":"The Romans sent cavalry to scout the hiding place of the escaped slaves.","content.waveFacts[5]":"Spartacus led his people to Mount Vesuvius and settled on its slope, while a Roman camp was set up below.","content.waveFacts[6]":"According to some accounts, hunters with dogs were sent after the fugitives, but they moved slowly on the steep slopes of Vesuvius.","content.waveFacts[7]":"The Romans blocked the only path down the mountain and decided to starve the fugitives into surrender.","content.waveFacts[8]":"At night, Roman patrols circled the mountain while the rebels above wove ropes from vines.","content.waveFacts[9]":"The hunger and siege continued, and the rebels prepared a daring plan to climb down the cliff.","content.waveFacts[10]":"The slaves climbed down on vines and reached the rear of the Roman camp, where Glaber's militiamen were stationed.","content.waveFacts[11]":"Glaber's three thousand militiamen did not expect an attack from behind and had no time to form ranks.","content.waveFacts[12]":"Glaber's camp was destroyed, and the rebels captured Roman weapons and supplies.","content.waveFacts[13]":"The surviving Romans fled, while new crowds of slaves and shepherds flocked to the victors.","content.waveFacts[14]":"Praetor Claudius Glaber was defeated. The Romans had not suffered such an embarrassing defeat in a long time.","content.waveFacts[15]":"Rome sent Praetor Publius Varinius against Spartacus, but his legates were defeated one after another.","content.waveFacts[16]":"Then Rome sent both consuls with four legions against the rebels.","content.waveFacts[17]":"Crixus separated from Spartacus, and his force was destroyed near Mount Garganus by Consul Gellius.","content.waveFacts[18]":"Spartacus defeated the army of the governor of Cisalpine Gaul, opening the road to the Alps.","content.waveFacts[19]":"Spartacus avenged Crixus by defeating both consuls and nearly reached the Alps, but then turned back.","content.waveFacts[20]":"The Senate gave Crassus command and six new legions to put an end to the revolt.","content.waveFacts[21]":"Crassus executed every tenth soldier from units that had fled the battlefield, restoring discipline through fear.","content.waveFacts[22]":"Spartacus retreated south into Bruttium, while Crassus began digging a trench across the peninsula.","content.waveFacts[23]":"On a snowy night, the rebels filled part of the trench with bodies and branches and broke through, losing many fighters.","content.waveFacts[24]":"The Senate called Pompey for help, and Crassus feared that Pompey would take the glory of victory.","content.waveFacts[25]":"Rome gathered every force it could against Spartacus, with legionaries approaching from all directions.","content.waveFacts[26]":"Spartacus's army camped near the Silarus River, and some of his warriors were already eager to fight.","content.waveFacts[27]":"Crassus deployed his heavy infantry and praetorian cohort, the most reliable part of his army.","content.waveFacts[28]":"Spartacus charged straight toward Crassus, but the Romans closed ranks and prevented him from breaking through.","content.waveFacts[29]":"According to some accounts, Spartacus fought to the very end and tried to reach Crassus himself.","content.quiz[0].q":"What was a slave considered to be in ancient Rome?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"The property of an owner","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"A free person without land","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"A servant who received wages","content.quiz[0].explain":"Slaves had almost no rights. Their owners could sell, punish, or give them away.","content.quiz[1].q":"Where was Spartacus from, according to ancient writers?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"Egypt","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"Thrace","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"Britain","content.quiz[1].explain":"Spartacus was a Thracian. He served in the Roman army, then fled, was captured, and became a gladiator.","content.quiz[2].q":"Where did most slaves in Rome come from?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"The people of Rome itself","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Settlers from Carthage under a treaty","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Prisoners captured in wars","content.quiz[2].explain":"Rome's victorious wars produced thousands of prisoners who were sold into slavery.","content.quiz[3].q":"In which city was the gladiator school that Spartacus escaped from?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"Athens","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"Capua","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"Carthage","content.quiz[3].explain":"Lentulus Batiatus's school was in Capua, not far from Mount Vesuvius.","content.quiz[4].q":"What was a latifundium?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"A fortress on the border","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"A slave market in Rome","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"A huge estate worked by slaves","content.quiz[4].explain":"Wealthy Romans bought large areas of land and used slave labor to farm them.","content.quiz[5].q":"What weapons did the fugitives have at first?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"Kitchen knives and roasting spits","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"Roman swords from the treasury","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Spears from the Capua armory","content.quiz[5].explain":"At first, they had only what they could find in the school's kitchen.","content.quiz[6].q":"Where were gladiator fights held in Rome and Italy?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"In the Senate building","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"In a temple","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"In an amphitheater","content.quiz[6].explain":"An amphitheater was a round building with rows of seats surrounding an arena.","content.quiz[7].q":"On which mountain did the rebels establish their stronghold?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Mount Vesuvius","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Mount Olympus","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Mount Etna","content.quiz[7].explain":"Vesuvius was considered dormant at the time. Its massive eruption did not occur until AD 79.","content.quiz[8].q":"What did the phrase 'bread and circuses' mean?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"A call for peaceful trade","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"Giving out bread and holding games to entertain Rome's poor","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"The name of a harvest festival","content.quiz[8].explain":"The authorities fed and entertained the crowds to prevent them from rebelling.","content.quiz[9].q":"How did Spartacus's fighters attack Glaber from behind?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"They climbed down ropes made from vines","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"They crossed a lake on rafts","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"They used an underground tunnel","content.quiz[9].explain":"The Romans guarded the path but left the steep cliff unguarded.","content.quiz[10].q":"Who were the Gracchi brothers?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"Hannibal's generals","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"Tribunes who wanted to give land to the poor","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"Greek philosophers","content.quiz[10].explain":"Tiberius and Gaius Gracchus fought to give land to the poor and died in the struggle.","content.quiz[11].q":"Who was the second rebel leader alongside Spartacus?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Brutus","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Crixus","content.quiz[11].explain":"Crixus commanded a group of Gauls and Germans and later separated from Spartacus.","content.quiz[12].q":"What did Gaius Marius change in the Roman army?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"He abolished the legions","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"He began recruiting poor volunteers into the legions","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"He introduced elephants into the army","content.quiz[12].explain":"After Marius, many soldiers served for pay and became loyal to their commanders.","content.quiz[13].q":"What did Spartacus propose after his first victories?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"Capture Rome","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"Surrender to the Senate","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"Leave Italy by crossing the Alps","content.quiz[13].explain":"He wanted his fighters to return home, but many stayed to plunder Italy.","content.quiz[14].q":"Who won the civil war against Marius and became dictator in 82 BC?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"Sulla","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"Caesar","content.quiz[14].explain":"Marius and Sulla fought for power, and Sulla won.","content.quiz[15].q":"According to Appian, how did Spartacus honor Crixus's memory?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"He built a temple","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"He released all the prisoners","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"He forced 300 captured Romans to fight as gladiators","content.quiz[15].explain":"The Romans were forced to fight in an arena, just as the slaves had once fought.","content.quiz[16].q":"What was the Appian Way?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"A paved road running south from Rome to Capua","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"A canal for ships","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"A wall around Rome","content.quiz[16].explain":"It was built around 312 BC, and captured rebels were later crucified along it.","content.quiz[17].q":"Who was Crassus?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"A pirate leader","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"Rome's richest man, who was given an army to fight Spartacus","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"A friend of Spartacus","content.quiz[17].explain":"Crassus had made an enormous fortune, and the war against Spartacus brought him fame.","content.quiz[18].q":"What was a triumph in ancient Rome?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"A ceremonial parade honoring a victorious general","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"A tax on slaves","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"A harvest festival","content.quiz[18].explain":"Victorious commanders marched through the city with prisoners and captured treasures.","content.quiz[19].q":"What did Crassus order his soldiers to build in Bruttium?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"A bridge across the sea","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"A long trench and earthwork across the peninsula","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"An underground tunnel","content.quiz[19].explain":"The trench was about 55 kilometers long and was meant to trap Spartacus.","content.quiz[20].q":"Who was a centurion?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"A priest serving with a legion","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"A cavalry commander","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"A commander of about one hundred soldiers","content.quiz[20].explain":"Centurions kept their soldiers in formation and maintained discipline.","content.quiz[21].q":"Who tricked Spartacus by promising to transport his army to Sicily?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"Greek merchants","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"Cilician pirates","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"Carthaginians","content.quiz[21].explain":"The pirates took the payment and sailed away, leaving the rebels on shore.","content.quiz[22].q":"Who were the plebeians?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Priests","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"Slaves","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"Ordinary free Roman citizens","content.quiz[22].explain":"The plebeians won equal rights with the patricians and gained their own tribunes.","content.quiz[23].q":"How many rebels were crucified along the Appian Way?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"About six thousand","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"About sixty","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"About six hundred thousand","content.quiz[23].explain":"The crosses stretched all the way from Capua to Rome.","content.quiz[24].q":"Who led the Roman Republic?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"One king who ruled for life","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"A council of slaves","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Two elected consuls","content.quiz[24].explain":"Consuls were elected for one year to prevent any one person from taking all the power.","content.quiz[25].q":"Who intercepted five thousand fleeing rebels and claimed the victory?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"Pompey","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"Caesar","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Antony","content.quiz[25].explain":"Pompey returned from Spain and defeated the remaining rebel groups, angering Crassus.","content.quiz[26].q":"Who formed the First Triumvirate in 60 BC?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Antony, Octavian, and Lepidus","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Caesar, Pompey, and Crassus","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Brutus, Cassius, and Cicero","content.quiz[26].explain":"Ten years after the war, the men who had defeated Spartacus became Rome's rulers.","content.quiz[27].q":"What is known about Spartacus's body?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"It was never found among the dead","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"It was buried with honors in Rome","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"It was displayed in the Colosseum","content.quiz[27].explain":"Ancient writers say that the rebel leader's body was never identified.","content.quiz[28].q":"Which city did the Romans destroy in 146 BC, selling its people into slavery?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"Sparta","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"Carthage","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"Memphis","content.quiz[28].explain":"The victory over Carthage brought Rome thousands of new slaves.","content.quiz[29].q":"Why is Spartacus still remembered today?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"He became a Roman emperor","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"He founded a city","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"A former slave fought Roman legions for almost three years in a struggle for freedom","content.quiz[29].explain":"The revolt failed, but its leader became a symbol of the struggle for freedom.","content.events[0].title":"Where Should We Hide?","content.events[0].text":"The fugitives have escaped from Capua, but troops are already gathering around them. Everyone looks to Spartacus. Where should he lead the group?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"To Mount Vesuvius","content.events[0].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Spartacus took a position on the slopes of Vesuvius, where there was access to water and forests, and slaves began gathering there.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Run straight to Rome","content.events[0].options[1].result":"Marching against the strongest city, where Roman legions were stationed, would be madness. The fugitives would be defeated before reaching its walls.","content.events[1].title":"Trapped on the Mountain","content.events[1].text":"Glaber has blocked the only path down and is waiting for the camp to run out of supplies. What should we do?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Weave ropes from vines and climb down the cliff","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That is what happened. The rebels climbed down the vines and attacked the Roman camp from behind, defeating its soldiers.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"Fight our way down the path","content.events[1].options[1].result":"On the narrow path, the Romans could have killed the attackers one by one. That is why Spartacus chose the cliff.","content.events[2].title":"Cross the Alps or Stay?","content.events[2].text":"Victories over Glaber and Varinius have brought fame, and thousands of people have joined the camp. Spartacus wants to cross the Alps, but many fighters want to plunder wealthy Italy.","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Leave Italy by crossing the Alps","content.events[2].options[0].result":"That was exactly what Spartacus wanted, but his warriors refused to leave. They lived off the loot they took in Italy, and soon the consular legions marched against them.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Stay and plunder the cities","content.events[2].options[1].result":"That is what happened because most of the fighters wanted to stay. But the revolt dragged on, and the Senate assembled a huge army against them.","content.events[3].title":"Crixus Breaks Away","content.events[3].text":"Crixus wants to take his Gauls and Germans away from Spartacus and fight on his own. What should we do?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Keep everyone together","content.events[3].options[0].result":"That would have been safer, but Crixus broke away and was defeated by Consul Gellius near Mount Garganus. About twenty thousand of his men died.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Let Crixus go on his own","content.events[3].options[1].result":"Crixus broke away, and his fighters, left without support, were killed in battle against the consul near Mount Garganus.","content.events[4].title":"The Pirates' Trick","content.events[4].text":"Crassus has blocked the road with a trench. Cilician pirates promise to transport the army to Sicily for a fee. Should we trust them?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Do not trust them. Break through the trench instead","content.events[4].options[0].result":"Spartacus trusted the pirates, but they took his payment and sailed away. The rebels then broke through the trench on a snowy night, losing many fighters.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Wait for the pirates' ships","content.events[4].options[1].result":"The pirates tricked Spartacus. They accepted his gifts and sailed away, while Crassus finished the trench.","content.events[5].title":"Fight Before Pompey Arrives","content.events[5].text":"Pompey is marching from Spain with an army to join Crassus. If Spartacus waits, Rome's forces will grow even stronger. What should he do?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Attack Crassus before Pompey arrives","content.events[5].options[0].result":"That is what Spartacus decided, but the Romans closed ranks. He died fighting near the Silarus River, and his army was defeated.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Retreat into the mountains","content.events[5].options[1].result":"Spartacus chose not to retreat into the mountains. He knew that time was on Rome's side, so he marched into his final battle.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Shepherd Slingers","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"Many shepherds in southern Italy were slaves or poor people who joined Spartacus. They were skilled at throwing stones with slings to protect their flocks from wolves. Their weapons were cheap, and they could fire quickly. The shepherds knew the mountains and paths well.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Murmillo Gladiators","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"A murmillo was a type of gladiator armed with a short gladius sword, a large shield, and a helmet with a tall crest. Schools in Capua trained these fighters for battles in the arena. After escaping, they became the strongest part of Spartacus's army. Their skills helped them defeat even Roman legionaries.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Crixus's Germans and Gauls","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"Spartacus's army included many Gauls and Germans who had been enslaved during Rome's wars. Their leader was Crixus. They fought fiercely and delivered powerful blows. Later, Crixus's group broke away and was destroyed in battle against a consul.","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Thracian Javelin Throwers","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"Thrace was in the Balkans, and its warriors were famous for their light weapons and accurate javelin throws. Spartacus was Thracian, and some of his fighters came from his homeland. Javelins were effective against cavalry but less useful against heavy Roman armor.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Captured Ballistae","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"A ballista was a Roman siege engine that launched heavy bolts and stones. The rebels armed themselves with weapons captured from defeated Roman camps. A ballista bolt could pierce armor and strike groups of enemies. This is the strongest weapon in the camp.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Vines and Traps","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"The rebels in Italy knew the vineyards and wild vines well. When the Romans trapped them on Mount Vesuvius, they wove ropes from vines and climbed down behind the enemy. In the game, vines and traps slow enemies down. Clever tactics often defeated brute strength.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Militia","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"At first, the Romans sent hastily assembled militiamen from the towns of Campania against the fugitives. They were poorly trained and afraid of the former gladiators. Even small groups of rebels could defeat them easily. The Romans soon realized they needed real legions.","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"After Marius's reforms, Roman legionaries served for pay and were well trained. They carried a gladius sword, pila javelins, and a large shield. They fought in tight formations. However, the Romans also sent hastily assembled units against Spartacus.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Heavy Legionaries","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"Heavy legionaries wore chain mail made of iron rings and sturdy helmets. Arrows and stones had trouble piercing their armor. They moved slowly but steadily. Ballistae and heavy axes were effective against them.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Mercenary Slingers","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"Rome hired slingers from the Balearic Islands, and their stones could strike accurately from far away. They were lightly equipped, fast, and wore no armor. Accurate shooters were effective against them. Slingers covered the advance of the legions.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Roman Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"The Roman cavalry was small and consisted of noble horsemen and allied troops. Horsemen quickly caught fleeing enemies and scouted ahead. Javelins were effective against them. Infantry formations with spears could also stop horses.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"Centurions","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"A centurion commanded about one hundred soldiers and kept them in formation. He was an experienced fighter, and nearby soldiers felt more confident. Defeating a centurion weakened the entire unit. His helmet had a crest running from side to side.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Runaway Slave Hunters","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"According to some accounts, dogs were used to track runaway slaves in Rome. Hunters quickly found their trails and brought soldiers with them. They were lightly equipped and very fast. Traps that slowed enemies were effective against them.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Praetorian Cohort","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"A praetorian cohort was a Roman commander's personal guard. The best soldiers were selected and given the finest weapons. They wore strong armor that arrows had trouble piercing. In battle, they held their ground better than other troops.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Claudius Glaber","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"Claudius Glaber was a praetor, a high-ranking Roman official, who received a force of about three thousand men. He trapped Spartacus on Mount Vesuvius and guarded the only path down. But the cliff was unguarded, and the rebels climbed down on vines to get behind his camp. This was the rebels' first major victory.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Consul Gellius","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Lucius Gellius Publicola was a consul in 72 BC. He defeated Crixus's force near Mount Garganus. Later, Spartacus defeated him and the other consul, Lentulus. This was one of the rebels' greatest victories.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Pompey","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Gnaeus Pompey was a young, talented military commander. He returned from Spain near the end of the war and intercepted five thousand rebels fleeing from the Battle of the Silarus. Afterward, Pompey boasted that he had 'pulled the war out by its roots.' Crassus was offended, and the two commanders remained rivals for years.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Marcus Crassus","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"Crassus was the richest man in Rome and received six new legions. He restored discipline and executed every tenth soldier from units that had fled. Then he ordered a huge trench to be dug across Bruttium. In the final battle near the Silarus River, the Romans defeated Spartacus.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Spartacus","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Spartacus was a Thracian who, according to some accounts, served in the Roman army before becoming a gladiator in Capua. In 73 BC, he led an escape and soon gathered tens of thousands of followers. Spartacus was a clever commander, but he was eventually defeated. He died in 71 BC at the Battle of the Silarus, and his body was never found.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Crixus","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Crixus was one of the leaders of the revolt and commanded a force of Gauls and Germans. He separated from Spartacus, and in 72 BC, Consul Gellius defeated his force near Mount Garganus. Crixus himself died in the battle. Spartacus held funeral games in his honor.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Crassus: Wealthy Man and Commander","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Marcus Crassus made an enormous fortune trading land and houses. After defeating Spartacus, he became consul and later joined the First Triumvirate with Caesar and Pompey. In 53 BC, he died during a campaign against the Parthians at Carrhae.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Pompey the Great","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Pompey became famous for his victories in Spain and against pirates at sea. After the war against Spartacus, he became consul alongside Crassus in 70 BC. Later, Pompey joined the Triumvirate, then became Caesar's rival and was killed in Egypt in 48 BC.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Glaber the Praetor","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"The office of praetor was one of the highest positions in Rome, second only to consul. Praetor Claudius Glaber received a militia force from the Senate to fight the slaves. His failure showed how poorly prepared the Roman forces in Italy were for this kind of war.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Escape from Capua, 73 BC","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"About two hundred gladiators at Batiatus's school planned to escape, but someone betrayed their plan, and only about seventy managed to get away. They took knives and roasting spits from the kitchen, then captured gladiator weapons being transported on wagons. Spartacus, Crixus, and Oenomaus became their leaders. Slaves from nearby estates flocked to join them.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"Mount Vesuvius, 73 BC","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"The fugitives established a stronghold on the slopes of Mount Vesuvius, where the terrain provided natural defenses. Rome sent a force to trap them. The only path down was narrow. Tens of thousands of slaves and shepherds gathered at the camp.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Victory over Glaber, 73 BC","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"Praetor Glaber guarded the path and waited for the rebels to surrender from hunger. Instead, they wove ropes from vines and climbed down the cliff. They attacked the Roman camp from behind and defeated its soldiers. The victory inspired thousands of new fighters to join them.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"Defeating the Consuls, 72 BC","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"First, the rebels defeated Praetor Varinius. Then Crixus separated from the army and died near Mount Garganus. Spartacus avenged him by defeating both consuls and marching north. Near Mutina, he also defeated the governor of Cisalpine Gaul, but then turned back.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"Crassus's Trench, 71 BC","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"Crassus dug a trench and built an earthwork about 55 kilometers long across Bruttium. Pirates tricked Spartacus by failing to transport his army to Sicily. On a snowy night, the rebels broke through the trench after filling part of it with bodies and branches. But they lost many fighters.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"Battle of the Silarus, 71 BC","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"Pompey and other Roman commanders were approaching, and Crassus was eager to finish the war. Spartacus attacked Crassus, but the Romans held their ground. Spartacus died, and his army was defeated. About six thousand prisoners were crucified along the Appian Way from Capua to Rome.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"Gladiator Schools","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"Gladiator schools belonged to private owners. Fighters were recruited from prisoners of war, slaves, and convicted criminals, although some people joined voluntarily. They learned to use different weapons, including swords, javelins, and shields. The most famous school of that time was in Capua.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"Slavery in Rome","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"Slaves had no rights: their owners could sell, punish, or give them away. Some were captured in wars, others were bought from pirates, and some were born into slavery. Huge numbers of slaves worked on the vast estates of wealthy landowners, called latifundia. Poor Romans complained that slaves were taking their jobs.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"The Late Republican Legion","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"After Marius's reforms, volunteers joined the legions and received pay. A legion was divided into cohorts, which were divided into smaller units commanded by centurions. Soldiers wore chain mail, helmets, and large shields called scuta. Their main weapons were the gladius sword and pila javelins.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Mount Vesuvius","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"Vesuvius is a volcano near the Bay of Naples. In 73 BC, it was considered dormant, and its slopes were covered with vineyards. In AD 79, it erupted and buried the cities of Pompeii and Herculaneum in ash. Spartacus's rebels spent their first winter on its slopes.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"What Happened Next","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"Crassus and Pompey became consuls in 70 BC. In 60 BC, they formed the First Triumvirate with Caesar. Crassus died in 53 BC during a campaign against the Parthians, Pompey died in 48 BC, and Caesar was assassinated in 44 BC. The Republic gave way to one-man rule, and the Roman Empire began."},"thermopylae":{"defenders[3].name":"Cretan Archers","locked[1].name":"Crossbow","locked[2].name":"Knight","locked[3].name":"Cannon","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Knight","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Cretan Archers","title":"Thermopylae","year":"480 BC","side":"Spartans and Greeks","foe":"Xerxes' Persians","intro[0]":"480 BC. Xerxes' enormous army is marching toward Greece. King Leonidas of Sparta and a small force have taken positions in the narrow pass of Thermopylae: the blue sea lies to the left, and steep mountains rise to the right.","intro[1]":"Your mission is to stop the Persians at the Phocian Wall and keep them from passing through. Survive 30 waves.","victoryText":"In history, Leonidas and his 300 Spartans died when the Persians went around them using a mountain path, but they delayed the enemy for three days. Soon afterward, the Greeks won at Salamis and Plataea, and Xerxes left Greece.","defeatText":"The Persians broke through the defenses and entered Greece, but you can gather your forces and try again.","theme.spawnSign":"From Thessaly","fortress.name":"THERMOPYLAE","features[0].text":"Thermopylae — the Hot Gates","defenders[0].name":"Thespians","defenders[0].note":"Cheap and fast spear attacks","defenders[0].fact":"Seven hundred volunteers from the city of Thespiae joined Leonidas and stayed in the pass until the end.","defenders[1].name":"Peltasts","defenders[1].note":"Fast javelins, effective against cavalry","defenders[1].fact":"Peltasts were light infantry soldiers who threw javelins and carried small crescent-shaped shields.","defenders[2].name":"Spartans","defenders[2].note":"Elite warriors who pierce armor","defenders[2].fact":"Spartans began military training at age seven and wore red cloaks so blood would not show.","defenders[3].note":"Shoot far and often","defenders[3].fact":"Cretans were famous for their accurate archery, and Greek cities hired them to serve in their armies.","defenders[4].name":"Rock Throwers","defenders[4].note":"Drop rocks on groups of enemies, dealing area damage","defenders[4].fact":"Steep cliffs towered over the pass, and warriors could drop rocks from them onto their enemies.","defenders[5].name":"Shield Wall","defenders[5].note":"Does not attack, but holds enemies back for 1.5 seconds","defenders[5].fact":"Hoplites locked their large round shields together, and enemies ran into the wall as if it were a fence.","locked[0].name":"Catapult","locked[0].line":"Will be invented in Syracuse in 80 years","locked[1].line":"Military crossbows will not be invented until the Middle Ages","locked[2].line":"Knights will not appear for another 1,500 years","locked[3].line":"Gunpowder will not reach Europe for another 1,700 years","abilities[0].name":"Phalanx","abilities[0].fact":"The Greek phalanx was a tight formation of warriors standing shoulder to shoulder and shield to shield. It was almost impossible to break through.","abilities[1].name":"Leonidas' Night Raid","abilities[1].fact":"The historian Diodorus wrote that Leonidas attacked the Persian camp on the final night and caused panic.","enemies[0].name":"Medes","enemies[0].fact":"The Medes lived in northwestern Iran and were related to the Persians. There were many of them in Xerxes' army.","enemies[1].name":"Cissians","enemies[1].fact":"The Cissians lived in Susiana, near the capital city of Susa, and wore headbands instead of helmets.","enemies[2].name":"Persian Archers","enemies[2].fact":"Persian archers placed woven shields in front of themselves and fired clouds of arrows from behind them.","enemies[3].name":"Saka","enemies[3].fact":"The Saka were steppe horsemen from Central Asia who were excellent at shooting bows while riding.","enemies[4].name":"Arab Camel Riders","enemies[4].fact":"Herodotus listed Arab camel riders among Xerxes' warriors.","enemies[5].name":"Immortals","enemies[5].fact":"The Immortals were the 10,000 best Persian warriors. When one was killed, a new soldier immediately took his place, so their number stayed the same.","enemies[6].name":"Egyptians","enemies[6].fact":"Egyptians in Xerxes' army wore linen armor and fought with heavy spears and swords.","enemies[7].name":"Indians","enemies[7].fact":"Indian warriors wore cotton clothing and shot reed arrows from bows made of reeds.","enemies[8].name":"Ethiopians","enemies[8].fact":"Ethiopians wore leopard and lion skins, and their arrowheads were made of sharp stone.","bosses[0].name":"Hydarnes","bosses[0].fact":"Hydarnes commanded the Immortals and later led them along a mountain path behind the Greeks.","bosses[1].name":"Mardonius","bosses[1].fact":"Mardonius was King Darius' son-in-law and one of his leading generals. He died at the Battle of Plataea.","bosses[2].name":"Ephialtes the Traitor","bosses[2].fact":"Ephialtes, a local man, showed the Persians a path around the pass in exchange for a reward. His name became another word for 'traitor.'","bosses[3].name":"Xerxes","bosses[3].fact":"Xerxes, the King of Kings, led an enormous army against Greece, but he lost his fleet at Salamis and returned to Asia.","chapters[0].title":"The Persians Approach","chapters[0].year":"480 BC","chapters[0].fact":"Xerxes crossed the Hellespont, marched through Thrace and Thessaly, and reached Thermopylae, where Leonidas was waiting.","chapters[1].title":"The First Day","chapters[1].year":"480 BC","chapters[1].fact":"The Medes and Cissians attacked the narrow pass for hours but could not push the Greeks back.","chapters[2].title":"The Second Day","chapters[2].year":"480 BC","chapters[2].fact":"Xerxes sent the Immortals into battle under Hydarnes, but even they were forced to retreat.","chapters[3].title":"Ephialtes' Betrayal","chapters[3].year":"480 BC","chapters[3].fact":"Ephialtes revealed the Anopaea path, and the Persians marched through the mountains at night to get around the Greek position.","chapters[4].title":"The Third Day: Come and Take Them","chapters[4].year":"480 BC","chapters[4].fact":"When Leonidas learned that the Persians were going around his position, he sent his allies away and stayed in the pass with those who chose to fight to the end.","chapters[5].title":"Salamis and Plataea","chapters[5].year":"480–479 BC","chapters[5].fact":"The Spartans died, but they bought time. The Greek fleet won at Salamis, and the Greek army won at Plataea.","waves[0].title":"Medes","waves[1].title":"The Medes Advance Like a Wall","waves[2].title":"Cissians","waves[3].title":"Archers Behind Woven Shields","waves[4].title":"Saka Horsemen","waves[5].title":"Battle for the Pass","waves[6].title":"The Narrow Pass","waves[7].title":"Arab Camels","waves[8].title":"Cavalry in the Pass","waves[9].title":"End of the First Day","waves[10].title":"The Immortals Advance","waves[11].title":"Egyptians","waves[12].title":"Arrows Block Out the Sun","waves[13].title":"Avalanche","waves[14].title":"Hydarnes and the Immortals","waves[15].title":"Indians","waves[16].title":"Egyptian Shields","waves[17].title":"Night on the Mountain Path","waves[18].title":"The Flanking Force","waves[19].title":"Mardonius","waves[20].title":"Ethiopians","waves[21].title":"Camels and Arrows","waves[22].title":"Come and Take Them","waves[23].title":"Twilight","waves[24].title":"Ephialtes Leads the Way Around","waves[25].title":"The Road to Athens","waves[26].title":"The Strait of Salamis","waves[27].title":"The Iron Wall","waves[28].title":"Plataea","waves[29].title":"King of Kings","content.waveFacts[0]":"In 480 BC, Xerxes led his army across the Hellespont on a bridge made of ships, and the Medes were the first to enter the pass.","content.waveFacts[1]":"Xerxes' army was enormous. Herodotus counted millions of warriors. Historians believe there were fewer, but still tens of thousands.","content.waveFacts[2]":"The Cissians lived in Susiana, and warriors from dozens of different peoples served in the Persian Empire.","content.waveFacts[3]":"Persian archers hid behind woven shields and showered their enemies with arrows.","content.waveFacts[4]":"Xerxes waited four days for the Greeks to run away. On the fifth day, he ordered his army to take the pass by force.","content.waveFacts[5]":"On the first day, Xerxes sent the Medes into battle and ordered them to capture the Spartans alive, but they could not break their formation.","content.waveFacts[6]":"The pass was so narrow that the Persians could not attack with their entire army, so their greater numbers did not help.","content.waveFacts[7]":"Herodotus listed Arab camel riders among Xerxes' warriors.","content.waveFacts[8]":"Saka cavalry was powerful on the open steppe, but there was no room to build up speed in the narrow pass.","content.waveFacts[9]":"By the evening of the first day, the Persians had retreated. According to stories, Xerxes kept jumping up from his throne as he watched the battle.","content.waveFacts[10]":"On the second day, Xerxes sent his best guards, the 10,000 Immortals, into battle.","content.waveFacts[11]":"Egyptians served in Xerxes' army and fought with heavy spears and swords.","content.waveFacts[12]":"The Greeks fought in turns, one unit after another, so each warrior had time to rest.","content.waveFacts[13]":"According to stories, the Spartans pretended to run away, then turned around and struck the enemies who chased them.","content.waveFacts[14]":"Hydarnes led the Immortals into battle, but even they could not break the Greek formation.","content.waveFacts[15]":"A local man named Ephialtes from Malis told Xerxes about a mountain path around the pass.","content.waveFacts[16]":"The path led through the mountains above the pass, and a thousand Phocians guarded it.","content.waveFacts[17]":"Hydarnes and the Immortals followed the path at night. According to Herodotus, the rustling of oak leaves warned the Phocians that the Persians were coming.","content.waveFacts[18]":"The Phocians retreated to a hill, while the Persians passed them and descended behind the Greeks.","content.waveFacts[19]":"Mardonius was one of Xerxes' leading generals and the son-in-law of King Darius.","content.waveFacts[20]":"Ethiopians in Xerxes' army wore leopard and lion skins, and their arrowheads were made of stone.","content.waveFacts[21]":"Before dawn, the seer Megistias and scouts told Leonidas that the Persians were going around the Greek position.","content.waveFacts[22]":"When the Persians demanded that the Greeks surrender their weapons, Leonidas reportedly answered, 'Come and take them.'","content.waveFacts[23]":"Leonidas sent almost all his allies away and kept 300 Spartans, 700 Thespians, and 400 Thebans.","content.waveFacts[24]":"Ephialtes led the Persians along the mountain path, trapping the defenders of the pass between two enemy forces.","content.waveFacts[25]":"When the Greeks' spears broke, they fought with swords and then, according to Herodotus, with their hands and teeth.","content.waveFacts[26]":"In 480 BC, near the island of Salamis, Themistocles' fleet lured the Persian ships into a narrow strait and defeated them.","content.waveFacts[27]":"After Salamis, Xerxes returned to Asia, while Mardonius stayed in Greece with the best part of the army.","content.waveFacts[28]":"In 479 BC, at Plataea, the Greeks under the Spartan commander Pausanias defeated Mardonius' army.","content.waveFacts[29]":"After losing his fleet and his best generals, Xerxes failed to conquer Greece, and the Greeks defended their freedom.","content.quiz[0].q":"Who commanded the Greeks at Thermopylae?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"King Leonidas of Sparta","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"Themistocles of Athens","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"Miltiades of Athens","content.quiz[0].explain":"King Leonidas of Sparta led the Greek army.","content.quiz[1].q":"What was a polis?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"A large ship","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"A temple on a mountain","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"A city-state","content.quiz[1].explain":"A polis was a small independent state made up of a city and the surrounding land.","content.quiz[2].q":"Which king led the Persian army into Greece in 480 BC?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Darius","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Xerxes","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Cyrus","content.quiz[2].explain":"Xerxes was the son of Darius I and wanted revenge for the Battle of Marathon.","content.quiz[3].q":"Where were the Olympic Games held?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"In Sparta","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"In Olympia","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"In Athens","content.quiz[3].explain":"The games were held in Olympia to honor Zeus, and wars were suspended during the games.","content.quiz[4].q":"Which strait did Xerxes cross on a bridge made of ships?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"The Hellespont","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"The Strait of Gibraltar","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"The Bosporus","content.quiz[4].explain":"The bridge across the Hellespont (Dardanelles) connected Asia and Europe.","content.quiz[5].q":"Who were the helots?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"Athenian sailors","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"Priests of Zeus","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Dependent farmers in Sparta","content.quiz[5].explain":"Helots worked on Spartan land and had almost no rights.","content.quiz[6].q":"How many Spartans were in Leonidas' force?","content.quiz[6].explain":"There were several thousand Greeks including the allies, but only 300 Spartans.","content.quiz[7].q":"Who, according to tradition, wrote the Iliad and the Odyssey?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Herodotus","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Homer","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Solon","content.quiz[7].explain":"The Greeks memorized Homer's poems.","content.quiz[8].q":"What did Leonidas say when the Persians demanded that the Greeks surrender their weapons?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"Come and take them.","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"We surrender.","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"Wait until tomorrow.","content.quiz[8].explain":"These words were recorded by the historian Plutarch.","content.quiz[9].q":"At what age did Spartan boys begin their state-run military education?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"At age 7","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"At age 3","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"At age 15","content.quiz[9].explain":"The Spartan education system was called the agoge.","content.quiz[10].q":"What were the Persian king's 10,000 best warriors called?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"Hoplites","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"Peltasts","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"Immortals","content.quiz[10].explain":"When a warrior was killed, another immediately replaced him, so their number never changed.","content.quiz[11].q":"What was a heavily armed Greek foot soldier called?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Legionary","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Hoplite","content.quiz[11].explain":"The name came from the hoplon, a large round shield.","content.quiz[12].q":"What did Persian archers hide behind?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Bronze breastplates","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Woven shields","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Stone walls","content.quiz[12].explain":"Woven shields were light but provided good protection against arrows.","content.quiz[13].q":"What was a phalanx?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"A tight formation of warriors standing shield to shield","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"A fast warship","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"A Greek fortress","content.quiz[13].explain":"In a phalanx, each warrior used his shield to protect both himself and the man beside him.","content.quiz[14].q":"Who showed the Persians a path around the pass?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"Demaratus","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"Hydarnes","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"Ephialtes","content.quiz[14].explain":"Ephialtes hoped to receive a reward from Xerxes.","content.quiz[15].q":"Who abolished debt slavery in Athens?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"Leonidas","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"Homer","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"Solon","content.quiz[15].explain":"Solon's reforms date to 594 BC.","content.quiz[16].q":"Where did the Athenians defeat Darius' Persians in 490 BC?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"At Salamis","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"At Marathon","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"At Plataea","content.quiz[16].explain":"Miltiades commanded the Athenian army at Marathon.","content.quiz[17].q":"What was the assembly of citizens in Athens called?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"Assembly of the People","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"Council of Elders","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"Senate","content.quiz[17].explain":"Citizens decided questions of war and peace by raising their hands to vote.","content.quiz[18].q":"Who convinced the Athenians to build a powerful fleet?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"Themistocles","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"Pericles","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"Solon","content.quiz[18].explain":"The fleet built on Themistocles' advice won the Battle of Salamis.","content.quiz[19].q":"Which of these states had two kings ruling at the same time?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"Athens","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"Persia","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"Sparta","content.quiz[19].explain":"Sparta's two kings were supervised by the elders and ephors.","content.quiz[20].q":"Where did the Greek fleet defeat the Persians in 480 BC?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"At Marathon","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"Near the island of Salamis","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"At Thermopylae","content.quiz[20].explain":"In the narrow strait, the large Persian ships got in each other's way.","content.quiz[21].q":"What does the word 'democracy' mean?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"Rule by a king","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"Rule by the people","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"Rule by the wealthy","content.quiz[21].explain":"The word comes from Greek: demos means 'people,' and kratos means 'power.'","content.quiz[22].q":"What did the warrior Dienekes say about the clouds of Persian arrows?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Then we will fight in the shade.","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"We need new shields.","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"We must retreat.","content.quiz[22].explain":"That is how Herodotus recorded the Spartan joke.","content.quiz[23].q":"Which god did the Greeks honor with the Olympic Games?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"Apollo","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"Ares","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"Zeus","content.quiz[23].explain":"The games were held near the Temple of Zeus in Olympia.","content.quiz[24].q":"Who was the Persian king at the Battle of Marathon?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Xerxes","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"Cyrus","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Darius","content.quiz[24].explain":"Darius I was Xerxes' father.","content.quiz[25].q":"What did full citizens of Sparta mainly do?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"Trade and crafts","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"Military service","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Sailing","content.quiz[25].explain":"Helots farmed the land, while perioikoi worked as craftsmen.","content.quiz[26].q":"What formation helped the Greeks hold the narrow pass?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Phalanx","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Column","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Cavalry wedge","content.quiz[26].explain":"The tightly packed phalanx was especially strong in the narrow pass.","content.quiz[27].q":"What did the Greeks establish along the shores of the Mediterranean and Black Seas?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"Colonies","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"Frontier forts","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Monasteries","content.quiz[27].explain":"Colonies were new Greek cities established across the sea.","content.quiz[28].q":"Who commanded the Greeks at the Battle of Plataea in 479 BC?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"Leonidas","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"Themistocles","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"Pausanias of Sparta","content.quiz[28].explain":"The Persian commander Mardonius also died at Plataea.","content.quiz[29].q":"What helped the Greeks win the Greco-Persian Wars?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"Help from Roman legions","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"Unity among the cities, a strong fleet, and determined infantry","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"Water from mountain rivers","content.quiz[29].explain":"The Greeks set aside their disagreements and united. Courage, their fleet, and skilled commanders all played important roles.","content.events[0].title":"Where Should We Face the Enemy?","content.events[0].text":"The Persians are marching through Thessaly. Many Greeks want to retreat south and defend only the Peloponnese. Leonidas must decide where to stop Xerxes.","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Take positions in the narrow pass of Thermopylae","content.events[0].options[0].result":"That is what Leonidas did. In the narrow pass between the sea and the mountains, the Persians could not use their advantage in numbers.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Retreat and wait for the enemy in the south","content.events[0].options[1].result":"Then all of central Greece would fall to the Persians without a fight. Leonidas decided to make his stand at Thermopylae, the Hot Gates.","content.events[1].title":"How Should We Hold the Line?","content.events[1].text":"The battle has been going on for hours. The warriors are getting tired, while the Persians keep bringing in fresh troops. What should the Greeks do?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Rotate the units and let them rest","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That is what happened. The units took turns fighting, and according to stories, the Spartans even pretended to retreat to lure the enemy forward.","content.events[1].options[1].label":"Keep everyone in formation without a break","content.events[1].options[1].result":"The exhausted formation would eventually collapse. The Greeks fought in turns to save their strength.","content.events[2].title":"Who Will Guard the Mountain Path?","content.events[2].text":"Above the pass lies the Anopaea mountain path. If the Persians use it, the Greeks will be surrounded.","content.events[2].options[0].label":"Station one thousand Phocians on the path","content.events[2].options[0].result":"That is what Leonidas did, but the Phocians could not hold their position when the Immortals attacked.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Leave the path unguarded","content.events[2].options[1].result":"That would be far too dangerous. Leonidas stationed the Phocians on the path, although after the betrayal, even that was not enough.","content.events[3].title":"Leonidas' War Council","content.events[3].text":"Scouts report that the Persians are using the mountain path to get around the Greeks. What should Leonidas do?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Send the allies away, but stay behind to delay the enemy","content.events[3].options[0].result":"That is what he did. Most of the allies left to continue the fight, while the Spartans and Thespians stayed behind to cover their retreat.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Everyone should run away together and abandon the pass","content.events[3].options[1].result":"Then the Persian cavalry could catch the fleeing Greeks. Leonidas stayed behind to give the others time to escape.","content.events[4].title":"The Demand to Surrender Our Weapons","content.events[4].text":"Xerxes sends a messenger: 'Lay down your weapons.' What will Leonidas answer?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Come and take them.","content.events[4].options[0].result":"Lay down our weapons to save our lives","content.events[4].options[1].label":"According to tradition, Leonidas spoke these words, which became a symbol of courage.","content.events[4].options[1].result":"The Spartans would never do that. Being captured after surrendering was considered shameful.","content.events[5].title":"The Final Battle in the Pass","content.events[5].text":"The Greeks are surrounded. Where should they make their last stand?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Move into a wider area and inflict as many losses as possible","content.events[5].options[0].result":"That is what happened. The Greeks moved out of the narrowest part of the pass and killed many Persians before falling themselves.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Hide behind the wall and wait","content.events[5].options[1].result":"The wall could not protect them from an attack from behind. The Greeks decided to fight to the death and inflict as many losses as possible.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Thespians","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"Thespiae was a small city in Boeotia. Seven hundred of its citizens volunteered to join Leonidas, even though they had not been ordered to. They were lightly equipped hoplites, but they stood alongside the Spartans and remained in the pass until the very end. The Spartans later held their courage in high honor.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Peltasts","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"Peltasts were light infantry soldiers named after their small shields, called peltae. They threw javelins, ran quickly, and could defend themselves against cavalry. Peltasts could not fight in a phalanx, but they were useful on slopes and flanks.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Spartans","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"Spartans trained for war from childhood and lived by strict rules. Every hoplite carried a bronze helmet, breastplate, and round shield. Their red cloaks and long hair became their symbols. The king's guard was chosen from Spartan warriors, and 300 of them marched with Leonidas.","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"Cretans were the best archers in Greece, and cities hired them for pay. Their bows were short, but they were accurate. Archers stood behind the hoplites and struck enemies from a distance before they could get close.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Rock Throwers","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"The pass at Thermopylae was squeezed between the sea and steep cliffs, so warriors could drop rocks onto their enemies. This was not a machine, just the strength of their arms and heavy boulders. A shower of rocks could strike a crowd, and armor offered little protection.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Shield Wall","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"A hoplite's shield, called a hoplon, was large and round. A warrior held it with his left arm, protecting himself and the man to his left. When warriors stood shoulder to shoulder, they formed a solid wall that was very difficult to break.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Medes","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"Media was a land in northwestern Iran. It was once a powerful kingdom before Cyrus conquered it, but the Medes remained an important part of the Persian army. They wore soft caps and colorful clothing and fought with spears and bows.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Cissians","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"The Cissians lived in the region of Cissia near Susa, one of Persia's capitals. Herodotus wrote that they dressed like Persians but wore headbands. At Thermopylae, they fought alongside the Medes on the first day of battle.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Persian Archers","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"The Persians were famous for their skill with bows. Before battle, they planted woven shields in the ground and fired from behind them. Persian arrows were light and flew in such great numbers that the Greeks said they blocked out the sun.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Saka","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"The Saka were nomadic tribes from Central Asia and excellent horsemen. They shot bows while riding and fought with battle-axes. Their cavalry was less effective in the narrow pass at Thermopylae because they needed open space to attack.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Arab Camel Riders","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"Arabs served in Xerxes' army and rode camels. Camels could endure heat and long journeys, making these units useful on distant campaigns. Herodotus mentioned them among Xerxes' warriors.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"Immortals","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"The Immortals were 10,000 elite warriors of the Persian king. Whenever someone died or became ill, another soldier immediately took his place, so their number stayed the same. They wore embroidered clothing and scale armor, and their spears had gold or silver balls at the ends.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Egyptians","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"Egypt was part of the Persian Empire at that time. Egyptian warriors wore linen armor, carried large shields, and fought with heavy spears. Herodotus wrote that the Egyptians also distinguished themselves in naval battles.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Indians","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"The region along the Indus River was part of Darius' empire. Indian warriors wore cotton clothing and shot arrows from bows made of reeds. They were fairly lightly equipped infantry.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Ethiopians","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"The Greeks called the people living south of Egypt Ethiopians. Herodotus wrote that they wore leopard and lion skins, painted their bodies, and shot arrows with stone tips.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Hydarnes","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Hydarnes was one of the highest-ranking Persian nobles and commanded the Immortals. He led them into battle on the second day and later guided them along a mountain path behind the Greeks. His descendants remained powerful nobles for many years.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Mardonius","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Mardonius, Darius' son-in-law, was one of Persia's most experienced generals. He advised Xerxes to invade Greece. After Salamis, he stayed in Greece with a large army. In summer 479 BC, the Greeks defeated him at Plataea, where he was killed.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Ephialtes the Traitor","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"Ephialtes came from Malis, a region near Thermopylae. He knew the Anopaea mountain path and showed it to the Persians in exchange for a reward. Later, the Greeks offered a reward for his capture. His name is still associated with betrayal.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Xerxes","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Xerxes I was the king of Persia and the son of Darius. In 480 BC, he invaded Greece with an enormous army and fleet. He captured and burned Athens but lost his fleet at Salamis and returned to Asia. He was later killed by conspirators.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Leonidas","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Leonidas was the king of Sparta. He brought 300 Spartans to Thermopylae and commanded several thousand Greek warriors in total. When the Persians went around his position, he sent his allies away and stayed in the pass. Leonidas died in battle, and the Greeks honored him as a hero.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Xerxes (Biography)","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Xerxes ruled Persia from 486 to 465 BC and was the son of Darius I. He put down rebellions in Egypt and Babylon, then decided to conquer Greece. His campaign in 480 BC failed, but Persia remained a powerful empire.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Themistocles","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Themistocles was an Athenian general and politician. He persuaded the Athenians to spend the money from their silver mines on a fleet. Under his command, Greek ships lured the Persians into the narrow strait near Salamis and defeated them.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Ephialtes","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Ephialtes was a man from Malis who betrayed the Greeks to their enemy. Herodotus wrote that he hoped to receive a reward from Xerxes. The Greeks remembered his name as that of a traitor.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Demaratus","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"Demaratus had been a king of Sparta, but he lost his throne and fled to Persia. Xerxes welcomed him, and Demaratus told the king about the Spartans. He warned that they would not retreat, no matter how few of them there were.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"The Persians Approach","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"In 480 BC, Xerxes gathered an army from dozens of nations. It crossed the Hellespont on a bridge made of ships, then marched through Thrace and Macedonia. In Thessaly, Xerxes met messengers from cities that had surrendered. Ahead lay the road south through Thermopylae.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"The First Day","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"Xerxes waited four days for the Greeks to leave. Then he sent the Medes and Cissians to capture the Greeks alive. But in the narrow pass, the Greeks defeated the Persians one after another and refused to retreat. By evening, the Persians had suffered heavy losses.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"The Second Day","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"Xerxes decided that the Medes were simply too weak and sent in the Immortals. But even they could not break through. The Greek units took turns fighting, while the Spartans pretended to retreat before turning around to attack. According to stories, Xerxes jumped up from his throne several times.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"Ephialtes' Betrayal","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"On the night before the third day, Ephialtes showed the Persians the Anopaea mountain path. Hydarnes led the Immortals along it. The thousand Phocians guarding the path could not stop them. By morning, Leonidas learned that the Persians had gone around his position.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"The Third Day: Come and Take Them","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"Leonidas sent almost all his allies away. The Spartans, Thespians, and Thebans remained. The Greeks moved into a wider area and fought to the end. When their spears broke, they fought with swords, hands, and teeth. Leonidas fell in battle, and the remaining warriors died defending his body.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"Salamis and Plataea","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"The Spartans died, but they delayed the Persians for three days and gave the Greeks more time. The Persians captured Athens, but the Athenians had already evacuated their people by ship. In September 480 BC, Themistocles defeated the Persian fleet at Salamis. In 479 BC, the Greeks defeated Mardonius' army at Plataea.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"Spartan Education","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"In Sparta, the state took charge of boys when they turned seven. They lived in groups, slept on reeds, walked barefoot, and learned to endure hunger and cold. This education system was called the agoge. It trained boys to become warriors who could endure pain and obey their commanders.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"The Phalanx and the Hoplite","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"A hoplite wore a helmet, breastplate, shin guards, and a large round shield. He carried a spear about two meters long. Hoplites stood in several rows. The rear ranks pushed forward and supported the front ranks. This formation was difficult to break as long as the warriors stayed organized.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"The Persian Empire","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"The Achaemenid Empire was the largest empire in the world at that time. It stretched from Egypt to India. The king divided the empire into provinces called satrapies, each governed by an official called a satrap. Warriors from dozens of nations served in the army, making it enormous but full of people who spoke different languages.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"The Pass of Thermopylae","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"Thermopylae means 'Hot Gates' because there were hot springs nearby. In 480 BC, the pass was a narrow strip of land between Mount Kallidromon and the sea. In some places, it was only a few meters wide. A large army could not spread out there. Since then, Thermopylae has become a symbol of courage and determination.","content.encyclopedia[34].title":"What Happened Next","content.encyclopedia[34].text":"After Thermopylae, the Persians reached Athens and burned the city. But in September 480 BC, the Greek fleet won at Salamis, and in 479 BC, the Greek army defeated Mardonius at Plataea. Xerxes never invaded Greece again. A monument to the fallen warriors at Thermopylae carried these words: 'Go tell the Spartans, traveler passing by, that here, obeying their laws, we lie.'"},"moscow1612":{"title":"Moscow 1612","year":"1612 AD","side":"The Second People's Militia of Minin and Pozharsky","foe":"The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth's Army and Hetman Chodkiewicz","intro[0]":"A Polish garrison has occupied the Kremlin for two years, and Russia is in the Time of Troubles.","intro[1]":"Kuzma Minin, a leader from Nizhny Novgorod, and Prince Dmitry Pozharsky gathered the Second People's Militia and approached Moscow in August 1612.","intro[2]":"Hetman Chodkiewicz is coming from the west to rescue the garrison. Hold your defenses and stop him from reaching the Kremlin.","victoryText":"Hetman Chodkiewicz left Moscow empty-handed. In October 1612, the militia captured Kitay-gorod, and the Polish garrison in the Kremlin surrendered. In 1613, the Zemsky Sobor elected Mikhail Romanov as tsar, and the Time of Troubles began to end.","defeatText":"The hetman broke through to the Kremlin! But in history, the militia held its ground, so try again.","theme.spawnSign":"From the Mozhaysk Road","fortress.name":"MOSCOW","features[0].text":"Novodevichy Convent","features[1].text":"Moskva River","features[2].text":"Crimean Court","features[3].text":"Zamoskvorechye","defenders[0].name":"Trubetskoy's Cossacks","defenders[0].note":"Cheap and fast; effective against infantry","defenders[0].fact":"Prince Trubetskoy's Cossacks had been stationed outside Moscow since the First People's Militia. On August 24, they recaptured a small fort near St. Clement's Church.","defenders[1].name":"Streltsy","defenders[1].note":"Main defensive force; bullets pierce armor","defenders[1].fact":"The streltsy were permanent infantry troops armed with firearms called pishchals. Ivan the Terrible established them in 1550.","defenders[2].name":"Noble Cavalry","defenders[2].note":"Powerful charge; effective against cavalry","defenders[2].fact":"Noblemen served as mounted warriors in exchange for land grants. Their cavalry companies were the main striking force of Pozharsky's militia.","defenders[3].name":"Archers with Saadaks","defenders[3].note":"Shoot far and frequently","defenders[3].fact":"A saadak was a set consisting of a bow in its case and a quiver of arrows. In the early 17th century, many Russian warriors still used bows.","defenders[4].name":"Artillerymen","defenders[4].note":"Cannonballs strike from far away and damage an area","defenders[4].fact":"Artillerymen fired iron and stone cannonballs. In fall 1612, the militia bombarded Kitay-gorod and the Kremlin with cannons.","defenders[5].name":"Mobile Wooden Fortress","defenders[5].note":"Does not attack, but delays enemies for 1.5 seconds","defenders[5].fact":"A gulyay-gorod was a mobile fortress made of thick wooden shields mounted on wagons. Russian armies used these defenses in the 16th and 17th centuries, including at the Battle of Molodi in 1572.","locked[0].name":"Tank","locked[0].line":"Tanks will not appear for another 300 years, during World War I","locked[1].name":"Airplane","locked[1].line":"The first airplane will not take flight until 1903","locked[2].name":"Bayonet","locked[2].line":"A blade attached to a gun will not be invented in France for several more decades","locked[3].name":"New-Formation Regiments","locked[3].line":"European-style infantry regiments will be established under Tsar Mikhail around 1630","abilities[0].name":"Minin's Charge","abilities[0].fact":"On August 24, Kuzma Minin crossed the Moskva River near the Crimean Court with three companies of noble cavalry and launched a surprise attack.","abilities[1].name":"Avraamy Palitsyn's Appeal","abilities[1].fact":"According to Avraamy Palitsyn, the cellarer of the Trinity-Sergius Monastery, he persuaded the Cossacks who had left the battlefield to return.","enemies[0].name":"Paholiks","enemies[0].fact":"Paholiks were servants and squires of Polish noblemen. They also took up weapons in battle.","enemies[1].name":"Zholners","enemies[1].fact":"In the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, zholners were soldiers who served for pay. The Kremlin garrison included them.","enemies[2].name":"Hungarian Hajduks","enemies[2].fact":"Hajduks were Hungarian infantrymen armed with guns and sabers. Polish kings and hetmans often hired these marksmen.","enemies[3].name":"Zaporozhian Cossacks (Cherkasy)","enemies[3].fact":"In Moscow, Zaporozhian and Dnieper Cossacks were called Cherkasy. Many came to Moscow with the hetman.","enemies[4].name":"Pyatigortsy","enemies[4].fact":"Pyatigortsy were light cavalrymen in the Polish army who carried spears and bows. Their name is said to come from the Pyatigorye region in the Caucasus.","enemies[5].name":"German Mercenaries","enemies[5].fact":"The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth employed mercenary infantry from German lands, including musketeers and pikemen wearing helmets and breastplates.","enemies[6].name":"Winged Hussars","enemies[6].fact":"Polish hussars were heavy cavalrymen armed with long lances. In 1610, they defeated a Russian-Swedish army at the Battle of Klushino.","enemies[7].name":"Hetman's Supply Convoy","enemies[7].fact":"The hetman was bringing hundreds of wagons filled with bread and gunpowder to the Kremlin. The Polish garrison there was already starving.","enemies[8].name":"Rotmistrs","enemies[8].fact":"A rotmistr commanded a cavalry company, or banner, in the Polish army. The best fighters stayed close to him.","bosses[0].name":"Alexander Gonsevsky","bosses[0].fact":"Gonsevsky commanded the Polish garrison in Moscow from 1610. During his command, much of the city burned in the uprising of 1611. He left Moscow in 1612.","bosses[1].name":"Colonel Nikolai Strus","bosses[1].fact":"Colonel Nikolai Strus commanded the Polish garrison in Moscow in 1612. That fall, he surrendered to the militia and was taken prisoner.","bosses[2].name":"Hajduk Commander","bosses[2].fact":"According to some accounts, on the night of August 23, a traitor named Grigory Orlov secretly led a unit of the hetman's infantry into Zamoskvorechye. Its commander's name is unknown, so he is identified by his rank.","bosses[3].name":"Hetman Jan Karol Chodkiewicz","bosses[3].fact":"Jan Karol Chodkiewicz was the Grand Hetman of Lithuania and a famous commander of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. The hetman's symbol of authority was a ceremonial mace.","chapters[0].title":"The First People's Militia","chapters[0].year":"1611 AD","chapters[0].fact":"In early 1611, Prokopy Lyapunov, a military governor from Ryazan, gathered the First People's Militia to free Moscow from the Polish garrison.","chapters[1].title":"Minin's Appeal","chapters[1].year":"Fall 1611","chapters[1].fact":"In fall 1611, Kuzma Minin, a local leader in Nizhny Novgorod, called on the townspeople to raise money and form an army.","chapters[2].title":"Yaroslavl","chapters[2].year":"Spring–Summer 1612","chapters[2].fact":"From April to July 1612, the Second People's Militia stayed in Yaroslavl, gathered strength, and established the Council of All the Land.","chapters[3].title":"Approaching Moscow","chapters[3].year":"August 1612","chapters[3].fact":"On August 20, 1612, the militia approached Moscow. The next day, Hetman Chodkiewicz reached Poklonnaya Hill.","chapters[4].title":"Battle near Novodevichy Convent","chapters[4].year":"August 22, 1612","chapters[4].fact":"On August 22, the hetman crossed the Moskva River near Novodevichy Convent and fought the militia until evening.","chapters[5].title":"Zamoskvorechye","chapters[5].year":"August 24, 1612","chapters[5].fact":"On August 24, Moscow's fate was decided in Zamoskvorechye. Minin's charge near the Crimean Court forced the hetman to retreat.","waves[0].title":"Paholiks","waves[1].title":"The Kremlin Garrison","waves[2].title":"Zholners","waves[3].title":"Cherkasy","waves[4].title":"The Burning of Moscow","waves[5].title":"Pyatigortsy","waves[6].title":"Hajduks","waves[7].title":"Supply Convoy","waves[8].title":"Hussars","waves[9].title":"Wagons to the Kremlin","waves[10].title":"Mercenaries","waves[11].title":"Mobile Detachments","waves[12].title":"Rotmistrs","waves[13].title":"Roads to Yaroslavl","waves[14].title":"Gonsevsky","waves[15].title":"Poklonnaya Hill","waves[16].title":"Hetman's Infantry","waves[17].title":"Sortie from the Kremlin","waves[18].title":"Winged Cavalry","waves[19].title":"Strus","waves[20].title":"Crossing near the Convent","waves[21].title":"Chertolye Gate","waves[22].title":"Flank Attack","waves[23].title":"The Hetman's Assault","waves[24].title":"Nighttime Flanking Maneuver","waves[25].title":"The Fort near St. Clement's","waves[26].title":"The Cossacks Return","waves[27].title":"Hetman's Supply Convoy","waves[28].title":"Near the Crimean Court","waves[29].title":"Chodkiewicz","content.waveFacts[0]":"In the Polish army, paholiks were servants and squires of noblemen. They also took up weapons in battle.","content.waveFacts[1]":"A Polish garrison had occupied the Kremlin since fall 1610, and the people of Moscow watched the foreign soldiers with fear.","content.waveFacts[2]":"Zholners were soldiers of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth who served for pay.","content.waveFacts[3]":"On March 19, 1611, an uprising broke out in Moscow. While fighting back, the garrison set the city on fire.","content.waveFacts[4]":"Prince Dmitry Pozharsky fought the enemy on Sretenka Street. He was badly wounded and carried out of burning Moscow.","content.waveFacts[5]":"In summer 1611, Cossacks killed Prokopy Lyapunov, weakening the First People's Militia.","content.waveFacts[6]":"In fall 1611, Kuzma Minin, a local leader in Nizhny Novgorod, called on the townspeople to spare nothing to save their homeland.","content.waveFacts[7]":"According to accounts, Minin was the first to donate most of his property to the army, and others followed his example.","content.waveFacts[8]":"The people of Nizhny Novgorod invited Prince Pozharsky, who was recovering from his wounds at his Mugreyevo estate, to command the army.","content.waveFacts[9]":"Money for the militia was collected from all the townspeople. According to tradition, everyone contributed one-fifth of their property.","content.waveFacts[10]":"In late winter 1612, the militia left Nizhny Novgorod and marched up the Volga River.","content.waveFacts[11]":"Along the way, people from Balakhna, Yuryevets, Kineshma, and Kostroma joined the army.","content.waveFacts[12]":"In early April 1612, the militia entered Yaroslavl and stayed there for about four months.","content.waveFacts[13]":"In Yaroslavl, the Council of All the Land was established as a temporary government. It sent official letters to towns and minted coins.","content.waveFacts[14]":"While the militia stayed in Yaroslavl, Gonsevsky left Moscow, and Nikolai Strus took command of the garrison.","content.waveFacts[15]":"When the militia learned that Hetman Chodkiewicz was coming to rescue the garrison, it left Yaroslavl for Moscow in late July 1612.","content.waveFacts[16]":"On the way, the army stopped at the Trinity-Sergius Monastery and reached Moscow on August 20.","content.waveFacts[17]":"Pozharsky took a position near the Arbat Gate, blocking the hetman's route. Trubetskoy's Cossacks were stationed to the east, near the Yauza River.","content.waveFacts[18]":"The hetman brought hussars, the finest heavy cavalry of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.","content.waveFacts[19]":"On August 21, Chodkiewicz's army reached Poklonnaya Hill, while Strus's starving garrison waited for him in the Kremlin.","content.waveFacts[20]":"On August 22, the hetman crossed the Moskva River near Novodevichy Convent and attacked the militia.","content.waveFacts[21]":"That same day, the garrison launched a sortie from the Kremlin to trap the militia between two attacking forces.","content.waveFacts[22]":"Cavalry companies assigned to Trubetskoy and some of his Cossacks attacked the hetman's flank without orders.","content.waveFacts[23]":"The battle near the Chertolye and Arbat Gates continued until evening, when the hetman withdrew.","content.waveFacts[24]":"According to some accounts, on the night of August 23, a traitor named Grigory Orlov secretly led a unit of the hetman's infantry into Zamoskvorechye.","content.waveFacts[25]":"On August 24, the hetman advanced through Zamoskvorechye and captured a small fort near St. Clement's Church, but the Cossacks recaptured it.","content.waveFacts[26]":"The Cossacks, upset by their poverty, had left the battlefield, but the cellarer Avraamy Palitsyn persuaded them to return.","content.waveFacts[27]":"The hetman tried to bring a supply convoy into the Kremlin, but according to some accounts, many wagons fell into the militia's hands.","content.waveFacts[28]":"Toward evening, Kuzma Minin crossed the river near the Crimean Court with three companies of noble cavalry and attacked the enemy.","content.waveFacts[29]":"Chodkiewicz retreated toward Donskoy Monastery. On August 25, he left Moscow without rescuing the garrison.","content.quiz[0].q":"What is the name of the troubled period in Russian history in the early 17th century?","content.quiz[0].options[0]":"The Time of Troubles","content.quiz[0].options[1]":"The Oprichnina","content.quiz[0].options[2]":"The Mongol Yoke","content.quiz[0].explain":"The Time of Troubles was a period of famine, impostors, and foreign invasions in the late 16th and early 17th centuries.","content.quiz[1].q":"Which dynasty ended in 1598 with the death of Tsar Fyodor Ivanovich?","content.quiz[1].options[0]":"The Romanovs","content.quiz[1].options[1]":"The Rurikids","content.quiz[1].options[2]":"The Godunovs","content.quiz[1].explain":"Fyodor Ivanovich, the son of Ivan the Terrible, was the last tsar from the Moscow branch of the Rurikid dynasty.","content.quiz[2].q":"Whom did the Zemsky Sobor elect as tsar in 1598?","content.quiz[2].options[0]":"Vasily Shuisky","content.quiz[2].options[1]":"Mikhail Romanov","content.quiz[2].options[2]":"Boris Godunov","content.quiz[2].explain":"Boris Godunov ruled from 1598 to 1605. A terrible famine began during his reign.","content.quiz[3].q":"Who did the impostor who seized Moscow in 1605 claim to be?","content.quiz[3].options[0]":"Tsarevich Dmitry, son of Ivan the Terrible","content.quiz[3].options[1]":"Tsarevich Fyodor","content.quiz[3].options[2]":"Prince Vladimir Staritsky","content.quiz[3].explain":"He was False Dmitry I. Godunov's government declared that he was a runaway monk named Grigory Otrepyev.","content.quiz[4].q":"What was the government of seven boyars in 1610 called?","content.quiz[4].options[0]":"Chosen Council","content.quiz[4].options[1]":"Rule of the Seven Boyars","content.quiz[4].options[2]":"Oprichnina","content.quiz[4].explain":"After Vasily Shuisky was overthrown, seven boyars took power. This period was called the Rule of the Seven Boyars.","content.quiz[5].q":"Whom did the Seven Boyars invite to become tsar of Russia in 1610?","content.quiz[5].options[0]":"The King of Sweden","content.quiz[5].options[1]":"False Dmitry II","content.quiz[5].options[2]":"Polish Prince Władysław","content.quiz[5].explain":"The boyars recognized Władysław, son of King Sigismund III, as tsar and allowed a Polish garrison into Moscow.","content.quiz[6].q":"Who was Kuzma Minin?","content.quiz[6].options[0]":"An elected local leader from Nizhny Novgorod","content.quiz[6].options[1]":"A Moscow boyar","content.quiz[6].options[2]":"A patriarch","content.quiz[6].explain":"Minin was a townsman and merchant. The townspeople elected him as their local leader.","content.quiz[7].q":"In which city did the Second People's Militia begin forming?","content.quiz[7].options[0]":"Yaroslavl","content.quiz[7].options[1]":"Nizhny Novgorod","content.quiz[7].options[2]":"Smolensk","content.quiz[7].explain":"In fall 1611, Minin rallied the people of Nizhny Novgorod, and the militia began forming there.","content.quiz[8].q":"Which patriarch called on the people to rise against the invaders and died in captivity?","content.quiz[8].options[0]":"Nikon","content.quiz[8].options[1]":"Filaret","content.quiz[8].options[2]":"Hermogenes","content.quiz[8].explain":"Patriarch Hermogenes died in February 1612 at Chudov Monastery, where the Poles and boyars had imprisoned him.","content.quiz[9].q":"Who led the First People's Militia in 1611?","content.quiz[9].options[0]":"Prokopy Lyapunov","content.quiz[9].options[1]":"Kuzma Minin","content.quiz[9].options[2]":"Mikhail Skopin-Shuisky","content.quiz[9].explain":"Lyapunov led the militia alongside Prince Trubetskoy and Cossack leader Zarutsky, but he was killed in summer 1611.","content.quiz[10].q":"How long did Smolensk withstand the siege by King Sigismund III?","content.quiz[10].options[0]":"About one month","content.quiz[10].options[1]":"Almost two years","content.quiz[10].options[2]":"Ten years","content.quiz[10].explain":"Military governor Mikhail Shein defended Smolensk from fall 1609 until summer 1611.","content.quiz[11].q":"Which city did the Swedes capture in 1611?","content.quiz[11].options[0]":"Pskov","content.quiz[11].options[1]":"Kazan","content.quiz[11].options[2]":"Novgorod","content.quiz[11].explain":"In summer 1611, the Swedes occupied Veliky Novgorod. They did not return it to Russia until 1617.","content.quiz[12].q":"Where did the Second People's Militia gather its strength in spring 1612?","content.quiz[12].options[0]":"Yaroslavl","content.quiz[12].options[1]":"Tver","content.quiz[12].options[2]":"Ryazan","content.quiz[12].explain":"The militia stayed in Yaroslavl for about four months and grew into a large army.","content.quiz[13].q":"What was the governing body established by the militia in Yaroslavl called?","content.quiz[13].options[0]":"Chosen Council","content.quiz[13].options[1]":"Council of All the Land","content.quiz[13].options[2]":"Boyar Duma","content.quiz[13].explain":"The Council of All the Land governed on behalf of the entire country while the invaders occupied Moscow.","content.quiz[14].q":"Who commanded the Polish garrison in Moscow in 1610–1611?","content.quiz[14].options[0]":"Jan Karol Chodkiewicz","content.quiz[14].options[1]":"Sigismund III","content.quiz[14].options[2]":"Alexander Gonsevsky","content.quiz[14].explain":"Gonsevsky governed Moscow on behalf of the king until he left the city in 1612.","content.quiz[15].q":"From which direction did Hetman Chodkiewicz approach Moscow?","content.quiz[15].options[0]":"From the west, along the Mozhaysk Road","content.quiz[15].options[1]":"From the south, from Crimea","content.quiz[15].options[2]":"From the north, near Vologda","content.quiz[15].explain":"The hetman approached from Vyazma and Mozhaysk, reaching Poklonnaya Hill on August 21.","content.quiz[16].q":"Near which gates did Pozharsky's militia take its position?","content.quiz[16].options[0]":"Spassky Gate","content.quiz[16].options[1]":"Arbat Gate","content.quiz[16].options[2]":"Golden Gate","content.quiz[16].explain":"Pozharsky took his position near the Arbat Gate on the western side, where the hetman was expected to arrive.","content.quiz[17].q":"Who commanded the Cossacks outside Moscow in 1612?","content.quiz[17].options[0]":"Ivan Bolotnikov","content.quiz[17].options[1]":"Yermak","content.quiz[17].options[2]":"Dmitry Trubetskoy","content.quiz[17].explain":"Prince Dmitry Trubetskoy had been stationed outside Moscow with his Cossacks since the First People's Militia.","content.quiz[18].q":"What was a gulyay-gorod?","content.quiz[18].options[0]":"A mobile fortress made of shields mounted on wagons","content.quiz[18].options[1]":"A marketplace","content.quiz[18].options[2]":"A wooden bridge","content.quiz[18].explain":"A gulyay-gorod was assembled from thick wooden shields right on the battlefield. Soldiers fired from behind them as if they were walls.","content.quiz[19].q":"Who were the streltsy?","content.quiz[19].options[0]":"Horsemen of the Horde","content.quiz[19].options[1]":"Full-time infantry soldiers armed with firearms","content.quiz[19].options[2]":"Warrior monks","content.quiz[19].explain":"Ivan the Terrible established the streltsy army in 1550.","content.quiz[20].q":"Near which monastery did the hetman cross the Moskva River on August 22?","content.quiz[20].options[0]":"Trinity-Sergius Monastery","content.quiz[20].options[1]":"Chudov Monastery","content.quiz[20].options[2]":"Novodevichy Convent","content.quiz[20].explain":"Novodevichy Convent stood near a bend in the Moskva River, west of the Kremlin.","content.quiz[21].q":"What were the light cavalrymen of the Polish army who carried spears and bows called?","content.quiz[21].options[0]":"Pyatigortsy","content.quiz[21].options[1]":"Streltsy","content.quiz[21].options[2]":"Oprichniki","content.quiz[21].explain":"The Pyatigortsy were light cavalrymen of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, while the streltsy and oprichniki were Russian.","content.quiz[22].q":"Who persuaded the Cossacks to return to battle on August 24?","content.quiz[22].options[0]":"Patriarch Hermogenes","content.quiz[22].options[1]":"Cellarer Avraamy Palitsyn","content.quiz[22].options[2]":"Prince Trubetskoy","content.quiz[22].explain":"Avraamy Palitsyn, the cellarer of Trinity-Sergius Monastery, promised the Cossacks a reward from the monastery's treasury. Hermogenes had already died by then.","content.quiz[23].q":"Where did Minin's detachment attack the enemy on August 24?","content.quiz[23].options[0]":"Near Novodevichy Convent","content.quiz[23].options[1]":"Near the Arbat Gate","content.quiz[23].options[2]":"Near the Crimean Court","content.quiz[23].explain":"Minin crossed the Moskva River near the Crimean Court, and his surprise attack sent the enemy fleeing.","content.quiz[24].q":"What did the militia capture by storm in October 1612?","content.quiz[24].options[0]":"Kitay-gorod","content.quiz[24].options[1]":"Smolensk","content.quiz[24].options[2]":"Novgorod","content.quiz[24].explain":"On October 22, 1612, the militia captured Kitay-gorod. A few days later, the Kremlin surrendered too.","content.quiz[25].q":"Which Russian holiday commemorates the liberation of Moscow in 1612?","content.quiz[25].options[0]":"Victory Day","content.quiz[25].options[1]":"National Unity Day","content.quiz[25].options[2]":"Russia Day","content.quiz[25].explain":"National Unity Day has been celebrated on November 4 since 2005.","content.quiz[26].q":"Whom did the Zemsky Sobor elect as tsar in 1613?","content.quiz[26].options[0]":"Dmitry Pozharsky","content.quiz[26].options[1]":"Prince Władysław","content.quiz[26].options[2]":"Mikhail Fyodorovich Romanov","content.quiz[26].explain":"Sixteen-year-old Mikhail Romanov became the first tsar of the new dynasty.","content.quiz[27].q":"According to legend, which peasant led a Polish detachment deep into the forest to save the young tsar?","content.quiz[27].options[0]":"Ivan Susanin","content.quiz[27].options[1]":"Kuzma Minin","content.quiz[27].options[2]":"Ivan Bolotnikov","content.quiz[27].explain":"According to legend, Ivan Susanin, a peasant from the Kostroma region, died rather than reveal where Mikhail Romanov was hiding.","content.quiz[28].q":"Where in Moscow is the monument to Minin and Pozharsky by sculptor Ivan Martos?","content.quiz[28].options[0]":"On Arbat Street","content.quiz[28].options[1]":"On Red Square","content.quiz[28].options[2]":"Near Novodevichy Convent","content.quiz[28].explain":"The monument was unveiled in 1818. People from across the country donated money to build it.","content.quiz[29].q":"Under which agreement of 1618 did Smolensk remain part of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth?","content.quiz[29].options[0]":"Treaty of Stolbovo","content.quiz[29].options[1]":"Treaty of Nystad","content.quiz[29].options[2]":"Truce of Deulino","content.quiz[29].explain":"The Truce of Deulino ended the war with the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. The Treaty of Stolbovo was signed with Sweden in 1617.","content.events[0].title":"The Patriarch's Letter","content.events[0].text":"Gonsevsky and the boyars demand that Patriarch Hermogenes write to the militia, telling its members to go home and recognize Prince Władysław as tsar. How should the patriarch respond?","content.events[0].options[0].label":"Write the letter to save himself","content.events[0].options[0].result":"The militia would lose the Church's blessing, and the invaders could rule Moscow without resistance.","content.events[0].options[1].label":"Refuse and bless the militia","content.events[0].options[1].result":"Hermogenes refused. He was imprisoned in Chudov Monastery, where he died in February 1612, but his letters continued to call on the Russians to fight.","content.events[1].title":"Whom Should We Choose as Commander?","content.events[1].text":"The money for the army has been collected, but the militia needs an experienced, honest commander who has never betrayed his country. Whom should the people of Nizhny Novgorod invite?","content.events[1].options[0].label":"Prince Pozharsky, who was wounded while defending Moscow","content.events[1].options[0].result":"That is what happened. Pozharsky agreed but asked Minin to manage the army's treasury. Minin was named 'the man elected by all the land.'","content.events[1].options[1].label":"A boyar from the Rule of the Seven Boyars","content.events[1].options[1].result":"The Seven Boyars had allowed the enemy into Moscow themselves, so the people did not trust them.","content.events[2].title":"Hurry or Gather Strength?","content.events[2].text":"The militia has arrived in Yaroslavl. Some want to march on Moscow immediately, while others advise gathering troops from all the cities first. What should we do?","content.events[2].options[0].label":"March on Moscow immediately with the troops we have","content.events[2].options[0].result":"With such a small army, we could easily repeat the fate of the First People's Militia, which fell apart outside Moscow.","content.events[2].options[1].label":"Stay in Yaroslavl and gather strength","content.events[2].options[1].result":"The militia stayed in Yaroslavl for about four months. People and money arrived from many towns, while the Council of All the Land governed the country.","content.events[3].title":"Where Should We Set Up Camp Outside Moscow?","content.events[3].text":"The militia has reached Moscow. Trubetskoy's Cossacks invite us to join their camp near the Yauza River. But the hetman is coming from the west. Where should we set up camp?","content.events[3].options[0].label":"Join the Cossacks at their camp near the Yauza River","content.events[3].options[0].result":"Prokopy Lyapunov was killed there, and the militiamen did not trust the Cossacks. The western road to the Kremlin would also be left unguarded.","content.events[3].options[1].label":"Set up a separate camp near the Arbat Gate, in the hetman's path","content.events[3].options[1].result":"That is what Pozharsky did. He took a position to the west, where Chodkiewicz was expected to pass, and ordered his men to dig trenches.","content.events[4].title":"No Orders! What Should We Do?","content.events[4].text":"On August 22, the hetman is pushing back the militia. The cavalry companies Pozharsky assigned to Trubetskoy are standing idle across the river because Trubetskoy is hesitating. What should their commanders do?","content.events[4].options[0].label":"Wait for Trubetskoy's orders","content.events[4].options[0].result":"While they waited for orders, the hetman might have broken through to the Kremlin.","content.events[4].options[1].label":"Go help Pozharsky on their own","content.events[4].options[1].result":"The cavalry companies and some of the Cossacks crossed the river on their own and attacked the hetman's flank. They stopped his advance, and Chodkiewicz withdrew.","content.events[5].title":"Minin's Request","content.events[5].text":"By the evening of August 24, both sides are exhausted. Minin asks Pozharsky for soldiers so he can cross the river and attack the enemy near the Crimean Court. Should we give him troops?","content.events[5].options[0].label":"Refuse. Minin is not a military commander, and we cannot take the risk.","content.events[5].options[0].result":"Without a surprise attack, the battle might have dragged on, allowing the hetman to bring his supply convoy into the Kremlin.","content.events[5].options[1].label":"Give Minin three companies of noble cavalry","content.events[5].options[1].result":"Pozharsky gave Minin three companies. Their attack near the Crimean Court sent the enemy fleeing. The entire army then charged, and the hetman retreated.","content.encyclopedia[0].title":"Trubetskoy's Cossacks","content.encyclopedia[0].text":"Cossacks were free warriors from the southern frontier who lived along the Don and other rivers. Many had come to Moscow with the First People's Militia and remained there under Prince Trubetskoy's command. They fought bravely but were often poor and unhappy. On August 24, the Cossacks recaptured a small fort near St. Clement's Church.","content.encyclopedia[1].title":"Streltsy","content.encyclopedia[1].text":"Ivan the Terrible established the streltsy army in 1550. Streltsy were full-time soldiers who wore colorful coats and fired guns called pishchals. They used a berdysh, an axe on a long handle, to support their guns while shooting. In 1612, streltsy served both in the militia and among the defenders of cities.","content.encyclopedia[2].title":"Noble Cavalry","content.encyclopedia[2].text":"Noblemen received land grants from the tsar in exchange for serving in the cavalry. On campaigns, they wore chain mail and helmets, fought with sabers, and shot bows. Their cavalry companies were the main strength of Pozharsky's militia. Three companies of noble cavalry joined Minin in the decisive attack on August 24.","content.encyclopedia[3].title":"Archers with Saadaks","content.encyclopedia[3].text":"A saadak was a set consisting of a bow in its case and a quiver of arrows. In the early 17th century, Russian horsemen and foot archers still often used bows. A bow could be fired faster than a pishchal, an early firearm. Archers were effective against lightly armored cavalry.","content.encyclopedia[4].title":"Artillerymen","content.encyclopedia[4].text":"Artillerymen operated cannons and fired iron and stone cannonballs. Russian cannons were cast at the Cannon Yard in Moscow. In fall 1612, the militia placed cannons opposite Kitay-gorod and the Kremlin and bombarded the garrison inside. Cannons had a long range, but reloading took a long time.","content.encyclopedia[5].title":"Mobile Wooden Fortress","content.encyclopedia[5].text":"A gulyay-gorod was a mobile fortress made of thick wooden shields transported on wagons. On the battlefield, the shields could quickly be arranged in a line or circle. Soldiers fired from behind them as if they were walls. The gulyay-gorod became especially famous at the Battle of Molodi in 1572.","content.encyclopedia[6].title":"Paholiks","content.encyclopedia[6].text":"In the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, paholiks were servants and squires of noblemen. They cared for their masters' horses and weapons. In battle, paholiks also fought with sabers and axes. There were many of them in the Moscow garrison.","content.encyclopedia[7].title":"Zholners","content.encyclopedia[7].text":"In the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth, a zholner was a soldier who served for pay. Zholners formed the backbone of the Polish garrison in Moscow. When their wages went unpaid for a long time, they complained and sometimes deserted. In the besieged Kremlin, they suffered terrible hunger.","content.encyclopedia[8].title":"Hungarian Hajduks","content.encyclopedia[8].text":"Hajduks were Hungarian infantry soldiers. They wore short coats and fur hats, fired guns, and fought with sabers. King Stephen Báthory, who came from Transylvania, gladly recruited them into his army. Hetman Chodkiewicz also had this kind of infantry.","content.encyclopedia[9].title":"Zaporozhian Cossacks","content.encyclopedia[9].text":"In Moscow, Zaporozhian and Dnieper Cossacks were called Cherkasy. They lived beyond the rapids of the Dnieper River and often served the Polish king for pay. During the Time of Troubles, Cherkasy units fought in Russian lands. Many Cossacks came to Moscow with Hetman Chodkiewicz, but historians debate their exact number.","content.encyclopedia[10].title":"Pyatigortsy","content.encyclopedia[10].text":"Pyatigortsy were light cavalrymen of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. They wore chain mail and carried spears, sabers, and bows. According to tradition, their name came from the Pyatigorye region of the Caucasus, where the first such horsemen originated. The Pyatigortsy attacked quickly and retreated just as fast.","content.encyclopedia[11].title":"German Mercenaries","content.encyclopedia[11].text":"In Europe at that time, many soldiers fought for money, and mercenary infantry from German lands served different rulers. Musketeers fired heavy muskets, while pikemen wearing helmets and breastplates protected them with long pikes. Mercenaries also served in the armies of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. Foreign soldiers served the Russian tsars as well.","content.encyclopedia[12].title":"Winged Hussars","content.encyclopedia[12].text":"Hussars were the pride of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth's heavy cavalry. They wore armor and charged with long lances. Later, hussars became famous for the feathered wings attached behind their backs. In 1610, Hetman Żółkiewski's hussars defeated a Russian-Swedish army at the Battle of Klushino.","content.encyclopedia[13].title":"Hetman's Supply Convoy","content.encyclopedia[13].text":"The Polish garrison in the Kremlin had been starving for a long time. The hetman brought hundreds of wagons loaded with bread, gunpowder, and other supplies. Getting the convoy into the Kremlin was the main goal of his campaign. The militia prevented him from doing so. Without supplies, the garrison held out only until fall.","content.encyclopedia[14].title":"Rotmistrs","content.encyclopedia[14].text":"A rotmistr commanded a cavalry company, or banner, in the army of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. Usually, he was an experienced nobleman who recruited his own soldiers. The company's best fighters stayed close to their rotmistr. When he fell, the company often retreated.","content.encyclopedia[15].title":"Alexander Gonsevsky","content.encyclopedia[15].text":"Alexander Gonsevsky was a Polish commander whom King Sigismund III placed in charge of the Moscow garrison in 1610. He governed the city on the king's behalf alongside the boyars. During the uprising on March 19, 1611, his soldiers set Moscow on fire. In 1612, Gonsevsky left the city and handed command to others.","content.encyclopedia[16].title":"Nikolai Strus","content.encyclopedia[16].text":"Nikolai Strus was a Polish colonel who commanded the Moscow garrison in 1612. He waited for help from the hetman, but Chodkiewicz could not break through. That fall, terrible hunger spread through the Kremlin. In late October 1612, the garrison surrendered, and Strus was taken prisoner.","content.encyclopedia[17].title":"Hajduk Commander","content.encyclopedia[17].text":"According to some accounts, on the night of August 23, a traitor named Grigory Orlov secretly led a unit of the hetman's infantry into Zamoskvorechye. The unit commander's name is unknown, so the game identifies him by his rank. Such nighttime flanking maneuvers were dangerous because the enemy could attack from behind. But by morning, the militia was ready to fight.","content.encyclopedia[18].title":"Jan Karol Chodkiewicz","content.encyclopedia[18].text":"Jan Karol Chodkiewicz was the Grand Hetman of Lithuania and one of the best commanders of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth. In 1605, he defeated the Swedes at the Battle of Kircholm. In August 1612, he tried to break through to the Kremlin, but the militia drove him back, and he withdrew. Chodkiewicz died in 1621 while defending Khotyn against the Ottomans.","content.encyclopedia[19].title":"Dmitry Pozharsky","content.encyclopedia[19].text":"Prince Dmitry Mikhailovich Pozharsky was a military commander who was badly wounded in Moscow in March 1611. The people of Nizhny Novgorod invited him to lead the Second People's Militia, and he marched with the army toward Moscow. Pozharsky defeated Hetman Chodkiewicz and, together with Trubetskoy, liberated the Kremlin. After the Time of Troubles, he served Tsar Mikhail and received the rank of boyar.","content.encyclopedia[20].title":"Kuzma Minin","content.encyclopedia[20].text":"Kuzma Minin was a townsman and merchant from Nizhny Novgorod whom the citizens elected as their local leader. In fall 1611, he called on the people to raise money for an army and donated much of his own property. Minin managed the militia's treasury and personally led cavalry companies in the decisive attack on August 24. Later, he became a member of the tsar's council and died in 1616.","content.encyclopedia[21].title":"Patriarch Hermogenes","content.encyclopedia[21].text":"Hermogenes had been Patriarch of Moscow since 1606. He refused to tell the militia to disband and sent letters encouraging people to fight. The Poles and boyars imprisoned him in Chudov Monastery. He died there in February 1612, reportedly from starvation.","content.encyclopedia[22].title":"Prokopy Lyapunov","content.encyclopedia[22].text":"Prokopy Lyapunov was a nobleman and military commander from Ryazan. In 1611, he gathered the First People's Militia and approached Moscow. Disagreements broke out between the noblemen and Cossacks in the militia. In summer 1611, the Cossacks killed Lyapunov, and the First People's Militia fell apart.","content.encyclopedia[23].title":"Dmitry Trubetskoy","content.encyclopedia[23].text":"Prince Dmitry Timofeyevich Trubetskoy was one of the leaders of the First People's Militia. After it fell apart, he remained outside Moscow with his Cossacks. At first, he kept his forces separate from Pozharsky's, but in September 1612, they united their armies. Together, they captured Kitay-gorod and accepted the Kremlin garrison's surrender.","content.encyclopedia[24].title":"The Moscow Uprising of 1611","content.encyclopedia[24].text":"On March 19, 1611, the people of Moscow rose against the Polish garrison. To fight back, the soldiers set the city on fire, and much of Moscow burned down. Pozharsky fought near his wooden fortification on Sretenka Street and was badly wounded. Soon afterward, the First People's Militia approached Moscow but failed to capture the Kremlin.","content.encyclopedia[25].title":"Gathering in Nizhny Novgorod","content.encyclopedia[25].text":"In fall 1611, Kuzma Minin called on the people of Nizhny Novgorod to raise money for a new army. The townspeople donated part of their property and invited Prince Pozharsky to command the militia. Soldiers from many towns joined the army. In late winter 1612, the militia marched up the Volga River.","content.encyclopedia[26].title":"The Stay in Yaroslavl","content.encyclopedia[26].text":"From April to July 1612, the militia stayed in Yaroslavl. There, it established the Council of All the Land, which governed on behalf of the entire country. People, money, and weapons arrived from many places. When news came that Chodkiewicz was marching toward Moscow, the militia set out to meet him.","content.encyclopedia[27].title":"The Battle of August 22","content.encyclopedia[27].text":"On August 22, Hetman Chodkiewicz crossed the Moskva River near Novodevichy Convent and attacked the militia near the Chertolye and Arbat Gates. At the same time, the garrison launched a sortie from the Kremlin. Cavalry fought until evening, and reinforcements arrived from the companies stationed with Trubetskoy. The hetman withdrew but did not give up his goal.","content.encyclopedia[28].title":"The Battle in Zamoskvorechye","content.encyclopedia[28].text":"On August 24, the hetman advanced through Zamoskvorechye and captured a small fort near St. Clement's Church. The Cossacks recaptured it but then began leaving the battlefield. Only Avraamy Palitsyn's appeals persuaded them to return. Toward evening, Minin attacked near the Crimean Court with three cavalry companies, sending the enemy fleeing. On August 25, Chodkiewicz left Moscow.","content.encyclopedia[29].title":"The Capture of Kitay-gorod and the Kremlin","content.encyclopedia[29].text":"After the hetman withdrew, the Polish garrison remained in the Kremlin without supplies and suffered from hunger. On October 22, 1612, the militia stormed Kitay-gorod. A few days later, the garrison surrendered, and the boyars came out of the Kremlin, including the young Mikhail Romanov. Moscow was free.","content.encyclopedia[30].title":"The Time of Troubles","content.encyclopedia[30].text":"The Time of Troubles was a difficult period in the early 17th century. After the Rurikid dynasty ended, famine, struggles for the throne, and wars followed. Impostors appeared, and Polish and Swedish armies invaded the country. The Time of Troubles ended after Mikhail Romanov was elected tsar in 1613 and peace agreements were reached with neighboring countries.","content.encyclopedia[31].title":"The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth","content.encyclopedia[31].text":"The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth was a state formed in 1569 by the union of Poland and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania. It was ruled by an elected king, who in 1612 was Sigismund III. The nobility, called the szlachta, held great power. The king wanted either to take the Moscow throne himself or place his son Władysław on it.","content.encyclopedia[32].title":"Pishchals and Muskets","content.encyclopedia[32].text":"In Rus', the word pishchal referred to firearms, including handheld guns and cannons. A handheld pishchal was loaded through the muzzle with gunpowder and a bullet, then fired using a burning match. Each shot took a long time to prepare, so soldiers fired in turns. But a bullet could pierce even plate armor.","content.encyclopedia[33].title":"The Kremlin and Kitay-gorod","content.encyclopedia[33].text":"The Kremlin is a fortress in the heart of Moscow, with red brick walls and towers built under Ivan III. Beside it stood Kitay-gorod, the city's trading district, which had its own walls. In 1612, the Polish garrison occupied both the Kremlin and Kitay-gorod. Dates in the game follow the old calendar, as used in the chronicles.","content.encyclopedia[34].title":"What Happened Next","content.encyclopedia[34].text":"In 1613, the Zemsky Sobor elected Mikhail Fyodorovich Romanov as tsar. Russia signed the Treaty of Stolbovo with Sweden in 1617 and the Truce of Deulino with the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth in 1618. Smolensk remained under Polish control. National Unity Day is celebrated on November 4 to commemorate the liberation of Moscow."}};
